const GUIDE_PAGES = {"nymrissa-wavecaller/heroic":{"boss":"Nymrissa Wavecaller","d":"h","title":"Nymrissa Wavecaller Heroic guide: mechanics, positions and every job · Debrief","desc":"Nymrissa Wavecaller Heroic guide: One loop every 110 seconds, rinse and repeat: kill the murlocs before they reach her bubble, soak the Frost Orbs with…","html":"<h1>Nymrissa Wavecaller Heroic guide</h1><p>One loop every 110 seconds, rinse and repeat: kill the murlocs before they reach her bubble, soak the Frost Orbs with care, get into the whirlpools' gap and land safely after the Pop. Every Abyssal Rain makes her stronger.</p><h3>What wipes us</h3><ul><li>No cooldown on a late Rain: Late in the fight nobody uses a healing cooldown, and the four hits kill the lowest players.</li><li>A pack nobody took: Nobody stops or kills one side pack: it walks into the bubble and becomes Berserkers that hit the whole raid until they die.</li><li>An orb left to shatter: 30 seconds after it dropped it shatters: a big hit and stacking damage over time on the whole raid.</li><li>Caught outside the gap: The whirlpools cross them on the way in and hit for 2.5 million damage.</li></ul><h3>Your job</h3><ul><li>Walk her to the big pack each set, then back to the quarter.</li><li>Iceblade Flurry: a defensive for the last two hits, then swap.</li><li>The off tank takes the orbs nobody else did, all within 30 seconds.</li><li>Abyssal Rain: four raid hits and long damage over time; she gets 5% stronger every cast.</li><li>The Pop: a raid cooldown.</li><li>Chilling Frost: keep the chilled players up while they soak their orbs.</li><li>Murlocs first, then the boss.</li><li>Squads: your side pack, then the one at the back.</li><li>Chilled: drop your three orbs at the edge, close together, and take two of them.</li></ul><h2>The loop: every 110 seconds</h2><p>Abyssal Rain, then her bubble and a murloc set, Chilling Frost and its orbs, a second murloc set and orbs, then the whirlpools and the Pop. Rinse and repeat.</p><h3>Abyssal Rain (Healing cooldowns, it ramps)</h3><p>She casts it 8 seconds in and then twice a loop: four raid hits, one a second, then damage over time for 24 seconds.</p><p>She casts it 8 seconds in and then twice a loop: four raid hits, one a second, then damage over time for 24 seconds. She gets 5% stronger with every cast, so later ones need bigger cooldowns.</p><ul><li>She casts Abyssal Rain: 2 seconds.</li><li>Four raid hits, one a second: a raid cooldown, personals if you're low.</li><li>Then damage over time on the raid for 24 seconds.</li><li>She is 5% stronger after every cast.</li></ul><h3>Alluring Bubble (Kill the murlocs before they reach it)</h3><p>She forms a giant bubble in the middle of the island; pools in the water round the edge show how many murlocs come from each (1 to 5).</p><p>She forms a giant bubble in the middle of the island; pools in the water round the edge show how many murlocs come from each (1 to 5). Any that reach the bubble become Berserkers that hit the raid until they die.</p><ul><li>The pools show where the murlocs come out and how many.</li><li>Tanks walk her to the biggest pack; the melee stun it and cleave it on her.</li><li>Crowd control (stuns, roots, slows) stops the side packs where they stand.</li><li>Two ranged squads then kill the side packs one by one, the biggest first.</li><li>A slow (a Death Knight, or any group slow) holds the far pack until a squad gets there.</li><li>Everyone brings some group crowd control: stuns, roots, Mass Entanglement, Frost Nova.</li><li>A grip or a knockback can drag a small pack into the big one you are killing.</li><li>One or two Berserkers is fine: cleave them on the boss, but kill them before the next Abyssal Rain or Pop.</li></ul><h3>Chilling Frost (Drop them at the edge, soak them all)</h3><p>A few players are chilled: an orb drops under them every 1.5 seconds, three each.</p><p>A few players are chilled: an orb drops under them every 1.5 seconds, three each. Touching one soaks it (stacking damage over time). An orb left 30 seconds shatters on the whole raid. Each soaked orb leaves ice that hurts and makes you slide.</p><ul><li>Chilled: go to the edge behind her.</li><li>Drop your three orbs close together.</li><li>Pop a defensive and take two of your own.</li><li>The off tank takes the rest, all within 30 seconds.</li><li>The ice stays until the Pop washes it away, so keep it in one corner.</li><li>A Chilling Frost lands just before the whirlpools: immunity players clean up any orbs left then.</li></ul><h3>Lingering Frost (Stay off the ice)</h3><p>Each soaked orb leaves ice that hurts every second and makes you slide.</p><p>Each soaked orb leaves ice that hurts every second and makes you slide. It stays until the Pop washes it away.</p><ul><li>The orbs are soaked: each one leaves ice.</li><li>Keep it all in one corner, at the edge behind her.</li><li>Stay off it: it hurts every second and makes you slide.</li><li>The Pop washes the ice away.</li></ul><h3>Swirling Whirlpools (Everyone in the gap)</h3><p>Whirlpools rise all round the edge of the island, leaving one gap, and close in on the bubble.</p><p>Whirlpools rise all round the edge of the island, leaving one gap, and close in on the bubble. They nearly one-shot anyone they cross. When they reach the bubble it pops.</p><ul><li>Find the gap as they rise: raid speed boosts help.</li><li>Everyone into it, the boss too, close to the bubble.</li><li>They reach the bubble: it pops.</li><li>Back to your spots.</li><li>Stand within about 30 yards of the bubble: the Pop throws you about 20 yards.</li><li>Further back than that? Cancel the throw: a charge, a glide, a teleport, Shimmer or Death's Advance right as it pops.</li><li>The gap can be anywhere: the raid lead calls it.</li></ul><h3>Pop! (Land on the island, not in the water)</h3><p>When the whirlpools reach the bubble it bursts: a raid hit, and everyone is knocked about 20 yards away from it.</p><p>When the whirlpools reach the bubble it bursts: a raid hit, and everyone is knocked about 20 yards away from it. Land in the water and the sharks bite: a 20-second bleed.</p><ul><li>Stand close to the bubble before it pops.</li><li>Pop: a raid cooldown for the hit, and the ice is washed away.</li><li>Walk back to your spots.</li></ul><p>Five ramping hits on her tank. A defensive for the last two, then the other tank taunts.</p><p>Murlocs before the boss, every set. Cleave the big pack on her so no damage is lost.</p><h2>Putting it all together</h2><p>The whole fight in the order of a real kill. Quiet stretches are cut, and a loop that repeats plays once; the time shown is the real fight clock. Pick a moment to jump there.</p><p>The whole fight in order, timed by a real kill (2:35). Coloured lines are moments when two things happen at once.</p><ul><li>The loop is 110 seconds: two murloc sets, Chilling Frost orbs and Iceblade Flurry on her tank, then the whirlpools and the Pop.</li><li>She gets 5% stronger every Abyssal Rain: the fight ramps up.</li></ul><h3>0:00–2:35</h3><p>Tanks take her to the south-west quarter of the island: she stays on the outside, the raid on the inside.</p><p>She casts Abyssal Rain: 2 seconds. Four raid hits, one a second: a raid cooldown, personals if you're low.</p><p>Nymrissa dies.</p><h2>Your job, by role</h2><p>Every line for each role. The Your job box at the top shows the first three.</p>"},"nymrissa-wavecaller/mythic":{"boss":"Nymrissa Wavecaller","d":"m","title":"Nymrissa Wavecaller Mythic guide: mechanics, positions and every job · Debrief","desc":"Nymrissa Wavecaller Mythic guide: Mythic adds three things to the loop: a Frostscale in every big pack that shields the murlocs near it, a raid hit on…","html":"<h1>Nymrissa Wavecaller Mythic guide</h1><p>Mythic adds three things to the loop: a Frostscale in every big pack that shields the murlocs near it, a raid hit on every orb soak, and Water Jet, a tank line that is the only way to clear the ice.</p><h3>What wipes us</h3><ul><li>No cooldown on a late Rain: Late in the fight nobody uses a healing cooldown, and the four hits kill the lowest players.</li><li>The Frostscale left in the pack: Nobody grips the Frostscale out: its shield gives the whole pack 99% less damage, nothing dies, and they walk on into the bubble.</li><li>The orbs in one pile: One step touches four at once, and four raid hits land together.</li><li>Caught outside the gap: The whirlpools cross them on the way in and hit for 2.5 million damage.</li></ul><h3>Your job</h3><ul><li>Walk her to the big pack each set, then back to the quarter.</li><li>Death Knight tank: grip the Frostscale out of the pack.</li><li>Swap on every Water Jet; sweep it across the ice.</li><li>Abyssal Rain: four raid hits and long damage over time; she gets 5% stronger every cast.</li><li>Every orb soak hits the raid: a cooldown on every set.</li><li>The Pop: a raid cooldown.</li><li>The Frostscale first, then the big pack, then the boss.</li><li>Squads: your side pack, then the one at the back.</li><li>Chilled: big steps in the lane, your orbs well apart.</li></ul><h2>The loop: every 110 seconds</h2><p>Abyssal Rain, then her bubble and a murloc set, Chilling Frost and its orbs, a second murloc set and orbs, then the whirlpools and the Pop. A Water Jet comes about every 22 seconds all the way through. Rinse and repeat.</p><h3>Abyssal Rain (Healing cooldowns, it ramps)</h3><p>She casts it 8 seconds in and then twice a loop: four raid hits, one a second, then damage over time for 24 seconds.</p><p>She casts it 8 seconds in and then twice a loop: four raid hits, one a second, then damage over time for 24 seconds. She gets 5% stronger with every cast, so later ones need bigger cooldowns.</p><ul><li>She casts Abyssal Rain: 2 seconds.</li><li>Four raid hits, one a second: a raid cooldown, personals if you're low.</li><li>Then damage over time on the raid for 24 seconds.</li><li>She is 5% stronger after every cast.</li></ul><h3>Alluring Bubble (Frostscale out, squads on the side packs)</h3><p>Pools round the edge show how many murlocs come from each side.</p><p>Pools round the edge show how many murlocs come from each side. The pack of five has a Bubblefin Frostscale: murlocs within 15 yards of it take 99% less damage. Any that reach her bubble become Berserkers, and the next Pop wipes the raid.</p><ul><li>The pools show where the murlocs come out: walk her to the big pack.</li><li>The melee stun the big pack and cleave it on her.</li><li>The Death Knight tank grips the Frostscale out; kill it first.</li><li>Two ranged squads (3 or 4 each, with crowd control) take the side packs, then the one at the back.</li><li>A third tank, a Blood Death Knight, only slows the far pack: Death and Decay, Chains of Ice.</li><li>Play in one quarter of the island and never drag her across it: the squads go to the murlocs instead.</li><li>Druids, Evokers and Hunters bring the best crowd control for the squads.</li><li>The Blood Death Knight needs no gear and does no damage: their whole job is slowing murlocs.</li><li>A Berserker that gets through: kill it before the next Abyssal Rain or Pop.</li><li>After the third Pop, ignore them: see The burn.</li></ul><h3>Chilling Frost (Big steps, soak one at a time)</h3><p>Three players are chilled and drop three orbs each.</p><p>Three players are chilled and drop three orbs each. Every soak also hits the whole raid (Frost Burst), and the ice an orb leaves stays until a Water Jet washes it away.</p><ul><li>Chilled: walk into the lane between her and the bubble.</li><li>Take big steps: your three orbs well apart.</li><li>The immunity player soaks them one at a time, a second or more apart.</li><li>Immunity over: stop, even with orbs left.</li><li>The tanks take what's left, two each at most.</li><li>Four immunity players take turns, one a set: Hunter Turtle, Paladin Bubble, Rogue Cloak. Three is enough: the tanks cover the middle set.</li><li>Two or three orbs left is fine: three tanks can take six between them.</li><li>Healers: a raid cooldown on every set.</li><li>The orbs' damage over time is shorter now (12 seconds) and Frost Burst hits softer than when the first guides were made: still stagger the soaks.</li></ul><h3>Lingering Frost (Stay off the ice)</h3><p>Each soaked orb leaves ice that hurts every second and makes you slide.</p><p>Each soaked orb leaves ice that hurts every second and makes you slide. It stays until a Water Jet washes it away: the Pop does not.</p><ul><li>Each soaked orb leaves ice in the lane.</li><li>Keep it in one place and stay off it: it hurts every second and makes you slide.</li><li>Water Jet: her tank sweeps the line across the lane.</li><li>The ice is washed away. Nothing else clears it, not even the Pop.</li></ul><h3>Water Jet (Sweep the ice, swap every cast)</h3><p>A tank line: after a 3-second cast she blasts her tank for 6 seconds, pushing them back, and the water runs right across the island.</p><p>A tank line: after a 3-second cast she blasts her tank for 6 seconds, pushing them back, and the water runs right across the island. It's the only thing that clears the ice, and each hit adds 20% to the next.</p><ul><li>Her tank steps to the edge of the lane.</li><li>Walk across it slowly: the line sweeps the ice away.</li><li>Nobody else in the line: it goes right across the island.</li><li>The other tank taunts after every cast.</li><li>The first one each loop hits air: no ice yet.</li><li>There is one more jet than there are sets of ice, so you can catch up if you fall behind.</li><li>The raid lead calls &quot;Jet&quot; when the line will pass through the bubble toward people on the far side.</li></ul><h3>Swirling Whirlpools (Everyone in the gap)</h3><p>Whirlpools rise all round the edge of the island, leaving one gap, and close in on the bubble.</p><p>Whirlpools rise all round the edge of the island, leaving one gap, and close in on the bubble. They nearly one-shot anyone they cross. When they reach the bubble it pops.</p><ul><li>Find the gap as they rise: raid speed boosts help.</li><li>Everyone into it, the boss too, close to the bubble.</li><li>They reach the bubble: it pops.</li><li>Back to your spots.</li><li>Stand within about 30 yards of the bubble: the Pop throws you about 20 yards.</li><li>Further back than that? Cancel the throw: a charge, a glide, a teleport, Shimmer or Death's Advance right as it pops.</li><li>The gap can be anywhere: the raid lead calls it.</li></ul><h3>Pop! (Land on the island, not in the water)</h3><p>When the whirlpools reach the bubble it bursts: a raid hit, and everyone is knocked about 20 yards away from it.</p><p>When the whirlpools reach the bubble it bursts: a raid hit, and everyone is knocked about 20 yards away from it. On Mythic the ice stays where it is. Land in the water and the sharks bite: a 20-second bleed.</p><ul><li>Stand close to the bubble before it pops.</li><li>Pop: a raid cooldown. Ice behind you? Line up with a Warlock gateway (their portal) or use any other way to cancel the knockback.</li><li>Walk back to your spots.</li></ul><p>The Frostscale first, then the big pack on her, then the boss. Cleave the pack on her so no boss damage is lost.</p><h2>The burn: after the third Pop</h2><p>About 5:40 in, after the third Pop. She enrages after the next whirlpool (about 7:15), so this is the last stretch.</p><p>Everyone on the boss. Murlocs can reach the bubble now: their big hit only comes with the next Pop, and the enrage comes with that Pop.</p><p>Keep soaking the orbs: one left alone shatters within 30 seconds, long before the enrage.</p><h2>Putting it all together</h2><p>The whole fight in the order of a real kill. Quiet stretches are cut, and a loop that repeats plays once; the time shown is the real fight clock. Pick a moment to jump there.</p><p>The whole fight in order, timed by a real kill (6:23). Coloured lines are moments when two things happen at once.</p><ul><li>The loop is 110 seconds: two murloc sets, two orb sets and four Water Jets, then the whirlpools and the Pop.</li><li>Every orb soak hits the whole raid: one at a time.</li><li>She gets 5% stronger every Abyssal Rain: the fight ramps up.</li></ul><h3>0:00–5:42</h3><p>Tanks take her to the south-west quarter of the island: she stays on the outside, the raid on the inside.</p><p>She casts Abyssal Rain: 2 seconds. Four raid hits, one a second: a raid cooldown, personals if you're low.</p><h3>The burn: after the third Pop5:42–6:23</h3><p>Nymrissa dies.</p><h2>Your job, by role</h2><p>Every line for each role. The Your job box at the top shows the first three.</p><ul><li>Leftover orbs: two each at most.</li><li>After the third Pop ignore the murlocs and burn her.</li></ul>"},"nekzali-the-soulcoiler/heroic":{"boss":"Nek'zali the Soulcoiler","d":"h","title":"Nek'zali the Soulcoiler Heroic guide: mechanics, positions and every job · Debrief","desc":"Nek'zali the Soulcoiler Heroic guide: Everything turns around the well: keep every add out of it, take Essence Rend to the edge, and let a tank run…","html":"<h1>Nek'zali the Soulcoiler Heroic guide</h1><p>Everything turns around the well: keep every add out of it, take Essence Rend to the edge, and let a tank run Possession Barrage away. At 50% kill her two Echoes and burn every body pile, then Bloodlust and race to the end.</p><h3>What wipes us</h3><ul><li>Standing where a spirit lands: A player stays where a spirit is landing: a big hit and a knockback, in the middle of five raid hits.</li><li>An add reaches the well: Nobody breaks one add's shield, so it walks into the well: a raid hit and 5 energy for her.</li><li>A body left to come back: A body at the edge of the pile is missed and nobody burns it: it comes back pulsing heavy damage.</li><li>In a cultist's path: A player stands in the path of a cultist circle when Invoke moves it: it dashes straight through them.</li></ul><h3>Your job</h3><ul><li>Drag the boss to each set of adds so melee cleave both.</li><li>Possession Barrage: if you hold her, run straight away from the raid, 30-40 yards. The other tank taunts as the echoes land.</li><li>Swap around 7-9 stacks of Hollowing Strikes.</li><li>Essence Rend: dispel only at the edge.</li><li>Ignition: five raid hits, a cooldown each time. Go in with no Ritual Burn stacks.</li><li>Intermission: Residual Toll drains a few players every second, then leaves them taking less healing: heal them first.</li><li>Casters break the add shields with magic; everyone cleaves them.</li><li>Never let an add reach the well.</li><li>Ranged: walk your fire circle over the bodies the pyre misses.</li></ul><h2>Phase 1: the well</h2><p>Her Ignitions fill her energy a quarter at a time; adds that reach the well fill it faster. Push her to 50% before the fourth Ignition.</p><h3>Soulcoil Ignition (Heal through it, dodge the spirits)</h3><p>About every 73 seconds she stirs the well: five raid hits with stacking damage over time, and 25 energy for her.</p><p>About every 73 seconds she stirs the well: five raid hits with stacking damage over time, and 25 energy for her. Each hit adds Ritual Burn (+15% Rite damage for a minute). Spirits land on players: a big hit and knockback within 5 yards.</p><ul><li>She floats over the well and stirs it. Stay out of the well.</li><li>Five raid hits: healers, a cooldown for each Ignition. Go in with no Ritual Burn stacks.</li><li>Spirits come out of the well: step out of the circle where one lands.</li></ul><h3>Restless Amani (Break the shields, cleave them)</h3><p>Coffins light up on one side and adds walk out for the well behind a shield only magic breaks.</p><p>Coffins light up on one side and adds walk out for the well behind a shield only magic breaks. One that reaches the well hits the raid and gives her 5 energy. A dead add leaves a body and damage over time within 15 yards.</p><ul><li>Coffins light up: adds are coming. The tank drags the boss toward them, and everyone follows.</li><li>They walk for the well behind a shield only magic breaks: casters, on the shields.</li><li>Shields down: they're normal adds now. Cleave them down with the boss.</li><li>Their bodies stay: kill the adds together, in one pile.</li><li>Death Grip, slows, knockbacks and a Druid's Ursol's Vortex all work on them, shield or not.</li><li>Hunters do physical damage: they can't break shields. Warlocks, Shadow Priests and Boomkins can.</li><li>The tank who isn't on her picks up adds as their shields break, so none wander through the middle.</li><li>As long as no add reaches the well, boss damage hardly matters in phase one.</li><li>Remember where each pile is: the intermission's pyres go on them.</li></ul><h3>Essence Rend (To the edge, then dispel)</h3><p>A few players are pulled toward the middle, then thrown out with a lingering hit you can dispel.</p><p>A few players are pulled toward the middle, then thrown out with a lingering hit you can dispel. Where the dispel lands, a cultist circle appears and stays all fight, slowly circling the room.</p><ul><li>Marked? You're pulled in, then thrown out.</li><li>Keep going to the edge of the room, behind the raid.</li><li>At the edge, healers dispel. A cultist circle appears there.</li><li>It slowly circles the room: keep away from it.</li></ul><h3>Possession Barrage (Tank runs, nobody in between)</h3><p>Echoes fly from her toward a tank and burst on arrival for raid damage: the farther they fly, the less it hurts.</p><ul><li>The tank holding her runs straight away from the raid, 30 to 40 yards.</li><li>Everyone else stays behind her. The other tank taunts as the echoes land.</li><li>Nobody steps into the line between her and the runner.</li></ul><p>Every hit of hers makes the tank take less healing. Swap around 7 to 9 stacks, right after a Barrage if you can. One tank can take every Possession Barrage.</p><p>Each Ignition gives her 25 energy and each add in the well 5; a player who dies in the well feeds her too. At 100 she enrages. Push her to 50% before the fourth Ignition.</p><h2>Intermission: the Echoes</h2><p>At 50% she floats over the well and can't be hurt while her two Echoes live. Kill them one at a time, and burn every body pile with their pyres.</p><h3>Echoes of Jawae (Tank it in the doorway)</h3><p>Two Echoes protect her, one at a time, from doors across the room from each other (which door goes first changes from pull to pull).</p><p>Two Echoes protect her, one at a time, from doors across the room from each other (which door goes first changes from pull to pull). Each has a big frontal at the end of a 15-second cast. Adds keep coming.</p><ul><li>She floats over the well: free damage on the way.</li><li>The Echo comes in by the door. Its tank stands in the doorway, so the frontal points into the door.</li><li>Everyone else stays behind the Echo, on the room side.</li><li>Then its tank takes it from body pile to body pile for the pyres.</li></ul><h3>Hungering Pyre (Dead centre on a body pile)</h3><p>A fire soak on the Echo's tank, split between everyone within 10 yards; it burns every body it hits.</p><p>A fire soak on the Echo's tank, split between everyone within 10 yards; it burns every body it hits. Everyone it misses gets a burn, which becomes a small fire circle round them for about 20 seconds that burns any body it touches.</p><ul><li>The Echo's tank stands it on a body pile.</li><li>Tanks and melee stack in the dead centre, on that tank. Everyone else stays out.</li><li>The pyre burns the bodies it hits.</li><li>Ranged: walk your fire circle over any body left, here or in another pile.</li></ul><h2>Phase 2: Uncoiling</h2><p>She comes back with her energy at zero. The raid takes damage all the time now: Bloodlust, cooldowns, and kill her before she fills again.</p><h3>Invoke (The circles move)</h3><p>She calls the Rite again (a raid hit) and the cultist circles dash to new spots round the edge.</p><ul><li>Phase two: Bloodlust and cooldowns. The raid takes damage all the time now.</li><li>Invoke: the circles will move. Watch the arrows.</li><li>Step out of the circles' new paths.</li></ul><p>Use Bloodlust as phase two starts, with your second round of cooldowns. Holding it 30 to 50 seconds to line up is fine.</p><h2>Putting it all together</h2><p>The whole fight in the order of a real kill. Quiet stretches are cut, and a loop that repeats plays once; the time shown is the real fight clock. Pick a moment to jump there.</p><p>The whole fight in order, timed by a real kill (3:50). Coloured lines are moments when two things happen at once.</p><ul><li>No add may reach the well: each one hits the raid and gives her 5 energy.</li><li>Essence Rend circles stay all fight: drop them at the edge, behind the raid.</li><li>Every body pile you make in phase one gets burned in the intermission.</li></ul><h3>0:00–1:12</h3><p>The boss tank takes her at the south edge of the well, standing between her and the well.</p><p>Marked? You're pulled in, then thrown out. Keep going to the edge of the room, behind the raid.</p><h3>Intermission: the Echoes1:12–2:30</h3><h3>Phase 2: Uncoiling2:30–3:50</h3><p>The second Echo is dead: she comes back. Bloodlust and every cooldown now.</p><p>A race to the end: keep every add out of the well and burn her before 100 energy.</p><h2>Your job, by role</h2><p>Every line for each role. The Your job box at the top shows the first three.</p><ul><li>Intermission: tank the Echo in its doorway, then take it from body pile to body pile for each pyre.</li><li>Phase two: the damage never stops; plan cooldowns for each Invoke.</li></ul>"},"nekzali-the-soulcoiler/mythic":{"boss":"Nek'zali the Soulcoiler","d":"m","title":"Nek'zali the Soulcoiler Mythic guide: mechanics, positions and every job · Debrief","desc":"Nek'zali the Soulcoiler Mythic guide: Hit squads: twice in every part of the fight the well pulls the raid in until a squad of 4 DPS and a healer goes…","html":"<h1>Nek'zali the Soulcoiler Mythic guide</h1><p>Hit squads: twice in every part of the fight the well pulls the raid in until a squad of 4 DPS and a healer goes down, kicks the Drowned Echo's curse and kills it. Invoke silences anyone casting.</p><h3>What wipes us</h3><ul><li>An add reaches the well: Nobody breaks one add's shield, so it walks into the well: a raid hit and 5 energy for her.</li><li>The curse not kicked: Nobody kicks the Drowned Echo's curse: the whole squad is thrown out of the well, Soulcoiled.</li><li>A body left to come back: A body at the edge of the pile is missed and nobody burns it: it comes back pulsing heavy damage.</li><li>In a cultist's path: A player stands in the path of a cultist circle when Invoke moves it: it dashes straight through them.</li></ul><h3>Your job</h3><ul><li>Drag the boss to each set of adds so melee cleave both.</li><li>Possession Barrage: if you hold her, run straight away from the raid, 30-40 yards. The other tank taunts as the echoes land.</li><li>Swap around 7-9 stacks of Hollowing Strikes.</li><li>Grasping Depths: one healer goes down with each hit squad and keeps it alive; the rest heal the raid through the pull.</li><li>Essence Rend: dispel only at the edge.</li><li>Ignition: five raid hits, a cooldown each time. Go in with no Ritual Burn stacks.</li><li>Casters break the add shields with magic; everyone cleaves them.</li><li>Never let an add reach the well.</li><li>Hit squads take turns in the well; kick the curse late. Skip a trip at the very start of a phase change or with her under 10%.</li></ul><h2>Phase 1: the well</h2><p>Grasping Depths: twice in this phase the well pulls the raid in until a hit squad kills its Echo.</p><h3>Soulcoil Ignition (Heal through it, dodge the spirits)</h3><p>About every 73 seconds she stirs the well: five raid hits with stacking damage over time, and 25 energy for her.</p><p>About every 73 seconds she stirs the well: five raid hits with stacking damage over time, and 25 energy for her. Each hit adds Ritual Burn (+15% Rite damage for a minute). Spirits land on players: a big hit and knockback within 5 yards.</p><ul><li>She floats over the well and stirs it. Stay out of the well.</li><li>Five raid hits: healers, a cooldown for each Ignition. Go in with no Ritual Burn stacks.</li><li>Spirits come out of the well: step out of the circle where one lands.</li></ul><h3>Restless Amani (Break the shields, cleave them)</h3><p>Coffins light up on one side and adds walk out for the well behind a shield only magic breaks.</p><p>Coffins light up on one side and adds walk out for the well behind a shield only magic breaks. One that reaches the well hits the raid and gives her 5 energy. A dead add leaves a body and damage over time within 15 yards.</p><ul><li>Coffins light up: adds are coming. The tank drags the boss toward them, and everyone follows.</li><li>They walk for the well behind a shield only magic breaks: casters, on the shields.</li><li>Shields down: they're normal adds now. Cleave them down with the boss.</li><li>Their bodies stay: kill the adds together, in one pile.</li><li>Death Grip, slows, knockbacks and a Druid's Ursol's Vortex all work on them, shield or not.</li><li>Hunters do physical damage: they can't break shields. Warlocks, Shadow Priests and Boomkins can.</li><li>The tank who isn't on her picks up adds as their shields break, so none wander through the middle.</li><li>As long as no add reaches the well, boss damage hardly matters in phase one.</li><li>Remember where each pile is: the intermission's pyres go on them.</li></ul><h3>Essence Rend (To the edge, then dispel)</h3><p>A few players are pulled toward the middle, then thrown out with a lingering hit you can dispel.</p><p>A few players are pulled toward the middle, then thrown out with a lingering hit you can dispel. Where the dispel lands, a cultist circle appears and stays all fight, slowly circling the room.</p><ul><li>Marked? You're pulled in, then thrown out.</li><li>Keep going to the edge of the room, behind the raid.</li><li>At the edge, healers dispel. A cultist circle appears there.</li><li>It slowly circles the room: keep away from it.</li><li>Healers: don't dispel early. It also soaks up healing, so don't race it.</li><li>Pick a side of the room for the circles and keep them there.</li></ul><h3>Possession Barrage (Tank runs, nobody in between)</h3><p>Echoes fly from her toward a tank and burst on arrival for raid damage: the farther they fly, the less it hurts.</p><ul><li>The tank holding her runs straight away from the raid, 30 to 40 yards.</li><li>Everyone else stays behind her. The other tank taunts as the echoes land.</li><li>Nobody steps into the line between her and the runner.</li></ul><h3>Grasping Depths (Hit squads take turns)</h3><p>Twice each phase (and twice in the intermission) a Drowned Echo wakes in the well, pulling the raid in and hurting everyone until it dies.</p><p>Twice each phase (and twice in the intermission) a Drowned Echo wakes in the well, pulling the raid in and hurting everyone until it dies. Touch the well to go down and fight it. Soul Exhaustion means nobody goes twice in a row.</p><ul><li>The well pulls and hurts everyone until its Echo dies.</li><li>This round's squad touches the well and goes down: 4 DPS and a healer.</li><li>Kick its curse late in the cast: first kicker, then second.</li><li>Echo dead: climb out. The other squad takes the next one.</li><li>Two squads of 4 DPS and a healer, alternating. Send physical DPS (Warriors, Hunters, Monks, Rogues, Death Knights).</li><li>Keep Warlocks, Shadow Priests and Boomkins upstairs: they break the add shields.</li><li>Two melee kicks or three ranged kicks cover each curse. Kick late in the cast.</li><li>Down there, dodge the circles on the floor and the swirling spirits.</li><li>Skip a trip that starts just as she goes to 50%: the phase change clears it. In phase two, with her under 10%, skip it too and just kill her.</li></ul><p>Every hit of hers makes the tank take less healing. Swap around 7 to 9 stacks, right after a Barrage if you can. One tank can take every Possession Barrage.</p><p>Each Ignition gives her 25 energy and each add in the well 5; a player who dies in the well feeds her too. At 100 she enrages. Push her to 50% before the fourth Ignition.</p><h2>Intermission: the Echoes</h2><p>At 50% she floats over the well and can't be hurt while her two Echoes live. Kill them one at a time, burn every body pile with their pyres, and send the hit squads down twice more.</p><h3>Echoes of Jawae (Tank it in the doorway)</h3><p>Two Echoes protect her, one at a time, from doors across the room from each other (which door goes first changes from pull to pull).</p><p>Two Echoes protect her, one at a time, from doors across the room from each other (which door goes first changes from pull to pull). Each has a big frontal at the end of a 15-second cast. Adds keep coming.</p><ul><li>She floats over the well: free damage on the way.</li><li>The Echo comes in by the door. Its tank stands in the doorway, so the frontal points into the door.</li><li>Everyone else stays behind the Echo, on the room side.</li><li>Then its tank takes it from body pile to body pile for the pyres.</li><li>Adds keep coming in the intermission: pile them where the Echo's next pyre will go (a Druid's Ursol's Vortex, grips, knockbacks).</li><li>Our kill's piles, in order: Echo one, northwest then southwest; Echo two, east then northwest. Yours depend on where your adds die: put each pyre on the biggest pile.</li><li>One 2-minute cooldown here; save the second and Bloodlust for phase two.</li></ul><h3>Hungering Pyre (Dead centre on a body pile)</h3><p>A fire soak on the Echo's tank, split between everyone within 10 yards; it burns every body it hits.</p><p>A fire soak on the Echo's tank, split between everyone within 10 yards; it burns every body it hits. Everyone it misses gets a burn, which becomes a small fire circle round them for about 20 seconds that burns any body it touches.</p><ul><li>The Echo's tank stands it on a body pile.</li><li>Tanks and melee stack in the dead centre, on that tank. Everyone else stays out.</li><li>The pyre burns the bodies it hits.</li><li>Ranged: walk your fire circle over any body left, here or in another pile.</li><li>The real hit is smaller than its circle: dead centre, or bodies at the edge survive.</li><li>Ranged can stand on loose bodies before the pyre lands.</li><li>A body that comes back pulses heavy damage: swap to it at once. One or two you can survive; three or more wipe.</li></ul><h2>Phase 2: Uncoiling</h2><p>Grasping Depths twice more, and Invoke silences anyone casting when it lands.</p><h3>Invoke (The circles move)</h3><p>She calls the Rite again (a raid hit) and the cultist circles dash to new spots round the edge.</p><p>She calls the Rite again (a raid hit) and the cultist circles dash to new spots round the edge. It also silences anyone casting when it lands.</p><ul><li>Phase two: Bloodlust and cooldowns. The raid takes damage all the time now.</li><li>Invoke: the circles will move. Watch the arrows.</li><li>Step out of the circles' new paths.</li><li>Stop casting before it lands.</li></ul><p>Use Bloodlust as phase two starts, with your second round of cooldowns. Holding it 30 to 50 seconds to line up is fine.</p><h2>Putting it all together</h2><p>The whole fight in the order of a real kill. Quiet stretches are cut, and a loop that repeats plays once; the time shown is the real fight clock. Pick a moment to jump there.</p><p>The whole fight in order, timed by a real kill (6:59). Coloured lines are moments when two things happen at once.</p><ul><li>No add may reach the well: each one hits the raid and gives her 5 energy.</li><li>Essence Rend circles stay all fight: drop them at the edge, behind the raid.</li><li>Grasping Depths comes twice in each part of the fight (phase one, the intermission, phase two): the two hit squads take turns.</li><li>Every body pile you make in phase one gets burned in the intermission.</li></ul><h3>0:00–2:30</h3><p>The boss tank takes her at the south edge of the well, standing between her and the well.</p><p>Marked? You're pulled in, then thrown out. Keep going to the edge of the room, behind the raid.</p><h3>Intermission: the Echoes2:30–4:55</h3><p>She floats over the well: free damage on the way. The Echo comes in by the door.</p><p>The Echo's tank stands it on a body pile. Tanks and melee stack in the dead centre, on that tank.</p><h3>Phase 2: Uncoiling4:55–6:59</h3><p>A race to the end: keep every add out of the well and burn her before 100 energy.</p><h2>Your job, by role</h2><p>Every line for each role. The Your job box at the top shows the first three.</p><ul><li>Intermission: tank the Echo in its doorway, then take it from body pile to body pile for each pyre.</li><li>Intermission: Residual Toll drains a few players every second, then leaves them taking less healing: heal them first.</li><li>Phase two: the damage never stops; plan cooldowns for each Invoke, and save some for the end.</li><li>Ranged: walk your fire circle over the bodies the pyre misses.</li><li>Stop casting before Invoke lands.</li></ul>"},"entombed-sentinels/heroic":{"boss":"Entombed Sentinels","d":"h","title":"Entombed Sentinels Heroic guide: mechanics, positions and every job · Debrief","desc":"Entombed Sentinels Heroic guide: Two bosses, two halves: Breath in the corner by the green gem, Blood by the red gem, 40+ yards apart. Stay with yours,…","html":"<h1>Entombed Sentinels Heroic guide</h1><p>Two bosses, two halves: Breath in the corner by the green gem, Blood by the red gem, 40+ yards apart. Stay with yours, keep the floor clean of red puddles, and make 4 at every intermission. Then the tanks swap and the round repeats.</p><h3>What wipes us</h3><ul><li>The add left alive: The add that spawns right before the intermission isn't killed, and its pulses hit the raid through the whole intermission.</li><li>Standing on a venom line: A player stays on the line between a soaked droplet and Breath: 4 seconds later its venom flies back along that line and hits them.</li><li>Puddles in the middle: The soakers drop their puddles in the middle of the room: that floor is gone for the rest of the fight.</li><li>A wrong pair: A 2 bumps into a pair that was already made: the wrong total sets off Cultivated Burst and a huge puddle.</li></ul><h3>Your job</h3><ul><li>Pull Breath into the corner by the green gem and Blood into the corner by the red gem, 40+ yards apart.</li><li>Both tanks: take the droplets on your side, always with a defensive, and grab the ones that land in red puddles.</li><li>Intermission: Taunt-swap as every intermission ends. Leaving Blood, drop your big puddle with its middle in old blood.</li><li>Dispel Blighted Blood fast.</li><li>Red soaks and droplets hurt the raid: be ready right after them.</li><li>Intermission: the bosses heal, the raid doesn't take much; make 4 like everyone else.</li><li>Stay with your boss: never in range of both.</li><li>Intermission: Every add dies fast: ranged on both halves hit it from max range. The second one dies before the intermission.</li><li>Red half: soak together, then puddles along the wall.</li></ul><h2>The fight: two Sentinels</h2><p>Breath of Ula'tek (green) in the corner by the green gem, Blood of Ula'tek (red) in the corner by the red gem, at the same end of the room. One half on each, then the intermission at full energy.</p><h3>Two bosses (Stay 40 yards apart)</h3><p>Within 40 yards of each other, both bosses take 99% less damage.</p><p>Within 40 yards of each other, both bosses take 99% less damage. Each boss also stacks its Mark on everyone within 40 yards, so stay with your own.</p><ul><li>One half on each boss. Pull Breath (green) into the corner by the green gem and Blood (red) into the corner by the red gem, at the same end of the room.</li><li>Keep the bosses 40+ yards apart. At those spots they're about 60 apart.</li><li>Stay inside your own boss's 40 yards and out of the other one's.</li></ul><h3>Venom Coagulation (Stack far, everyone burns it)</h3><p>The green boss drops a big slime on a ranged player.</p><p>The green boss drops a big slime on a ranged player. It pulses raid damage while it lives, so everyone who can hits it.</p><ul><li>The add is coming. Green ranged and healers: stack far, together, away from the tank and any big puddle.</li><li>It lands in your stack. Step off it.</li><li>The tank keeps Breath on the far side of the add. Melee cleave both; every ranged on both halves hits it from max range.</li><li>Dead: back to your spots.</li></ul><h3>Toxic Droplets (Tanks and the venom lines)</h3><p>Droplets land around both halves: step on each before it bursts on the raid.</p><p>Droplets land around both halves: step on each before it bursts on the raid. Every pickup hurts, so the tanks take most. 4 seconds after a soak, its venom flies straight back into Breath and hits anyone in the way.</p><ul><li>Droplets land all around both halves.</li><li>Each tank takes the ones on their side, always with a defensive up.</li><li>Everyone else takes one here and there.</li><li>Step off the line between your droplet and Breath, and never stand between the two bosses: 4 seconds after you soak it, its venom flies back into the boss.</li></ul><h3>Unstable Miasma (Soak together, puddles on the wall)</h3><p>The red boss puts a soak on a player in its half.</p><p>The red boss puts a soak on a player in its half. Everyone in it splits the hit and then drops a red puddle, so soak in your stack by Blood and drop the puddles side by side along the wall.</p><ul><li>Soak on you? Stay in the red half's stack by Blood.</li><li>The red half stacks in it with you.</li><li>It lands. Every soaker gets a puddle to drop.</li><li>Walk along the wall between the two bosses and drop them there, side by side.</li><li>Back to the boss. The middle stays clean.</li></ul><h3>Blighted Blood (Dispel it fast)</h3><p>Blood puts a poison on a few players in its half.</p><p>Blood puts a poison on a few players in its half. It keeps ticking until a healer dispels it. If it runs out, it drops a red puddle where the player stands.</p><ul><li>Blood puts Blighted Blood on a few players in its half, one after another.</li><li>Healers dispel it fast, each on a different player.</li><li>No dispel in time? Walk to the old puddles before it runs out, so the puddle lands next to them.</li></ul><h3>Helical Toxins (Make 4)</h3><p>At full energy both bosses go to the middle and take 99% less damage until everyone pairs up (up to 30 seconds); the weaker one heals to match.</p><p>At full energy both bosses go to the middle and take 99% less damage until everyone pairs up (up to 30 seconds); the weaker one heals to match. Everyone gets 1 to 3 green orbs: pair with one player to make exactly 4.</p><ul><li>The bosses go to the middle. Spread out.</li><li>Read your number: 1, 2 or 3 green orbs.</li><li>Pair up to make 4: a 1 with a 3, or two 2s. Touch only your partner.</li><li>A wrong pair blows up into a huge red puddle.</li></ul><h3>Tank swap (Taunt after every intermission)</h3><p>Each boss wears its tank down: Breath's Empowering Slam hits 10% harder with every hit on the same tank, and Blood's Bloodvenom Injection stacks a poison.</p><p>Each boss wears its tank down: Breath's Empowering Slam hits 10% harder with every hit on the same tank, and Blood's Bloodvenom Injection stacks a poison. So the tanks taunt-swap as every intermission ends, and the tank leaving Blood drops a huge puddle.</p><ul><li>Intermission over: the bosses leave the middle.</li><li>Both tanks taunt the other boss at the same moment. Your half stays where it is and the other boss comes to you.</li><li>Green ranged and healers: stack far, so the next add lands away from the tank's big puddle.</li><li>The tank leaving Blood drops a huge puddle a few seconds later: put its middle in a gap between old puddles.</li></ul><h3>Where each boss stands (Clean floor every round)</h3><p>The bosses start in the corners by their own gems.</p><p>The bosses start in the corners by their own gems. After every intermission the tanks swap and the bosses cross to the other half. From the third round they work up the walls onto clean floor.</p><ul><li>The pull: Breath goes into the corner by the green gem and Blood into the corner by the red gem, at the same end of the room.</li><li>First intermission: the tanks swap bosses. Your half stays on its side and the other boss crosses over to you.</li><li>Second intermission: the bosses go up the walls onto clean floor, each by its own gem again.</li></ul><h3>Where the puddles go (Along the walls)</h3><p>Every red puddle stays until the end of the fight.</p><p>Every red puddle stays until the end of the fight. They go along the walls in a set order, each soak right up against the one before, so the middle stays free.</p><ul><li>Round 1: the soak goes off in the corner by Blood. The nine soakers drop their puddles in a row along the wall behind it, side by side.</li><li>Round 2: the bosses swap sides. The old Blood tank drops the big puddle with its middle in the old puddles, and the new Blood half starts its own wall. The second soak goes right up against the first.</li><li>Round 3: Blood is back on the first side, further up the room. The big puddle goes into the old blood again and each soak stacks against the one before.</li></ul><h2>Putting it all together</h2><p>The whole fight in the order of a real kill. Quiet stretches are cut, and a loop that repeats plays once; the time shown is the real fight clock. Pick a moment to jump there.</p><p>The whole fight in order, timed by a real kill (4:06). Coloured lines are moments when two things happen at once.</p><ul><li>Every round after the first is rinse and repeat: the same order of events, the bosses swapped, a little further along the walls.</li><li>Stay with your boss: never inside 40 yards of the other one.</li><li>Red puddles stay down all fight: drop every new one next to the old ones, along the walls, and keep the middle clean.</li><li>After every intermission the tanks swap bosses: your half stays put and the other boss comes to you.</li></ul><h3>0:00–4:06</h3><p>We split in two before the pull: one half by the red gem, one half by the green gem.</p><p>Second droplets. Same jobs: each tank takes the ones on their side with a defensive, everyone else one here and there.</p><p>The last round: the soak puddles go next to the old ones. Burn both bosses down together.</p><h2>Your job, by role</h2><p>Every line for each role. The Your job box at the top shows the first three.</p><ul><li>Taunt-swap as every intermission ends. Leaving Blood, drop your big puddle with its middle in old blood.</li><li>Keep Breath on the far side of every add, so all the ranged can hit it.</li><li>Every add dies fast: ranged on both halves hit it from max range. The second one dies before the intermission.</li><li>Droplets: step off the line between your droplet and Breath.</li><li>Intermission: pair a 1 with a 3, or two 2s.</li></ul>"},"entombed-sentinels/mythic":{"boss":"Entombed Sentinels","d":"m","title":"Entombed Sentinels Mythic guide: mechanics, positions and every job · Debrief","desc":"Entombed Sentinels Mythic guide: Mistakes now kill: two droplets at once, a hit from a venom line, a short red soak. Shifting Protovenom puts circles on…","html":"<h1>Entombed Sentinels Mythic guide</h1><p>Mistakes now kill: two droplets at once, a hit from a venom line, a short red soak. Shifting Protovenom puts circles on eight players, and at the intermission the numbers hide after a few seconds. Clean play matters more than damage.</p><h3>What wipes us</h3><ul><li>Two droplets at once: A player who already took a droplet runs onto another one: two at once kills.</li><li>The soak left short: Only four players stand in Unstable Miasma, and it kills them.</li><li>A circle meets an empty player: A player with a circle walks into someone without one: Protovenom Eruption blasts and knocks back both, and the circle is still there.</li><li>A wrong pair: A 2 bumps into a pair that was already made: the wrong total sets off Cultivated Burst and a huge puddle.</li></ul><h3>Your job</h3><ul><li>Pull Breath into the corner by the green gem and Blood into the corner by the red gem, 40+ yards apart.</li><li>Both tanks: take the droplets on your side, always with a defensive, and grab the ones that land in red puddles.</li><li>Intermission: Taunt-swap as every intermission ends. Leaving Blood, drop your big puddle with its middle in old blood.</li><li>Dispel Blighted Blood fast.</li><li>Red soaks and droplets hurt the raid: be ready right after them.</li><li>Intermission: the bosses heal, the raid doesn't take much; make 4 with everyone else.</li><li>Stay with your boss: never in range of both.</li><li>Intermission: Every add dies fast: ranged on both halves hit it from max range. The second one dies before the intermission.</li><li>Red half: 8 or more in every soak, then puddles along the wall.</li></ul><h2>The fight: two Sentinels</h2><p>Round one is short, with the intermission at 0:46. Later rounds last about 100 seconds: two adds, two red soaks, three droplet sets, two sets of circles. The worst moment is just before the intermission: the second add, last droplets and second circles land together.</p><h3>Two bosses (Stay 40 yards apart)</h3><p>Within 40 yards of each other, both bosses take 99% less damage.</p><p>Within 40 yards of each other, both bosses take 99% less damage. Each boss also stacks its Mark on everyone within 40 yards, so stay with your own.</p><ul><li>One half on each boss. Pull Breath (green) into the corner by the green gem and Blood (red) into the corner by the red gem, at the same end of the room.</li><li>Keep the bosses 40+ yards apart. At those spots they're about 60 apart.</li><li>Stay with your boss. 20 Marks of Acid and 20 Marks of Blood on you at once kills you, so multi-dotters don't refresh into both.</li><li>Split the players who damage several targets at once (multi-dotters) evenly (a Warlock and a Balance Druid on one side, a Warlock and a Shadow Priest on the other) and put the strong two-target melee on green: it has more adds.</li><li>If damage is tight, line up the bosses' health before the intermission by swapping players around, not by stopping damage: the weaker one heals up to the stronger.</li></ul><h3>Venom Coagulation (Stack far, everyone burns it)</h3><p>The green boss drops a big slime on a ranged player.</p><p>The green boss drops a big slime on a ranged player. It pulses raid damage while it lives, so everyone who can hits it.</p><ul><li>The add is coming. Green ranged and healers: stack far, together, away from the tank and any big puddle.</li><li>It lands in your stack. Step off it.</li><li>The tank keeps Breath on the far side of the add. Melee cleave both; every ranged on both halves hits it from max range.</li><li>Dead: back to your spots.</li><li>The first add of each round after the first matters most: the tank who just left Blood is dropping a huge puddle, and an add baited near it can't be cleaved. Stack far from that puddle.</li><li>With Breath on the far side, the boss is more than 40 yards from the ranged hitting the add, so nobody picks up a Mark.</li><li>The second add of a round spawns right before the intermission and starts the worst overlap: burn it.</li></ul><h3>Toxic Droplets (Tanks and immunities)</h3><p>Droplets land around both halves: step on each before it bursts on the raid.</p><p>Droplets land around both halves: step on each before it bursts on the raid. Every pickup hurts and two at once kills, so the tanks take most. 4 seconds after a soak, its venom flies straight back into Breath and hits anyone in the way.</p><ul><li>Droplets land all around both halves.</li><li>Each tank takes the ones on their side, always with a defensive up.</li><li>Everyone else takes one here and there. Never two at once without an immunity.</li><li>Step off the line between your droplet and Breath: 4 seconds after you soak it, its venom flies back into the boss.</li><li>Tanks: a defensive for every set (for example a Druid's Barkskin, Incarnation or Survival Instincts). If nothing is up, wait for it, then soak hard.</li><li>Droplets that land in red puddles: the tank goes and grabs them.</li><li>The worst overlap of each later round: the second add, these droplets and the second circles, right after the second soak. Immunities soak them (Divine Shield, a Hunter's Turtle, a Rogue's Cloak; two per side), or raid cooldowns (Rallying Cry, Devotion Aura).</li></ul><h3>Unstable Miasma (8 or more soak, puddles on the wall)</h3><p>The red boss puts a soak on a player in its half.</p><p>The red boss puts a soak on a player in its half. Everyone in it splits the hit and then drops a red puddle, so soak in your stack by Blood and drop the puddles side by side along the wall.</p><ul><li>Soak on you? Stay in the red half's stack by Blood.</li><li>The red half stacks in it with you: 8 or more.</li><li>It lands. Every soaker gets a puddle to drop.</li><li>Walk along the wall between the two bosses and drop them there, side by side.</li><li>Back to the boss. The middle stays clean.</li><li>Have a set pattern for the puddles: along the wall between the bosses first, then the other walls. A clean floor is what saves a bad Protovenom or a bad soak.</li><li>Stack defensives on the soak: a Death Knight's Anti-Magic Zone or a Shaman's Spirit Link Totem helps.</li><li>Eight is the safe number. Our kills held with 6 or 7 later in the fight, but don't try that early.</li><li>Set up the next soak right next to the old puddles so the new ones land beside them, and use movement speed (a Druid's Stampeding Roar) to get in and out. Each round repeats a little further along the wall.</li><li>Dispel Blighted Blood fast: if it runs out it drops a puddle too, so let it drop near the others.</li></ul><h3>Shifting Protovenom (Circle to circle)</h3><p>Eight players get a 6-yard circle that hurts them every 2 seconds until it's cleared.</p><p>Eight players get a 6-yard circle that hurts them every 2 seconds until it's cleared. Two circles touching clear each other. A circle touching anyone without one erupts: damage and a big knockback for both, and the circle stays.</p><ul><li>Spread out before it lands. Tanks and melee too.</li><li>Four circles go to each side (sometimes five on one side).</li><li>Circle on you? Wait a moment, find your partner circle, and walk to them.</li><li>No circle? Stay put and let them come to each other.</li><li>Both circles clear when they touch.</li><li>Don't rush: pause a second or two before walking, unless the path is obvious.</li><li>Sometimes a side gets five. The extra one runs over to the other half to clear.</li><li>A multi-dotter in range of both bosses can be picked as a fifth on one side: step out of the other boss's range.</li></ul><h3>Blighted Blood (Dispel it fast)</h3><p>Blood puts a poison on a few players in its half.</p><p>Blood puts a poison on a few players in its half. It keeps ticking until a healer dispels it. If it runs out, it drops a red puddle where the player stands.</p><ul><li>Blood puts Blighted Blood on a few players in its half, one after another.</li><li>Healers dispel it fast, each on a different player.</li><li>No dispel in time? Walk to the old puddles before it runs out, so the puddle lands next to them.</li></ul><h3>Helical Toxins (Make 4)</h3><p>At full energy both bosses go to the middle and take 99% less damage until everyone pairs up (up to 30 seconds); the weaker one heals to match.</p><p>At full energy both bosses go to the middle and take 99% less damage until everyone pairs up (up to 30 seconds); the weaker one heals to match. Everyone gets 1 to 3 green orbs: pair with one player to make exactly 4.</p><ul><li>The bosses go to the middle. Spread out.</li><li>Your number shows for a moment, then hides. Remember it. If a partner is right next to you, clear at once.</li><li>Players with a 1 jump in place and ping themselves. Players with a 3 run to a jumping, pinging player. Players with a 2 go under a boss and pair up there.</li><li>A wrong pair blows up into a huge red puddle: that usually wipes.</li><li>The traffic rules never change, so practice them: players with a 2 go under the boss, 1s jump and ping (a ping macro helps), 3s find the pingers. Some guides swap the 1s and 3s: pick one system for the whole raid.</li><li>Keep the bosses' health even going in: a 5% gap is a lot of free healing for the weaker one. Multi-dotters push the higher one.</li><li>Failed? Go to a far corner and stop bumping: bumping grows the puddle. An immunity survives it.</li><li>It ends the moment everyone has paired up (11 to 19 seconds on our kills), so a clean solve gives you the bosses back sooner.</li></ul><h3>Tank swap (Taunt after every intermission)</h3><p>Each boss wears its tank down: Breath's Empowering Slam hits 10% harder with every hit on the same tank, and Blood's Bloodvenom Injection stacks a poison.</p><p>Each boss wears its tank down: Breath's Empowering Slam hits 10% harder with every hit on the same tank, and Blood's Bloodvenom Injection stacks a poison. So the tanks taunt-swap as every intermission ends, and the tank leaving Blood drops a huge puddle.</p><ul><li>Intermission over: the bosses leave the middle.</li><li>Both tanks taunt the other boss at the same moment. Your half stays where it is and the other boss comes to you.</li><li>Green ranged and healers: stack far, so the next add lands away from the tank's big puddle.</li><li>The tank leaving Blood drops a huge puddle a few seconds later: put its middle in a gap between old puddles.</li><li>The big puddle grows from its middle and doesn't drift off other puddles, so with its middle in old blood most of it overlaps used floor.</li><li>Your Mark changes with your boss after the swap: the old stacks fall off on their own.</li><li>Call the taunts on voice: both at once, as the bosses walk out of the middle.</li></ul><h3>Where each boss stands (Clean floor every round)</h3><p>The bosses start in the corners by their own gems.</p><p>The bosses start in the corners by their own gems. After every intermission the tanks swap and the bosses cross to the other half. From the third round they work up the walls onto clean floor.</p><ul><li>The pull: Breath goes into the corner by the green gem and Blood into the corner by the red gem, at the same end of the room.</li><li>First intermission: the tanks swap bosses. Your half stays on its side and the other boss crosses over to you.</li><li>Second intermission: the bosses go up the walls onto clean floor, each by its own gem again.</li><li>Third intermission: swap again. The bosses cross to each other's side.</li><li>Fourth intermission: swap one last time. The last round is played out here, and both bosses die before the 7:00 enrage.</li></ul><h3>Where the puddles go (Along the walls)</h3><p>Every red puddle stays until the end of the fight.</p><p>Every red puddle stays until the end of the fight. They go along the walls in a set order, each soak right up against the one before, so the middle stays free.</p><ul><li>Round 1: the soak goes off in the corner by Blood. The nine soakers drop their puddles in a row along the wall behind it, side by side.</li><li>Round 2: the bosses swap sides. The old Blood tank drops the big puddle with its middle in the old puddles, and the new Blood half starts its own wall. The second soak goes right up against the first.</li><li>Round 3: Blood is back on the first side, further up the room. The big puddle goes into the old blood again and each soak stacks against the one before.</li><li>Round 4: the bosses swap again, further up the room. The same again: the big puddle into the old blood, each soak against the one before.</li><li>Round 5: the last soak goes into the corner nook, to save room.</li></ul><p>Both bosses go berserk at exactly 7:00, about a minute after the fourth intermission. Burn them down together before then, and kill the last add fast.</p><h2>Putting it all together</h2><p>The whole fight in the order of a real kill. Quiet stretches are cut, and a loop that repeats plays once; the time shown is the real fight clock. Pick a moment to jump there.</p><p>The whole fight in order, timed by a real kill (7:08). Coloured lines are moments when two things happen at once.</p><ul><li>Every round after the first is rinse and repeat: the same order of events, the bosses swapped, a little further along the walls.</li><li>Stay with your boss: never inside 40 yards of the other one.</li><li>Red puddles stay down a long time: drop every new one next to the old ones, along the walls, and keep the middle clean.</li><li>After every intermission the tanks swap bosses: your half stays put and the other boss comes to you.</li></ul><h3>0:00–7:08</h3><p>We split in two before the pull: one half by the red gem, one half by the green gem.</p><p>The worst overlap of the fight: the second add, the last droplets and the second set of circles all land together, right after the second soak.</p><p>The bosses go to the middle. Spread out. Your number shows for a moment, then hides.</p><p>Second droplets. Same jobs: each tank takes the ones on their side with a defensive, everyone else one here and there.</p><p>Fifth round: the soak puddles go into the corner nooks to save space.</p><h2>Your job, by role</h2><p>Every line for each role. The Your job box at the top shows the first three.</p><ul><li>Taunt-swap as every intermission ends. Leaving Blood, drop your big puddle with its middle in old blood.</li><li>Keep Breath on the far side of every add, so all the ranged can hit it.</li><li>Every add dies fast: ranged on both halves hit it from max range. The second one dies before the intermission.</li><li>Intermission: players with a 1 jump and ping, players with a 3 find a pinger, players with a 2 go under a boss.</li><li>Droplets: step off the line between your droplet and Breath.</li></ul>"},"the-lost-explorers/heroic":{"boss":"The Lost Explorers","d":"h","title":"The Lost Explorers Heroic guide: mechanics, positions and every job · Debrief","desc":"The Lost Explorers Heroic guide: Three bosses: two tanked together, the third 30+ yards away, and whichever boss has eaten the fish is the one on its own.…","html":"<h1>The Lost Explorers Heroic guide</h1><p>Three bosses: two tanked together, the third 30+ yards away, and whichever boss has eaten the fish is the one on its own. Break crates in pairs, feed the fish at 95 energy, survive each boss's ultimate, and bring all three down together.</p><h3>What wipes us</h3><ul><li>The fish fed too late: The fish is held too long: Mor'zahi reaches full energy and Final Ascension wipes the raid.</li><li>Off the mushroom when the wave hits: A player isn't on the mushroom when the Blast Wave arrives: it hits for millions.</li><li>A missile next to a debuffed player: A second-volley target stands next to someone who still has Burning Flames: Elemental Explosion hits the whole raid.</li><li>The last landing left alone: The raid doesn't reach the last landing: Nama's leap hits the marked player alone.</li></ul><h3>Your job</h3><ul><li>Iku next to Gebbo, Nama 30+ yards away; the boss with its ultimate is the one apart.</li><li>Swap on every Shredding Shards: trade bosses.</li><li>Kick Icebound Flames first in the order.</li><li>Dispel any Icebound Flames that lands.</li><li>Blink Nova hurts the whole raid: be ready for it.</li><li>Cooldowns for Frostfire clears and the Mighty Thud soaks.</li><li>Melee: never step on a crate by accident.</li><li>Shell Spin on you: take it to the outside.</li><li>Blink Nova on you: out in front, toward the edge.</li></ul><h2>Phase 1: before the first fish</h2><p>All fight long: two bosses together, one apart, crates, shells, Blink Nova, kicks and tank swaps. Then the fish, three times.</p><h3>United Defense (Two together, one apart)</h3><p>If all three are within 30 yards of each other they take 99% less damage.</p><p>If all three are within 30 yards of each other they take 99% less damage. Gebbo can't be tanked: he walks a big circle round the room all fight.</p><ul><li>Iku's tank keeps her right next to Gebbo as he walks. Melee cleave both.</li><li>Ranged stand in the middle and hit all three.</li><li>Nama's tank keeps her 30+ yards behind the pair.</li></ul><h3>Crates (Break them in pairs)</h3><p>Gebbo throws crates onto marked spots.</p><p>Gebbo throws crates onto marked spots. Walking over one breaks it and bleeds you (Splinters). One crate holds the fish. A crate left too long explodes.</p><ul><li>Marked spots: crates are landing. Step out of them.</li><li>Two assigned breakers walk over two crates together.</li><li>Wait a few seconds for the bleed to fade, then the next two.</li><li>The fish carrier picks up the fish. Everyone else watches their feet.</li></ul><h3>Shell Spin (Take it outside)</h3><p>Nama throws three spinning shells in a cone at a random melee.</p><p>Nama throws three spinning shells in a cone at a random melee. Anyone they touch is stunned for 4 seconds.</p><ul><li>The melee picked steps to the outside of the room.</li><li>The shells fly outward, away from the ranged.</li><li>Then step out of their line.</li></ul><h3>Blink Nova (Out in front)</h3><p>Iku marks a ranged player and teleports to them 7 seconds later.</p><p>Iku marks a ranged player and teleports to them 7 seconds later. The nova hits the whole raid, less the farther away you are.</p><ul><li>Marked? Walk out in front of the raid, toward the edge.</li><li>Iku lands on you: the nova is weaker for everyone else.</li><li>Her tank picks her back up.</li></ul><p>Plan: Go ahead of where Gebbo is walking, so the bosses don't drift into each other and become immune. It always picks a ranged player.</p><h3>Icebound Flames (Kick every one)</h3><p>Iku casts Icebound Flames over and over.</p><p>Iku casts Icebound Flames over and over. A kick stops it; if one goes off, it lands on a player and needs a dispel.</p><ul><li>Kick on a set order: her tank first, then melee with short kicks.</li><li>Next kicker ready as soon as one lands.</li><li>Healers: if one goes off, dispel it.</li></ul><h3>Shredding Shards (Swap at once)</h3><p>Iku's hit raises her tank's magic damage taken.</p><p>Iku's hit raises her tank's magic damage taken. Nama's hits stack Steady Strikes on hers, more physical damage each time; above about 25 stacks her hits kill.</p><ul><li>Shredding Shards: swap right away.</li><li>The tanks trade bosses: each taunts the other's.</li><li>Never wait for a Shell Spin or a Blink to swap.</li></ul><h3>The fish (Feed it at 95 energy)</h3><p>Mor'zahi's energy climbs to Final Ascension, a wipe.</p><p>Mor'zahi's energy climbs to Final Ascension, a wipe. Feeding the fish from Gebbo's crates to one of the bosses resets it, and gives that boss its ultimate for a while. Each boss eats once.</p><ul><li>The carrier picks the fish out of a crate.</li><li>At about 95 energy, feed it to the next boss.</li><li>Mor'zahi starts over; that boss gets its ultimate.</li><li>That boss becomes the one kept apart.</li></ul><h3>Evil Eyes (Step out of the swirls)</h3><p>Right after each fish, the statues fire swirls at the raid for about 5 seconds.</p><p>Right after each fish, the statues fire swirls at the raid for about 5 seconds. Anyone within 3 yards of where one lands takes a big hit.</p><ul><li>The fish is fed. A moment later the statues start firing swirls at the raid.</li><li>Watch the floor: each swirl lands in a circle about 3 yards wide. Step out of it before it hits.</li><li>They keep coming for about 5 seconds. Move off each new one as it shows.</li></ul><h2>Nama's turn</h2><p>After her fish Nama casts Mighty Thud. Gebbo and Iku are tanked together, Nama on her own. Then bring all three down together.</p><h3>Mighty Thud (Ride the knockbacks)</h3><p>Nama marks three players and leaps to each in turn.</p><p>Nama marks three players and leaps to each in turn. Each landing is split between everyone within 6 yards and knocks them back; a landing that hits nobody hits the whole raid. Each leaves quaking ground behind. She does it twice, a little while apart.</p><ul><li>The three marked players stand in a line.</li><li>Everyone stacks on the near side of the first landing.</li><li>Each knockback throws the raid on to the next landing.</li></ul><p>Bring all three down together: one dying early enrages the others. If one has to enrage, make it Gebbo: a tank taunts him and walks him around. (Some guides kill Gebbo first; this plan leaves him last because he's the easiest to walk around.)</p><h2>Gebbo's turn</h2><p>After his fish Gebbo throws bombs: two rounds of Explosive Surprise. Iku and Nama are tanked together, away from his circle.</p><h3>Explosive Surprise (Bounce over the wave)</h3><p>Gebbo tosses a Bouncy Mushroom and marks a player: 10 seconds later a bomb lands on them, then blows, sending a deadly Blast Wave across the room.</p><p>Gebbo tosses a Bouncy Mushroom and marks a player: 10 seconds later a bomb lands on them, then blows, sending a deadly Blast Wave across the room. A mushroom bounces you high into the air, over the wave.</p><ul><li>The marked player takes the bomb to the edge, across from the mushroom.</li><li>Everyone else gathers on the mushroom. Don't touch it yet.</li><li>The bomb lands, then blows: the wave rolls out.</li><li>Touch the mushroom just as the wave reaches it: bounce over.</li></ul><h2>Iku's turn</h2><p>After her fish Iku casts Frostfire Volley, twice. Gebbo and Nama are tanked together, and Iku on her own.</p><h3>Frostfire Volley (Spread, then clear in pairs)</h3><p>Missiles hit several players: ranged get Burning Flames, melee Piercing Frost, and everyone within 10 yards takes the hit.</p><p>Missiles hit several players: ranged get Burning Flames, melee Piercing Frost, and everyone within 10 yards takes the hit. Each leaves a fire or frost patch. A missile hitting someone who still has either debuff sets off Elemental Explosion on the whole raid.</p><ul><li>Fire on the ranged, frost on the melee: spread round the edge of the raid in fire and frost pairs, 12 yards apart.</li><li>Keep away from the next volley's targets while you still have your debuff.</li><li>Clear: frost steps into the fire patch, fire into the frost patch.</li><li>One pair at a time: each clear is a raid hit.</li></ul><h2>Putting it all together</h2><p>The whole fight in the order of a real kill. Quiet stretches are cut, and a loop that repeats plays once; the time shown is the real fight clock. Pick a moment to jump there.</p><p>The whole fight in order, timed by a real kill (3:06). Coloured lines are moments when two things happen at once.</p><ul><li>Two bosses together, one 30+ yards away: never all three within 30 yards.</li><li>The boss that ate the fish is the one kept apart.</li><li>Crates two at a time, a few seconds apart: each one makes the whole raid bleed.</li><li>Kick every Icebound Flames; swap tanks on every Shredding Shards.</li></ul><h3>0:00–0:43</h3><p>Iku's tank brings her right next to Gebbo, and the melee cleave both.</p><p>Marked? Walk out in front of the raid, toward the edge. Iku lands on you: the nova is weaker for everyone else.</p><h3>Nama's turn0:43–2:47</h3><h3>Gebbo's turn2:47–3:06</h3><p>Bring all three down together: one dying early enrages the others.</p><h2>Your job, by role</h2><p>Every line for each role. The Your job box at the top shows the first three.</p><ul><li>Frostfire: spread in fire and frost pairs; clear one pair at a time.</li></ul>"},"the-lost-explorers/mythic":{"boss":"The Lost Explorers","d":"m","title":"The Lost Explorers Mythic guide: mechanics, positions and every job · Debrief","desc":"The Lost Explorers Mythic guide: Three bosses: two tanked together and the one that ate the fish 30+ yards away. Crates make the raid bleed, so pop them…","html":"<h1>The Lost Explorers Mythic guide</h1><p>Three bosses: two tanked together and the one that ate the fish 30+ yards away. Crates make the raid bleed, so pop them two at a time. Feed the fish at 95 energy, survive each ultimate, and bring all three down together.</p><h3>What wipes us</h3><ul><li>The fish fed too late: The fish is held too long: Mor'zahi reaches full energy and Final Ascension wipes the raid.</li><li>Off the mushroom when the wave hits: A player isn't on the mushroom when the Blast Wave arrives: it hits for millions.</li><li>A missile next to a debuffed player: A second-volley target stands next to someone who still has Burning Flames: Elemental Explosion hits the whole raid.</li><li>The last landing left alone: The raid doesn't reach the last landing: Nama's leap hits the marked player alone.</li></ul><h3>Your job</h3><ul><li>Iku next to Gebbo, Nama 30+ yards away; the boss with its ultimate is the one apart.</li><li>Swap on every Shredding Shards: trade bosses.</li><li>Kick Icebound Flames first in the order.</li><li>Crates bleed the whole raid: heal through each pair. Our kills used four healers; the fastest raids use three.</li><li>Dispel any Icebound Flames that lands.</li><li>Blink Nova hurts the whole raid: be ready for it.</li><li>Melee: never step on a crate by accident.</li><li>Shell Spin on you: take it to the outside.</li><li>Blink Nova on you: out in front, toward the edge.</li></ul><h2>Phase 1: before the first fish</h2><p>All fight long: two bosses together, one apart, crates, shells, Blink Nova, kicks and tank swaps. Then the fish, three times.</p><h3>United Defense (Two together, one apart)</h3><p>If all three are within 30 yards of each other they take 99% less damage.</p><p>If all three are within 30 yards of each other they take 99% less damage. Gebbo can't be tanked: he walks a big circle round the room all fight.</p><ul><li>Iku's tank keeps her right next to Gebbo as he walks. Melee cleave both.</li><li>Ranged stand in the middle and hit all three.</li><li>Nama's tank keeps her 30+ yards behind the pair.</li><li>Hit Gebbo early: you won't be near him much later.</li><li>Bosses that die early enrage the others (Gebbo's shovel, Iku's Cataclysmic Invocation, Nama's Relentless Escalation): keep their health even.</li><li>Evokers, Boomkins, Shadow Priests and Warlocks can hit all three from the middle.</li></ul><h3>Crates (Break them in pairs)</h3><p>Gebbo throws crates onto marked spots.</p><p>Gebbo throws crates onto marked spots. Walking over one breaks it and bleeds the whole raid (Splinters). One crate holds the fish. A crate left too long explodes.</p><ul><li>Marked spots: crates are landing. Step out of them.</li><li>Two assigned breakers walk over two crates together.</li><li>Wait a few seconds for the bleed to fade, then the next two.</li><li>The fish carrier picks up the fish. Everyone else watches their feet.</li><li>Four crates a set, six when the fish is in it: pop two, wait for the bleed to drop, pop two more. Never three at once, and never during a Blink Nova.</li><li>Breakers: the off-tank with a mobile ranged, then two mobile ranged. The fish carrier is someone else.</li><li>Melee: stepping on a crate by accident is the most common wipe here.</li><li>Don't break crates during Gebbo's bomb.</li></ul><h3>Shell Spin (Take it outside)</h3><p>Nama throws three spinning shells in a cone at a random melee.</p><p>Nama throws three spinning shells in a cone at a random melee. Anyone they touch is stunned for 4 seconds.</p><ul><li>The melee picked steps to the outside of the room.</li><li>The shells fly outward, away from the ranged.</li><li>Then step out of their line.</li><li>Call every Shell Spin.</li><li>When Nama is the boss on her own, the shells come from far away: just dodge them.</li></ul><h3>Blink Nova (Out in front)</h3><p>Iku marks a ranged player and teleports to them 7 seconds later.</p><p>Iku marks a ranged player and teleports to them 7 seconds later. The nova hits the whole raid, less the farther away you are.</p><ul><li>Marked? Walk out in front of the raid, toward the edge.</li><li>Iku lands on you: the nova is weaker for everyone else.</li><li>Her tank picks her back up.</li></ul><p>Plan: Go ahead of where Gebbo is walking, so the bosses don't drift into each other and become immune. It always picks a ranged player.</p><h3>Icebound Flames (Kick every one)</h3><p>Iku casts Icebound Flames over and over.</p><p>Iku casts Icebound Flames over and over. A kick stops it; if one goes off, it lands on a player and needs a dispel.</p><ul><li>Kick on a set order: her tank first, then melee with short kicks.</li><li>Next kicker ready as soon as one lands.</li><li>Healers: if one goes off, dispel it.</li></ul><h3>Shredding Shards (Swap at once)</h3><p>Iku's hit raises her tank's magic damage taken.</p><p>Iku's hit raises her tank's magic damage taken. Nama's hits stack Steady Strikes on hers, more physical damage each time; above about 25 stacks her hits kill.</p><ul><li>Shredding Shards: swap right away.</li><li>The tanks trade bosses: each taunts the other's.</li><li>Never wait for a Shell Spin or a Blink to swap.</li></ul><h3>The fish (Feed it at 95 energy)</h3><p>Mor'zahi's energy climbs to Final Ascension, a wipe.</p><p>Mor'zahi's energy climbs to Final Ascension, a wipe. Feeding the fish from Gebbo's crates to one of the bosses resets it, and gives that boss its ultimate for a while. Each boss eats once.</p><ul><li>The carrier picks the fish out of a crate.</li><li>At about 95 energy, feed it to the next boss.</li><li>Mor'zahi starts over; that boss gets its ultimate.</li><li>That boss becomes the one kept apart.</li><li>The raid's order: Gebbo, then Iku, then Nama. Any order works (Tactyks feeds Iku, Nama, then Gebbo; the written guides Gebbo, Nama, Iku).</li><li>Northern Sky Raid Tools (an addon) counts the energy down.</li><li>Some raids wait for the short cast Mor'zahi starts at 100. It buys a few seconds but leaves no room for a late fish.</li></ul><h3>Evil Eyes (Step out of the swirls)</h3><p>Right after each fish, the statues fire swirls at the raid for about 5 seconds.</p><p>Right after each fish, the statues fire swirls at the raid for about 5 seconds. Anyone within 3 yards of where one lands takes a big hit.</p><ul><li>The fish is fed. A moment later the statues start firing swirls at the raid.</li><li>Watch the floor: each swirl lands in a circle about 3 yards wide. Step out of it before it hits.</li><li>They keep coming for about 5 seconds. Move off each new one as it shows.</li></ul><h2>Gebbo's turn</h2><p>After his fish Gebbo throws bombs: two rounds of Explosive Surprise. Iku and Nama are tanked together, away from his circle.</p><h3>Explosive Surprise (Bounce over the wave)</h3><p>Gebbo tosses a single Bouncy Mushroom for each bomb and marks a player: 10 seconds later a bomb lands on them, then blows, sending a deadly Blast Wave across the room.</p><p>Gebbo tosses a single Bouncy Mushroom for each bomb and marks a player: 10 seconds later a bomb lands on them, then blows, sending a deadly Blast Wave across the room. The mushroom bounces you high into the air, over the wave.</p><ul><li>The marked player takes the bomb to the edge, across from the mushroom.</li><li>Everyone else gathers on the mushroom. Don't touch it yet.</li><li>The bomb lands, then blows: the wave rolls out.</li><li>Touch the mushroom just as the wave reaches it: bounce over.</li><li>There is one mushroom for each bomb: bait it to a good spot, and don't waste it.</li><li>The second bomb's mushroom comes up near you as you land from the first bounce: stay near Gebbo, and don't touch it until the wave comes.</li><li>Each bomb leaves a fire puddle: drop it at the edge.</li></ul><h2>Iku's turn</h2><p>After her fish Iku casts Frostfire Volley, twice. Gebbo and Nama are tanked together, and Iku on her own.</p><h3>Frostfire Volley (Spread, then clear in pairs)</h3><p>Missiles hit several players: ranged get Burning Flames, melee Piercing Frost, and everyone within 10 yards takes the hit.</p><p>Missiles hit several players: ranged get Burning Flames, melee Piercing Frost, and everyone within 10 yards takes the hit. Each leaves a fire or frost patch. A missile hitting someone who still has either debuff sets off Elemental Explosion on the whole raid.</p><ul><li>Fire on the ranged, frost on the melee: spread round the edge of the raid in fire and frost pairs, 12 yards apart.</li><li>Keep away from the next volley's targets while you still have your debuff.</li><li>Clear: frost steps into the fire patch, fire into the frost patch.</li><li>One pair at a time: each clear is a raid hit.</li><li>Use a personal defensive here: the damage-over-time on fire and frost targets is dangerous.</li><li>Ranged: a tank with frost needs a fire patch to clear on: leave them one.</li><li>Patches stay if their player dies.</li></ul><h2>Nama's turn</h2><p>After her fish Nama casts Mighty Thud. Gebbo and Iku are tanked together, Nama on her own. Then bring all three down together.</p><h3>Mighty Thud (Ride the knockbacks)</h3><p>Nama marks three players and leaps to each in turn.</p><p>Nama marks three players and leaps to each in turn. Each landing is split between everyone within 6 yards and knocks them back; a landing that hits nobody hits the whole raid. Each leaves quaking ground behind. She does it twice, a little while apart.</p><ul><li>The three marked players stand in a line.</li><li>Everyone stacks on the near side of the first landing.</li><li>Each knockback throws the raid on to the next landing.</li><li>Don't let crate bleeds or a Blink Nova land on top of the soaks: that's the scariest moment of the fight. Use a Shaman's Spirit Link Totem or defensives if they do.</li><li>The soaks leave quaking ground behind: stay off it, and line the second set up on fresh floor.</li></ul><p>Bring all three down together: one dying early enrages the others. If one has to enrage, make it Gebbo: a tank taunts him and walks him around. (Some guides kill Gebbo first; this plan leaves him last because he's the easiest to walk around.)</p><h2>Putting it all together</h2><p>The whole fight in the order of a real kill. Quiet stretches are cut, and a loop that repeats plays once; the time shown is the real fight clock. Pick a moment to jump there.</p><p>The whole fight in order, timed by a real kill (6:10). Coloured lines are moments when two things happen at once.</p><ul><li>Two bosses together, one 30+ yards away: never all three within 30 yards.</li><li>The boss that ate the fish is the one kept apart.</li><li>Crates two at a time, a few seconds apart: each one makes the whole raid bleed.</li><li>Kick every Icebound Flames; swap tanks on every Shredding Shards.</li></ul><h3>0:00–0:57</h3><p>Iku's tank brings her right next to Gebbo, and the melee cleave both.</p><p>Marked? Walk out in front of the raid, toward the edge. Iku lands on you: the nova is weaker for everyone else.</p><h3>Gebbo's turn0:57–2:13</h3><h3>2:13–2:50</h3><h3>Iku's turn2:50–2:54</h3><h3>2:54–3:01</h3><h3>Iku's turn3:01–4:17</h3><h3>4:17–4:50</h3><h3>Nama's turn4:50–6:10</h3><p>Bring all three down together: one dying early enrages the others.</p><h2>Your job, by role</h2><p>Every line for each role. The Your job box at the top shows the first three.</p><ul><li>Cooldowns for Frostfire clears and the Mighty Thud soaks.</li><li>Frostfire: spread in fire and frost pairs; clear one pair at a time.</li></ul>"},"vashnik-the-malignant/heroic":{"boss":"Vashnik the Malignant","d":"h","title":"Vashnik the Malignant Heroic guide: mechanics, positions and every job · Debrief","desc":"Vashnik the Malignant Heroic guide: Hold Vashnik by the Shadow fountain for every Imbibe, so it's always Shadow and Flame and never Blood. Grip the adds…","html":"<h1>Vashnik the Malignant Heroic guide</h1><p>Hold Vashnik by the Shadow fountain for every Imbibe, so it's always Shadow and Flame and never Blood. Grip the adds in and kill the two Burning Venoms apart, soak every bile, dispel the fire infections a few at a time, and keep your Plague Froth waves off the raid.</p><h3>What wipes us</h3><ul><li>Imbibe from the wrong spot: The boss is tanked toward the Blood fountain: Imbibe takes Shadow and Blood instead of Shadow and Flame.</li><li>Two fire adds die together: Two Burning Venoms die at once: two Caustic Surges hit the raid together.</li><li>A wave through the raid: A Froth target stays by the raid: their lines run straight through the people next to them.</li><li>An empty bile: Nobody stands in one bile circle: it hits the whole raid instead.</li></ul><h3>Your job</h3><ul><li>Hold him by the Shadow fountain for every Imbibe.</li><li>Swap on every Dripping Fangs, with a defensive for the bite.</li><li>Four healers: raid cooldowns on every Imbibe, which hits harder each time.</li><li>Exploding Infection: dispel a few at a time, spaced out, when the raid is healthy.</li><li>Stygian Infection: heal the absorb off while the player walks the edge.</li><li>Death Knights: grip one Burning Venom to the boss, and the other in when it is time. The rest: kill what the boss is cleaving.</li><li>Plague Froth: walk out, stand still and aim the lines away from the raid.</li><li>Biles: melee take the ones by the boss, ranged the far ones.</li></ul><h2>The loop: Imbibe to Imbibe</h2><p>One phase on repeat: Imbibe, the venoms, a Plague Froth, biles and infections, then the next Imbibe. Rinse and repeat, each time with bigger hits and healthier adds.</p><h3>Imbibe (Always Shadow and Flame)</h3><p>At full energy, about every 84 seconds, he drains the two fountains nearest him.</p><p>At full energy, about every 84 seconds, he drains the two fountains nearest him. Each one gives a raid hit, its own adds and infection, and an infusion that makes that fountain's next hit and adds stronger.</p><ul><li>The boss tank holds him by the Shadow fountain, every Imbibe.</li><li>He drains Shadow and Flame: two raid hits, bigger each time.</li><li>Shadow and Flame adds come out; never touch Blood.</li></ul><p>Plan: Same spot every time means little movement, but the infusions stack, so every Imbibe hits harder. Stack up for raid cooldowns on each one.</p><h3>The venoms (Grip them in, kill the fire adds apart)</h3><p>Each Imbibe sends venoms to the cavity.</p><p>Each Imbibe sends venoms to the cavity. Shrouded Venoms (Shadow) have a shield as big as their health and leave swirls when they die. Burning Venoms (Flame) hurt the raid while alive and when they die. Any that reach the cavity hit the raid hard.</p><ul><li>Shrouded Venoms (five): pull them onto the boss and cleave.</li><li>Burning Venoms (two): crowd control the far one, a Warlock banish or a trap.</li><li>A Death Knight grips the first one to the boss and kills it.</li><li>Then grip the second one in and kill it about 9 seconds later.</li><li>Gripping means pulling an add to you with Death Grip. Gorefiend's Grasp (Death Knight), Typhoon (Druid) or Sigil of Chains (Demon Hunter) gathers the Shrouded Venoms onto the boss.</li><li>Anything alive for 60 seconds hardens: immune to crowd control and faster, so kill on spawn.</li><li>Short of Death Knights? Ranged swap to the Burning Venoms and keep them crowd controlled so they never reach the middle.</li></ul><h3>Plague Froth (Walk out, aim away)</h3><p>About one player in three is marked.</p><p>About one player in three is marked. Six seconds later each fires a wave north, south, east and west.</p><ul><li>About one player in three gets Plague Froth.</li><li>Walk out from the raid to a spot where none of your four lines points at anyone.</li><li>Stand still until it fires.</li><li>Everyone else: step out of the lines.</li><li>The waves run straight north, south, east and west on the room, so stand off to one corner of the raid, not in line with anyone.</li><li>The first one comes about 13 seconds in, before the first Imbibe; then twice after every Imbibe, about 30 and 63 seconds after it.</li></ul><h3>Malignant Catalyst (Someone in every bile)</h3><p>A raid hit, then bile circles land around the room 7 seconds later.</p><p>A raid hit, then bile circles land around the room 7 seconds later. Each one is split between the players in it; one that lands on nobody hits the whole raid.</p><ul><li>Raid hit: biles are coming.</li><li>Cover every bile: melee take the ones by the boss, ranged and a healer the far ones.</li><li>Stay in until it goes off.</li></ul><p>Plan: About one bile for every three players: cover yours. They come twice per Imbibe, about 11 and 50 seconds after it.</p><h3>Infections (Walk out, dispel a few at a time)</h3><p>Fire and Shadow each hand out an infection.</p><p>Fire and Shadow each hand out an infection. Exploding Infection (Flame): removing it blasts the raid, less the farther away. Stygian Infection (Shadow): a big heal absorb, and you drop circles as you move.</p><ul><li>Exploding Infection (two players): walk out from the raid.</li><li>Healers dispel them one at a time, spaced out, and only when the raid is healthy.</li><li>Stygian Infection (two players): keep walking at the edge, leaving your circles away from everyone.</li><li>Healers heal the absorb off.</li></ul><p>Plan: Twice per Imbibe, about 19 and 71 seconds after it. Never dispel right before an Imbibe: the blast lands on top of the Imbibe hit.</p><h3>Dripping Fangs (Swap on every one)</h3><p>About every 28 seconds he bites his tank: much more physical damage taken and stacking damage over time.</p><ul><li>The boss tank uses a defensive for the bite.</li><li>Dripping Fangs lands: much more physical damage taken.</li><li>The other tank taunts right away, every time.</li></ul><p>Each Imbibe hits harder than the last. Stack up and use raid cooldowns and defensives on every one.</p><p>Constant raid damage that climbs with every Imbibe, so the longer the fight, the harder the healing. Burn him before it piles up.</p><h2>Putting it all together</h2><p>The whole fight in the order of a real kill. Quiet stretches are cut, and a loop that repeats plays once; the time shown is the real fight clock. Pick a moment to jump there.</p><p>The whole fight in order, timed by a real kill (7:07). Coloured lines are moments when two things happen at once.</p><ul><li>Every Imbibe from the same spot by the Shadow fountain: Shadow and Flame, never Blood.</li><li>The two Burning Venoms die apart: one is gripped to the boss and killed, the other comes in about 9 seconds later.</li><li>Dripping Fangs about every 28 seconds: the tanks swap every time.</li><li>Plague Froth comes about 13 seconds in and twice after every Imbibe: walk out, stand still, aim the lines away from the raid.</li><li>Biles and infections come twice per Imbibe; the second infections land about 13 seconds before the next Imbibe.</li><li>Every Imbibe hits harder than the last: the infusions stack, so cooldowns go on every one.</li></ul><h3>0:00–7:07</h3><p>Bloodlust on the pull. Imbibe comes early: the tank takes him straight to his spot by the Shadow fountain.</p><p>The boss tank uses a defensive for the bite. Dripping Fangs lands: much more physical damage taken.</p><p>About one player in three gets Plague Froth. Walk out from the raid to a spot where none of your four lines points at anyone.</p><p>Raid hit: biles are coming. Cover every bile: melee take the ones by the boss, ranged and a healer the far ones.</p><p>Vashnik is down.</p><h2>Your job, by role</h2><p>Every line for each role. The Your job box at the top shows the first three.</p>"},"vashnik-the-malignant/mythic":{"boss":"Vashnik the Malignant","d":"m","title":"Vashnik the Malignant Mythic guide: mechanics, positions and every job · Debrief","desc":"Vashnik the Malignant Mythic guide: Hold Vashnik by the Shadow fountain for every Imbibe, so it's always Shadow and Flame and never Blood. Each Imbibe…","html":"<h1>Vashnik the Malignant Mythic guide</h1><p>Hold Vashnik by the Shadow fountain for every Imbibe, so it's always Shadow and Flame and never Blood. Each Imbibe raises about two dozen Malignant Totems, and only Plague Froth waves destroy them: one line across the lawn (the open ground where they stand), then clean up. Three Burning Venoms die apart, and Exploding Infection ramps until it is dispelled.</p><h3>What wipes us</h3><ul><li>Imbibe from the wrong spot: The boss is tanked toward the Blood fountain: Imbibe takes Shadow and Blood instead of Shadow and Flame.</li><li>Two fire adds die together: Two Burning Venoms die at once: two Caustic Surges hit the raid together.</li><li>A wave through the melee: A Froth target stands next to the boss instead of on their marker: their wave runs through the stacked melee.</li><li>Two totems left standing: Nobody goes to the last two totems: at the next Imbibe both cast Malignance on the raid, and two is a wipe.</li></ul><h3>Your job</h3><ul><li>Hold him by the Shadow fountain for every Imbibe.</li><li>Swap on every Dripping Fangs, with a defensive for the bite.</li><li>After the venoms walk him down to the lawn; back to the Shadow fountain before every Imbibe.</li><li>Five healers: the infusions stack to five. Raid cooldowns on every Imbibe from the second, the fifth biggest.</li><li>Exploding Infection: dispel in pairs, one pair at a time; never right before an Imbibe.</li><li>Stygian Infection: heal the absorb off while the player walks the edge.</li><li>Death Knights: grip the Burning Venoms in. Shrouded Venoms: Gorefiend's Grasp (Death Knight), Typhoon (Druid) or Sigil of Chains (Demon Hunter).</li><li>Kill Burning Venoms 9 to 10 seconds apart. Biles: melee take the ones by the boss, ranged the far ones.</li><li>Mythic Froth: take your marker on the first set; line up a totem on the second.</li></ul><h2>The loop: Imbibe to Imbibe</h2><p>One phase on repeat: Imbibe, the venoms, biles and infections twice over, and two Plague Froths to clear the totems, then the next Imbibe. Each time the hits are bigger and the adds healthier.</p><h3>Imbibe (Always Shadow and Flame)</h3><p>At full energy, about every 84 seconds, he drains the two fountains nearest him.</p><p>At full energy, about every 84 seconds, he drains the two fountains nearest him. Each one gives a raid hit, its own adds and infection, and an infusion that makes that fountain's next hit and adds stronger. On Mythic about two dozen totems rise.</p><ul><li>The boss tank holds him by the Shadow fountain, every Imbibe.</li><li>He drains Shadow and Flame: two raid hits, bigger each time.</li><li>Shadow and Flame adds come out; never touch Blood.</li><li>About two dozen totems rise: the Froth waves must hit them all.</li><li>Same spot every time means little movement and no Clotting Venoms (the big slow Blood adds). Killing it in time gets easy; healing gets hard, so bring five healers.</li><li>Every Imbibe hits harder: raid cooldowns on each one from the second on. The fifth is the biggest, about double the fourth.</li></ul><h3>The venoms (Grip them in, kill the fire adds apart)</h3><p>Each Imbibe sends venoms to the cavity.</p><p>Each Imbibe sends venoms to the cavity. Shrouded Venoms (Shadow) have a shield as big as their health and leave swirls when they die. Burning Venoms (Flame) hurt the raid while alive and when they die. Any that reach the cavity hit the raid hard.</p><ul><li>Shrouded Venoms (eight): gather them on the boss and cleave.</li><li>Burning Venoms (three): one Death Knight grips one to the boss, the other walks out and grips two to the middle.</li><li>A Warlock banishes the middle one; slow or trap the far one.</li><li>Kill the Burning Venoms 9 to 10 seconds apart.</li><li>Two Death Knights is ideal, any spec: gripping means pulling an add to you with Death Grip. Gorefiend's Grasp works every other set; Typhoon (Druid) or Sigil of Chains (Demon Hunter) work every time.</li><li>At four or five infusion stacks the adds get very healthy: crowd control the far fire add for good.</li><li>Anything alive for 60 seconds hardens: immune to crowd control and faster.</li><li>Short of Death Knights or a Warlock? Ranged swap to the fire adds and keep them crowd controlled so they never reach the middle.</li></ul><h3>Plague Froth, first set (Mow the lawn)</h3><p>Five players are marked; 6 seconds later each fires big waves north, south, east and west.</p><p>Five players are marked; 6 seconds later each fires big waves north, south, east and west. The waves are the only thing that destroys the totems.</p><ul><li>Ranged targets stand on the X, triangle and moon raid markers; melee targets on the diamond and star.</li><li>The boss sits between the diamond and the star, melee stacked under him.</li><li>Stand on your marker: the markers are on a slanted line, so no wave runs through another target.</li><li>The waves mow down most of the totems.</li><li>Damage doesn't matter during the Froths: the totems do.</li><li>If more melee get picked than there are melee markers, the ranged slide outward along the line.</li><li>Totems turn white when a wave hits them.</li><li>The lawn is the open ground between the Flame and Shadow fountains where most totems stand: the waves &quot;mow&quot; it.</li></ul><h3>Plague Froth, second set (Clean up the last totems)</h3><p>Any totem still up at the next Imbibe casts Malignance: a raid hit and 1 minute of damage over time.</p><p>Any totem still up at the next Imbibe casts Malignance: a raid hit and 1 minute of damage over time. One is survivable; two or more wipe the raid.</p><ul><li>Call where totems are left: &quot;two left, one to the entrance&quot;.</li><li>Each target walks to a totem and lines a wave up on it.</li><li>Totems first; not hitting melee second.</li><li>Two targets favor the left side and two the right; the fifth stays central. A mobile melee target runs out for a far totem.</li><li>Then move back to the Shadow fountain: Imbibe comes right after.</li></ul><h3>Malignant Catalyst (Someone in every bile)</h3><p>A raid hit, then bile circles land around the room 7 seconds later.</p><p>A raid hit, then bile circles land around the room 7 seconds later. Each one is split between the players in it; one that lands on nobody hits the whole raid.</p><ul><li>Raid hit: biles are coming.</li><li>Melee take the biles by the boss; ranged and a healer take the far ones.</li><li>Five biles on Mythic, five soakers.</li></ul><p>Plan: They come twice per Imbibe, about 11 and 50 seconds after it.</p><h3>Infections (Walk out, dispel a few at a time)</h3><p>Twice per Imbibe (19 and 71 seconds after it), four players get Exploding Infection (Flame) and four Stygian (Shadow).</p><p>Twice per Imbibe (19 and 71 seconds after it), four players get Exploding Infection (Flame) and four Stygian (Shadow). Removing Exploding blasts the raid, less the farther away, and ramps until dispelled. Stygian is a big heal absorb, and you drop circles as you move.</p><ul><li>Exploding Infection (four players): walk out from the raid.</li><li>Two healers dispel two of them, then two more a few seconds later: a pair at a time.</li><li>Stygian Infection (four players): keep walking at the edge, leaving your circles away from everyone.</li><li>Healers heal the absorb off.</li><li>The ramp is slow, so there is no rush: players can take close to ten stacks. If you are dispelled last, use a personal.</li><li>Never dispel right before an Imbibe: the blast lands on top of the Imbibe hit. The second set comes about 13 seconds before the next Imbibe, so start it early or hold it.</li><li>Stygian: do not run so far out that you leave healing range.</li></ul><h3>Dripping Fangs (Swap on every one)</h3><p>About every 28 seconds he bites his tank: much more physical damage taken and stacking damage over time.</p><ul><li>The boss tank uses a defensive for the bite.</li><li>Dripping Fangs lands: much more physical damage taken.</li><li>The other tank taunts right away, every time.</li></ul><p>Five stacks of both infusions: the biggest raid hit of the fight, about double the one before, and the last Imbibe of a normal kill. Rallying Cry, Spirit Link, Aura Mastery, Anti-Magic Zone and personals on it.</p><p>In the last set, crowd control the Burning Venoms instead of killing them, ignore the totems (just don't hit melee), and burn Vashnik before Imbibe 6. The set is under a minute, so the crowd control holds until he dies.</p><p>Constant raid damage that climbs with every Imbibe, so the longer the fight, the harder the healing. Burn him before it piles up.</p><p>In our kill a tick was about four times as strong in minute seven as in minute one.</p><h2>Putting it all together</h2><p>The whole fight in the order of a real kill. Quiet stretches are cut, and a loop that repeats plays once; the time shown is the real fight clock. Pick a moment to jump there.</p><p>The whole fight in order, timed by a real kill (6:51). Coloured lines are moments when two things happen at once.</p><ul><li>Every Imbibe from the same spot by the Shadow fountain: Shadow and Flame, never Blood.</li><li>Burning Venoms die 9 to 10 seconds apart.</li><li>Dripping Fangs about every 28 seconds: the tanks swap every time.</li><li>Biles and infections come twice per Imbibe; the second infections land about 13 seconds before the next Imbibe.</li><li>Every totem hit before the next Imbibe. One left is survivable, two wipe.</li><li>Five healers: the infusions stack to five, so the healing check is the hard part.</li></ul><h3>0:00–6:51</h3><p>Bloodlust on the pull. Imbibe comes early: the tank takes him straight to his spot by the Shadow fountain.</p><p>The boss tank uses a defensive for the bite. Dripping Fangs lands: much more physical damage taken.</p><p>Plague Froth comes at about 13 seconds, before the first Imbibe and before any totems: five players are marked.</p><p>Raid hit: biles are coming. Melee take the biles by the boss; ranged and a healer take the far ones.</p><p>One more raid hit coming. The raid hit lands.</p><p>Down before the sixth Imbibe.</p><h2>Your job, by role</h2><p>Every line for each role. The Your job box at the top shows the first three.</p>"},"sszorak/heroic":{"boss":"Sszorak","d":"h","title":"Sszorak Heroic guide: mechanics, positions and every job · Debrief","desc":"Sszorak Heroic guide: One boss on a loop: the tank combo (Apex Predator), Venomous Surge and Raging Crosswinds, twice, then Dig In and its three winds.…","html":"<h1>Sszorak Heroic guide</h1><p>One boss on a loop: the tank combo (Apex Predator), Venomous Surge and Raging Crosswinds, twice, then Dig In and its three winds. The first combo before each Dig In at the entrance side, the second at the exit side, the winds and Dig In in the middle. Tornadoes kill more raids than anything else here.</p><h3>What wipes us</h3><ul><li>A Mutilate with too few in it: Most of the second group drifts off and only four take the Mutilate: it needs at least five, and split four ways it kills.</li><li>One more hit in a tornado: A melee keeps hitting the boss as a tornado comes through: one touch and they're dead.</li><li>A cyst dropped in the raid: A Surge player drops their cyst in the raid: someone walks into it, it pops early, and it's missing for the winds.</li><li>Off the stack when the wind blows: A player isn't in the stack when a wind comes: nothing stops them and they're blown off the platform.</li></ul><h3>Your job</h3><ul><li>Ravage alone, aimed at the edge; taunt after the first Ravage or Mutilate.</li><li>Pull deep to the entrance side; the second combo at the exit side.</li><li>Move the boss off the Caustic Claws puddles. Swap around 6 Corroding Venom stacks.</li><li>Two soak groups take turns on Mutilate: heal the group that just soaked.</li><li>Heal the Surge players on their way out, and the raid after each Surge.</li><li>The cyst pops in Dig In are the hardest healing: save cooldowns you can cast while moving.</li><li>Tornadoes: step off at once, even mid-cast. Dispel the tornado's damage over time at once.</li><li>Mutilate with your group, never the next one.</li><li>Crosswinds: collide with a partner, or cancel and tell them.</li></ul><h2>The loop: two tank combos, then Dig In</h2><p>Apex Predator (the tank combo), Surge and Crosswinds at the entrance side, the same again at the exit side, then Dig In in the middle. Rinse and repeat three times.</p><h3>Apex Predator (Soaks take turns, Ravage at the edge)</h3><p>The tank combo: five casts on the tank in random order (Tempest never first or last).</p><p>The tank combo: five casts on the tank in random order (Tempest never first or last). Two Ravage: a huge hit, and the next hurts more. Two Mutilate: a frontal soak shared by those in it; the next hits them far harder. One Tempest: tornadoes.</p><ul><li>Mutilate: one of the two soak groups stands in it with the tank. 5 or more.</li><li>Ravage: the tank takes it alone, aimed at the edge.</li><li>The other tank taunts after the first Ravage or Mutilate.</li><li>The other group takes the next Mutilate.</li><li>Tempest: tornadoes. Step clear.</li><li>Two soak groups, left and right, about nine each. They never take two Mutilates in a row: the second hit on the same players is far harder.</li><li>At least five must be in each Mutilate or it kills: it splits between everyone in it.</li><li>A Blood Death Knight can soak a Mutilate alone with Vampiric Blood and Anti-Magic Shell.</li></ul><h3>Tempest (Step off for the tornadoes)</h3><p>Tornadoes roam out from the boss for about half a minute.</p><p>Tornadoes roam out from the boss for about half a minute. They kill anyone but a tank, sometimes in one touch. The number one killer on this boss.</p><ul><li>Call the tornadoes as they pass through the boss.</li><li>Melee: step off, come back once it has passed.</li><li>Never stay in for one more hit: killing him in time only gets easier.</li><li>Leave the melee a big pocket round the boss, so they have room to dodge.</li><li>A tornado touch leaves damage over time: dispel it at once (a Shaman's Poison Cleansing Totem works).</li></ul><h3>Venomous Surge (Cysts to the markers)</h3><p>Two players get 10 seconds of damage over time.</p><p>Two players get 10 seconds of damage over time. When it ends the raid takes a hit and a Viscous Cyst drops there. A cyst lasts 2 minutes; touching it pops it: a knockback and a slow. Dig In needs one per wind.</p><ul><li>Read the tunnels: one mote blows first, three motes last. Put a raid marker across from each one.</li><li>Surge on you: walk to your marker. The cyst drops where you stand when the damage over time ends.</li><li>The tank pulls the boss off the Caustic Claws puddles: standing in one is +30% damage.</li><li>Healers: the damage over time hurts, so heal them on their way out. The raid takes a hit when it ends.</li><li>Four cysts for three winds: the fourth is a spare. One misplaced is recoverable, two is a wipe.</li><li>The tank keeps the boss away from the markers, so nobody pops a cyst early.</li></ul><h3>Raging Crosswinds (Collide with a partner)</h3><p>Marked players get an arrow and are launched that way about 8 seconds later.</p><p>Marked players get an arrow and are launched that way about 8 seconds later. Two players whose arrows meet collide in the air and drop safely.</p><ul><li>Arrow on you: find a partner whose arrow points at you.</li><li>Head on: you collide and drop safely.</li><li>Can cancel your own launch (an immunity or a movement ability)? Tell your partner first, or they fly alone.</li></ul><p>Plan: Priests, Paladins and Shamans have no reliable cancel: call it out so a partner lines up head on with you.</p><p>His hits stack more physical damage taken on the tank (3% a stack). Swap at about 6 stacks; it lines up with the combo swaps. A Blood Death Knight can hold more.</p><h2>Dig In: the winds</h2><p>At full energy, about every 2 minutes, he digs in for 25 seconds and takes 30% more damage. Three winds blow from the tunnels; each pushes the raid into a cyst. Then the exit cyst throws everyone back to the entrance.</p><h3>Dig In (Three winds, three cysts, then the exit)</h3><p>For 25 seconds he takes 30% more damage while three winds blow from the tunnels, in the order the motes show (one mote first, three last).</p><p>For 25 seconds he takes 30% more damage while three winds blow from the tunnels, in the order the motes show (one mote first, three last). Each wind pushes everyone into the cyst across from its tunnel, which pops and knocks them back.</p><ul><li>Stack in the middle.</li><li>Each wind pushes the stack into the cyst across from its tunnel. It pops: you're back in the middle.</li><li>Re-centre before the next wind.</li><li>After the third wind, a mobile player pops the exit cyst.</li><li>Everyone flies to the entrance side: the next combo starts there.</li><li>Some raids name one popper per wind; otherwise the first player pushed into a cyst pops it for everyone.</li><li>The fourth cyst is a spare for a misplaced one. If it is not needed, pop it at the exit to clear the board.</li><li>The cyst pops are the hardest healing of the fight: save healing cooldowns you can cast while moving.</li></ul><h2>Putting it all together</h2><p>The whole fight in the order of a real kill. Quiet stretches are cut, and a loop that repeats plays once; the time shown is the real fight clock. Pick a moment to jump there.</p><p>The whole fight in order, timed by a real kill (6:44). Coloured lines are moments when two things happen at once.</p><ul><li>Tornadoes: step off at once, even mid-cast. Call them when they pass through the boss.</li><li>Each Mutilate on one group, the next on the other.</li><li>Tempest comes once in every Apex Predator, in a random place in the order (never first or last).</li><li>Every loop is rinse and repeat: entrance side, exit side, then Dig In.</li></ul><h3>0:00–0:05</h3><p>Pull him deep, to the entrance side of the platform: the puddles that follow the tank combo stay out of the middle.</p><h3>The loop: two tank combos, then Dig In0:05–0:21</h3><h3>0:21–0:57</h3><h3>The loop: two tank combos, then Dig In0:57–1:05</h3><h3>1:05–1:51</h3><p>Arrow on you: find a partner whose arrow points at you. Head on: you collide and drop safely.</p><h3>Dig In: the winds1:51–2:23</h3><p>Stack in the middle: three winds, three cysts, then the exit.</p><h3>2:23–4:09</h3><h3>4:09–4:41</h3><h3>4:41–6:27</h3><h3>6:27–6:44</h3><p>Stack in the middle: three winds, three cysts. Each wind pushes the stack into the cyst across from its tunnel.</p><p>Sszorak is down in the third Dig In. No exit cyst is needed: the fight is over.</p><h2>Your job, by role</h2><p>Every line for each role. The Your job box at the top shows the first three.</p>"},"sszorak/mythic":{"boss":"Sszorak","d":"m","title":"Sszorak Mythic guide: mechanics, positions and every job · Debrief","desc":"Sszorak Mythic guide: One boss on a loop: the tank combo (Apex Predator), Venomous Surge and Raging Crosswinds, twice, then Dig In and its three winds. On…","html":"<h1>Sszorak Mythic guide</h1><p>One boss on a loop: the tank combo (Apex Predator), Venomous Surge and Raging Crosswinds, twice, then Dig In and its three winds. On Mythic his rage climbs all fight and a wipe comes at 100: the farthest player is marked, and stacking 14 players on them right after each tank combo resets it. Everyone in the stack gets Virulence and spreads. Crosswinds has four directions. Tornadoes kill more raids than anything else here.</p><h3>What wipes us</h3><ul><li>A Mutilate with too few in it: Most of the second group drifts off and only four take the Mutilate: it needs at least five, and split four ways it kills.</li><li>One more hit in a tornado: A melee keeps hitting the boss as a tornado comes through: one touch and they're dead.</li><li>Too few in the Fury stack: Only 12 players stack on the marked player instead of 14: he never charges, his rage hits 100 and the raid dies.</li><li>Off the stack when the wind blows: A player isn't in the stack when a wind comes: nothing stops them and they're blown off the platform.</li></ul><h3>Your job</h3><ul><li>Ravage alone, aimed at the edge; taunt after the first Ravage or Mutilate.</li><li>Pull deep to the entrance side; the second combo at the exit side.</li><li>After each Fury pop and Surge, move the boss off the puddles. Swap around 6 Corroding Venom stacks.</li><li>Mobile healers join the Fury stack.</li><li>Two soak groups take turns on Mutilate: heal the group that just soaked.</li><li>The cyst pops in Dig In are the hardest healing: save cooldowns you can cast while moving.</li><li>Tornadoes: step off at once, even mid-cast. Dispel the tornado's damage over time at once.</li><li>Fury: into the stack right after the combo, then to your Virulence spot.</li><li>Mutilate with your group, never the next one. Crosswinds: collide with a partner.</li></ul><h2>The loop: two tank combos, then Dig In</h2><p>Apex Predator (the tank combo), Surge and Crosswinds at the entrance side, the same again at the exit side, then Dig In in the middle, three times over. Serpent's Fury goes off right after every tank combo.</p><h3>Apex Predator (Soaks take turns, Ravage at the edge)</h3><p>The tank combo: five casts on the tank in random order (Tempest never first or last).</p><p>The tank combo: five casts on the tank in random order (Tempest never first or last). Two Ravage: a huge hit, and the next hurts more. Two Mutilate: a frontal soak shared by those in it; the next hits them far harder. One Tempest: tornadoes.</p><ul><li>Mutilate: one of the two soak groups stands in it with the tank. 5 or more.</li><li>Ravage: the tank takes it alone, aimed at the edge.</li><li>The other tank taunts after the first Ravage or Mutilate.</li><li>The other group takes the next Mutilate.</li><li>Tempest: tornadoes. Step clear.</li><li>Two soak groups, left and right, about nine each; they never take two in a row. At least five must be in a Mutilate or it kills.</li><li>Corroding Venom stacks on the tank: swap around 6 stacks (it lines up with the combo swaps). A Blood Death Knight can tank most of the fight.</li></ul><h3>Tempest (Step off for the tornadoes)</h3><p>Tornadoes roam out from the boss for about half a minute.</p><p>Tornadoes roam out from the boss for about half a minute. They kill anyone but a tank, sometimes in one touch. The number one killer on this boss.</p><ul><li>Call the tornadoes as they pass through the boss.</li><li>Melee: step off, come back once it has passed.</li><li>Never stay in for one more hit: killing him in time only gets easier.</li><li>Leave the melee a big pocket round the boss, so they have room to dodge.</li><li>A tornado touch leaves damage over time: dispel it at once (a Shaman's Poison Cleansing Totem works).</li></ul><h3>Serpent's Fury (14 in the circle, then spread)</h3><p>The farthest player from the boss is marked while his rage climbs; at 100 the raid dies.</p><p>The farthest player from the boss is marked while his rage climbs; at 100 the raid dies. When 14 players stack within 8 yards of the mark, he charges them and his rage resets. The stack gets Virulence: lines 5 seconds later pass it on.</p><ul><li>Right after each tank combo, the raid stacks near the boss and the marked player walks in.</li><li>Your Fury player, a mobile Hunter or Mage, is the farthest from the boss: they walk into the waiting stack about 1 second before Venomous Surge goes off.</li><li>14 in the circle: he charges, rage resets.</li><li>Spread to your Virulence spot, half outside and half inside. The Fury player goes to the farthest one, so they get the mark again.</li><li>Soak with all DPS, the other tank and the mobile healers. Keep the stack close to melee range, or it can fail to count.</li><li>The first combo before each Dig In (entrance side): pop it the moment the last tank cast lands. If Ravage is last, pre-stack.</li><li>The second combo before each Dig In (exit side): wait until about 1 second before Venomous Surge. Popped even 2 seconds early, the first pop after Dig In becomes impossible.</li><li>In Dig In the Fury can't pop: the marked player waits for Dig In to start, stacks with everyone, and steps out after the third knock-back so it doesn't pop as Dig In ends.</li><li>Virulence lines burst after 5 seconds and pass it to anyone they hit, with longer lines. Spread at once: it leaves puddles (+30% damage taken), so a personal helps every other set.</li></ul><h3>Venomous Surge (Cysts to the markers)</h3><p>Two players get 10 seconds of damage over time.</p><p>Two players get 10 seconds of damage over time. When it ends the raid takes a hit and a Viscous Cyst drops there. A cyst lasts 2 minutes; touching it pops it: a knockback and a slow. Dig In needs one per wind.</p><ul><li>Read the tunnels: one mote blows first, three motes last. Put a raid marker across from each, and the fourth at the exit.</li><li>Surge on you: walk to your marker. The cyst drops where you stand when the damage over time ends.</li><li>The tank pulls the boss off the Caustic Claws puddles: standing in one is +30% damage.</li><li>Healers: the damage over time hurts, so heal them on their way out. The raid takes a hit when it ends.</li><li>Northern Sky Raid Tools' wind helper: the raid leader clicks the marker across from each wind and everyone sees their drop spot (the group must be 20 or fewer).</li><li>The fourth cyst is always at the exit marker: a mobile player pops it after the third wind to throw the raid back to the entrance.</li></ul><h3>Raging Crosswinds (Collide with a partner)</h3><p>Marked players get an arrow and are launched that way about 8 seconds later.</p><p>Marked players get an arrow and are launched that way about 8 seconds later. Two players whose arrows meet collide in the air and drop safely.</p><ul><li>Arrow on you: find a partner whose arrow points at you.</li><li>Head on, or at right angles: you collide and drop safely.</li><li>Can cancel your own launch (an immunity or a movement ability)? Tell your partner first, or they fly alone.</li><li>Priests, Paladins and Shamans usually can't cancel: call it out (or ping yourself) so a partner lines up head on with you. A Dracthyr Priest or an Evoker's Rescue helps.</li><li>Don't stand too close to a flying player's path.</li></ul><p>His hits stack more physical damage taken on the tank (3% a stack). Swap at about 6 stacks; it lines up with the combo swaps. A Blood Death Knight can hold more.</p><p>The enrage comes right after the third Dig In, at about 6:20. Burn him inside that Dig In and don't pop the exit cyst. People can taunt a Mutilate each and die to buy about 20 seconds.</p><h2>Dig In: the winds</h2><p>At full energy, about every 2 minutes, he digs in for 25 seconds and takes 30% more damage. Three winds blow from the tunnels; each pushes the raid into a cyst. Then the exit cyst throws everyone back to the entrance.</p><h3>Dig In (Three winds, three cysts, then the exit)</h3><p>For 25 seconds he takes 30% more damage while three winds blow from the tunnels, in the order the motes show (one mote first, three last).</p><p>For 25 seconds he takes 30% more damage while three winds blow from the tunnels, in the order the motes show (one mote first, three last). Each wind pushes everyone into the cyst across from its tunnel, which pops and knocks them back.</p><ul><li>Stack in the middle.</li><li>Each wind pushes the stack into the cyst across from its tunnel. It pops: you're back in the middle.</li><li>Re-centre before the next wind.</li><li>After the third wind, a mobile player pops the exit cyst.</li><li>Everyone flies to the entrance side: the next combo starts there.</li><li>Cooldowns that come back every 2 minutes: open Dig In with one press and keep the rest for the back half, rather than lose a whole use to fit the 30%.</li><li>Evokers can cast Time Spiral (a free movement ability for everyone) to help with the knockbacks: nice, not needed.</li><li>The Fury player waits for Dig In to start, stacks with everyone, and steps out after the third knock-back.</li><li>Some raids name one popper per wind; otherwise the first player pushed into a cyst pops it for everyone.</li><li>After the last Dig In, don't pop the exit cyst: the enrage follows.</li></ul><h2>Putting it all together</h2><p>The whole fight in the order of a real kill. Quiet stretches are cut, and a loop that repeats plays once; the time shown is the real fight clock. Pick a moment to jump there.</p><p>The whole fight in order, timed by a real kill (5:55). Coloured lines are moments when two things happen at once.</p><ul><li>Tornadoes: step off at once, even mid-cast. Call them when they pass through the boss.</li><li>Each Mutilate on one group, the next on the other.</li><li>Tempest comes once in every Apex Predator, in a random place in the order (never first or last).</li><li>Serpent's Fury: 14 in the circle right after every tank combo, or the raid dies at 100 rage.</li><li>Every loop is rinse and repeat: entrance side, exit side, then Dig In.</li></ul><h3>0:00–0:05</h3><p>Pull him deep, to the entrance side of the platform: the puddles that follow the tank combo stay out of the middle.</p><h3>The loop: two tank combos, then Dig In0:05–0:21</h3><h3>0:21–0:52</h3><h3>The loop: two tank combos, then Dig In0:52–1:07</h3><h3>1:07–1:40</h3><h3>Dig In: the winds1:40–2:09</h3><p>Stack in the middle: three winds, three cysts, then the exit.</p><h3>2:09–3:47</h3><h3>3:47–4:16</h3><h3>4:16–5:55</h3><p>Third Dig In: burn him down and don't pop the exit cyst: the enrage follows.</p><h2>Your job, by role</h2><p>Every line for each role. The Your job box at the top shows the first three.</p>"},"the-twin-fangs/heroic":{"boss":"The Twin Fangs","d":"h","title":"The Twin Fangs Heroic guide: mechanics, positions and every job · Debrief","desc":"The Twin Fangs Heroic guide: Two bosses, three platforms, two sets on each, then a Submerge to the next. Everything adds venom stacks and too many kill…","html":"<h1>The Twin Fangs Heroic guide</h1><p>Two bosses, three platforms, two sets on each, then a Submerge to the next. Everything adds venom stacks and too many kill you: soak globules on pace, kill the Spawns before they Spit twice, dodge the waves and beams. Kill both together.</p><h3>What wipes us</h3><ul><li>A globule left to explode: Nobody soaks one Caustic Globule: 10 seconds after it lands it explodes on the whole raid.</li><li>A second Corrosive Spit: The Spawns live too long and Spit a second time: another venom stack for each target.</li><li>Too few in the third hit: Only three players stand in the third Ravenous Feast hit: split between too few, it kills them.</li><li>Caught by the beam: A player stays where the Vile Flood beam starts: it turns straight over them, more venom stacks.</li></ul><h3>Your job</h3><ul><li>Caustic Deluge shoves Vexhul's tank back: stay in range.</li><li>Stone Breaker: step into each slam in order; swap after each set.</li><li>Stir the Depths: look back and call where each set of waves comes from.</li><li>Tank hits on the beams and slams: externals.</li><li>Coiling Ichor ramps: heal the last ticks.</li><li>Soak globules too.</li><li>Swap to the Spawns at once: never a second Spit.</li><li>Feast: your group's hit only.</li><li>Soak a globule when your stacks are low.</li></ul><h2>A platform: two sets</h2><p>Each set: both beams and the globules, Stone Breaker, the Spawns, Coiling Ichor, Stir the Depths, then Ravenous Feast. Two sets, then Submerge.</p><h3>Caustic Deluge (Soak the globules)</h3><p>Vexhul's beam hits his tank and shoves them back; acid splashes drop Caustic Globules that explode on the raid 10 seconds later unless someone soaks them, which costs that player a venom stack.</p><ul><li>Vexhul casts Caustic Deluge.</li><li>His tank is shoved back: stay in range.</li><li>Globules land all over the platform.</li><li>Low-stack players soak one each.</li></ul><h3>Stone Breaker (The tank takes each slam in order)</h3><p>Ithraz shoves his tank back, then slams three marked spots in the order they appeared.</p><p>Ithraz shoves his tank back, then slams three marked spots in the order they appeared. A slam with nobody in it hits the raid and knocks everyone back.</p><ul><li>Ithraz's tank is shoved back.</li><li>Three spots light up: nobody else near them.</li><li>The tank steps into each, in order.</li></ul><p>Plan: Each slam stacks a vulnerability on the tank: swap after each set.</p><h3>Venomous Emergence (Kill the Spawns before the second Spit)</h3><p>Three Spawn of Vexhul rise at the pool's edge (a venom stack for everyone).</p><p>Three Spawn of Vexhul rise at the pool's edge (a venom stack for everyone). Each fires Corrosive Spit beams at players, another stack each.</p><ul><li>Three Spawns rise at the pool's edge: a stack for everyone.</li><li>Almost everyone swaps to the Spawns.</li><li>Spit on you: move once, then stand still.</li><li>Kill all three before their second Spit.</li></ul><h3>Coiling Ichor (Puddles packed at the edge)</h3><p>Damage over time that ramps up as its circle shrinks.</p><p>Damage over time that ramps up as its circle shrinks. When it ends a Congealed Gore puddle drops, stays, and slows anyone in it.</p><ul><li>Coiling Ichor goes out: a shrinking circle on each target.</li><li>Targets walk to their spots on the outer edge.</li><li>Use a defensive that still covers the last ticks.</li><li>The puddles drop there, packed tight together.</li><li>Keep the middle clear: the raid needs it for the Spawns, the soaks and the end of the fight.</li><li>Coiling Ichor is the most likely thing to kill a single player late in the fight.</li></ul><h3>Stir the Depths (Step into the gaps)</h3><p>Raid-wide pulses, and sets of small waves rolling in from behind the raid, each set from its own direction: left, right or middle, in an order that changes.</p><p>Raid-wide pulses, and sets of small waves rolling in from behind the raid, each set from its own direction: left, right or middle, in an order that changes. Each wave that hits you is another venom stack.</p><ul><li>Tanks look back and call where each set comes from: left, right or middle.</li><li>Sets of small waves roll in from behind, one after another.</li><li>Step sideways into a gap between the waves before each set reaches you.</li></ul><h3>Ravenous Feast (Three groups, one hit each)</h3><p>Ithraz smashes a big soak three times: split between everyone in it, one venom stack off each, then a knockback.</p><p>Ithraz smashes a big soak three times: split between everyone in it, one venom stack off each, then a knockback. A player can only take one hit.</p><ul><li>Left group takes the first hit, and is knocked left.</li><li>Right group takes the second, knocked right.</li><li>Center group takes the third, knocked to the front.</li><li>Six or more in each hit.</li></ul><p>Almost everything adds a venom stack, and 10 kills you. Share the globule soaks so nobody climbs too high, and swap tanks every set.</p><p>Keep them close in health and kill them together: the one left alive gets stronger every few seconds.</p><p>At the third Submerge, about 7:30 to 8 minutes in, they enrage. Both must be dead before it. Once Vexhul is dead, stacks stop killing and globules stop exploding.</p><h2>Submerge: to the next platform</h2><p>After two sets both bosses dive. Let Vexhul's turning beam pass, then follow behind it round the walkway to the next corner of the triangle.</p><h3>Submerge (Let the beam pass, then follow it)</h3><p>Both bosses dive.</p><p>Both bosses dive. Vexhul's Vile Flood beam turns round the room from the pool (the orbs circling him show which way), Ithraz's Sanguine Storm drops gore swirls, and venom showers leave puddles that make you take more damage.</p><ul><li>Watch the orbs circling Vexhul: the beam turns the way they spin.</li><li>Stand on the side the beam turns away from.</li><li>Follow behind it, the long way round, to the next platform.</li><li>If you must, step on a puddle rather than into a wave: a puddle only makes you take more damage; a wave is more stacks.</li><li>Mobile players just walk; others can take a Warlock gateway once the waves have passed.</li><li>In our kill and a top team's kill the beam turned clockwise both times, so the raid always went the long way round.</li></ul><h2>Putting it all together</h2><p>The whole fight in the order of a real kill. Quiet stretches are cut, and a loop that repeats plays once; the time shown is the real fight clock. Pick a moment to jump there.</p><p>The whole fight in order, timed by a real kill (6:58). Coloured lines are moments when two things happen at once.</p><ul><li>Eternal Venom: 10 stacks kills. Only low-stack players soak the globules.</li><li>Every set is rinse and repeat: beams and globules, slams, Spawns, Coiling Ichor, waves, Feast.</li><li>Kill both bosses together: the survivor gets stronger every few seconds.</li></ul><h3>0:00–2:30</h3><p>Both tanks under the tip, one boss each; melee between them, ranged and healers behind toward the pool.</p><p>Low-stack players soak one globule each, within 10 seconds of it landing.</p><p>Ithraz's tank steps into each of the three slams, in the order they appeared.</p><p>Tanks call where each set of waves comes from; step into a gap before it reaches you.</p><p>Three hits, one per group: left, then right, then center, six or more in each.</p><h3>Submerge: to the next platform2:30–2:55</h3><h3>2:55–5:19</h3><p>The same two sets on the next corner. Low-stack players soak the globules; 10 stacks kills.</p><h3>5:19–5:44</h3><h3>5:44–6:58</h3><p>The last platform: stacks are high now. Only low-stack players soak; nobody goes to 10.</p><p>Vexhul first, around the last soak (then stacks stop killing and globules stop exploding), Ithraz right after.</p><h2>Your job, by role</h2><p>Every line for each role. The Your job box at the top shows the first three.</p>"},"the-twin-fangs/mythic":{"boss":"The Twin Fangs","d":"m","title":"The Twin Fangs Mythic guide: mechanics, positions and every job · Debrief","desc":"The Twin Fangs Mythic guide: Two bosses, three platforms, two sets on each, then a Submerge. Everything adds venom stacks; 10 kills. Crowd control the…","html":"<h1>The Twin Fangs Mythic guide</h1><p>Two bosses, three platforms, two sets on each, then a Submerge. Everything adds venom stacks; 10 kills. Crowd control the shielded globules, then soak them on pace; kick all 10 Broodlings; soak an orb after each Feast hit. Vexhul to 35% first, then Ithraz.</p><h3>What wipes us</h3><ul><li>A globule left to explode: Nobody soaks one Caustic Globule: 10 seconds after it lands it explodes on the whole raid.</li><li>A second Corrosive Spit: The Spawns live too long and Spit a second time: another venom stack for each target.</li><li>A missed Broodling kick: Nobody kicks one Broodling: its Visceral Burst hits the whole raid with stacking damage over time, and that usually wipes the raid.</li><li>Caught by the beam: A player stays where the Vile Flood beam starts: it turns straight over them, more venom stacks.</li></ul><h3>Your job</h3><ul><li>Both beams at once: your biggest defensives.</li><li>Stone Breaker: step into each slam in order; swap after each set.</li><li>Stir the Depths: look back and call where each set of waves comes from.</li><li>Blood Torrent is the biggest tank damage: externals.</li><li>Coiling Ichor ramps: heal the last ticks.</li><li>Ravenous Feast: each Tainted Blood orb leaves a heal absorb on its soakers: heal it off.</li><li>Swap to the Spawns at once: never a second Spit.</li><li>Kick your Broodling slot.</li><li>Feast: your group's hit only, then its Tainted Blood orb.</li></ul><h2>A platform: two sets</h2><p>Each set: both beams and the globules, Stone Breaker, the Spawns and the Broodlings, Coiling Ichor, Stir the Depths, then Ravenous Feast. Two sets, then Submerge.</p><h3>Caustic Deluge (Crowd control, then soak the globules)</h3><p>Vexhul's beam shoves his tank back while Blood Torrent puts a huge heal absorb on Ithraz's tank: the biggest tank damage of the fight.</p><p>Vexhul's beam shoves his tank back while Blood Torrent puts a huge heal absorb on Ithraz's tank: the biggest tank damage of the fight. Fifteen globules drop, each shielded until it's crowd controlled, then soaked within 10 seconds.</p><ul><li>Both beams at once: Caustic Deluge and Blood Torrent.</li><li>Both tanks: big defensives (Paladins: Bubble, Spellwarding, Bubble).</li><li>Use group crowd control on the globules near you once they land.</li><li>Low-stack players soak one each: 15 a set.</li><li>Spread the group crowd control round the room; single-target crowd control is the backup for the last one.</li><li>Shielded globules knock back anyone trying to soak them.</li></ul><h3>Stone Breaker (The tank takes each slam in order)</h3><p>Ithraz shoves his tank back, then slams three marked spots in the order they appeared.</p><p>Ithraz shoves his tank back, then slams three marked spots in the order they appeared. A slam with nobody in it hits the raid and knocks everyone back.</p><ul><li>Ithraz's tank is shoved back.</li><li>Three spots light up: nobody else near them.</li><li>The tank steps into each, in order.</li></ul><p>Plan: Each slam stacks a vulnerability on the tank: swap after each set.</p><h3>Venomous Emergence (Kill the Spawns before the second Spit)</h3><p>Three Spawn of Vexhul rise at the pool's edge (a venom stack for everyone).</p><p>Three Spawn of Vexhul rise at the pool's edge (a venom stack for everyone). Each fires Corrosive Spit beams at players, another stack each.</p><ul><li>Three Spawns rise at the pool's edge: a stack for everyone.</li><li>Almost everyone swaps to the Spawns.</li><li>Spit on you: move once, then stand still.</li><li>Kill all three before their second Spit.</li></ul><h3>Rouse the Brood (A kick for every Broodling)</h3><p>With the Spawns, 10 Broodlings rise in ten slots round the platform.</p><p>With the Spawns, 10 Broodlings rise in ten slots round the platform. Each casts Visceral Burst: a raid hit and stacking damage over time. Each platform's first set has one per slot; the second is random, so some slots get two and some none.</p><ul><li>Every kicker covers their slot: left 1 to 4, right 1 to 4.</li><li>The Protection Paladins take the two at the tip, and fill in with Divine Toll and Avenger's Shield.</li><li>Spare kickers on each side for double spawns: &quot;back up left 3&quot;.</li><li>A missed kick is usually a wipe.</li><li>Raid markers on the floor show the slots, so every kicker knows where to look.</li><li>Warlocks: watch your pet: one stuck out of range missed a kick and wiped the raid.</li><li>Two Protection Paladins, or a Paladin and a Warrior, make this much easier.</li></ul><h3>Coiling Ichor (Puddles packed at the edge)</h3><p>Damage over time that ramps up as its circle shrinks.</p><p>Damage over time that ramps up as its circle shrinks. When it ends a Congealed Gore puddle drops, stays, and slows anyone in it.</p><ul><li>Coiling Ichor goes out: a shrinking circle on each target.</li><li>Targets walk to their spots on the outer edge.</li><li>Use a defensive that still covers the last ticks.</li><li>The puddles drop there, packed tight together.</li><li>Keep the middle clear: the raid needs it for the Spawns, the soaks and the end of the fight.</li><li>Coiling Ichor is the most likely thing to kill a single player late in the fight.</li></ul><h3>Stir the Depths (Step into the gaps)</h3><p>Raid-wide pulses, and sets of small waves rolling in from behind the raid, each set from its own direction: left, right or middle, in an order that changes.</p><p>Raid-wide pulses, and sets of small waves rolling in from behind the raid, each set from its own direction: left, right or middle, in an order that changes. Each wave that hits you is another venom stack.</p><ul><li>Tanks look back and call where each set comes from: left, right or middle.</li><li>Sets of small waves roll in from behind, one after another.</li><li>Step sideways into a gap between the waves before each set reaches you.</li></ul><h3>Ravenous Feast (Three groups, one hit each)</h3><p>Ithraz smashes a big soak three times: split between everyone in it, one venom stack off each, then a knockback.</p><p>Ithraz smashes a big soak three times: split between everyone in it, one venom stack off each, then a knockback. A player can only take one hit. After each hit a Tainted Blood orb appears: unsoaked, it wipes the raid.</p><ul><li>Left group takes the first hit, then its Tainted Blood orb.</li><li>Right group takes the second hit and the second orb.</li><li>Center group takes the third hit and the last orb.</li><li>Six or more in each.</li><li>Each group soaks its own orb about 2 seconds after its hit, so the orbs go in the same order.</li><li>Mind where each hit throws you: never into a Coiling Ichor puddle.</li><li>Tanks split into the first two groups so they get back to their bosses in time.</li><li>Each orb gives you about 8 seconds to get there.</li><li>An immunity can't soak an orb alone any more (a hotfix): six or more in each.</li></ul><p>Almost everything adds a stack; 10 kills. Aim for about one stack per globule set (skip a globule at 4 on set 3, 5 on set 4, and so on). Never carry more than 8: the Spawns add one, and a Spit on 9 kills.</p><p>Cleave Vexhul down to 35% first (some specs hit harder below that), then swap to Ithraz. On pace on the last platform: Vexhul about 25%, Ithraz about 40%. Kill Vexhul around the last soak, then Ithraz right after.</p><p>At the third Submerge, about 7:30 to 8 minutes in, they enrage. Both must be dead before it. Once Vexhul is dead, stacks stop killing and globules stop exploding.</p><h2>Submerge: to the next platform</h2><p>After two sets both bosses dive. Let Vexhul's turning beam pass, then follow behind it round the walkway to the next corner of the triangle.</p><h3>Submerge (Let the beam pass, then follow it)</h3><p>Both bosses dive.</p><p>Both bosses dive. Vexhul's Vile Flood beam turns round the room from the pool (the orbs circling him show which way), Ithraz's Sanguine Storm drops gore swirls, and venom showers leave puddles that make you take more damage.</p><ul><li>Watch the orbs circling Vexhul: the beam turns the way they spin.</li><li>Stand on the side the beam turns away from.</li><li>Follow behind it, the long way round, to the next platform.</li><li>If you must, step on a puddle rather than into a wave: a puddle only makes you take more damage; a wave is more stacks.</li><li>On Mythic the beam turns further: you can't cheat it any more.</li><li>Mobile players just walk; others can take a Warlock gateway once the waves have passed.</li><li>In our kill and a top team's kill the beam turned clockwise both times, so the raid always went the long way round.</li><li>Movement speed helps a lot, and so does an Evoker's Time Spiral (everyone can use their movement ability once more, even on cooldown).</li></ul><h2>Putting it all together</h2><p>The whole fight in the order of a real kill. Quiet stretches are cut, and a loop that repeats plays once; the time shown is the real fight clock. Pick a moment to jump there.</p><p>The whole fight in order, timed by a real kill (6:43). Coloured lines are moments when two things happen at once.</p><ul><li>Eternal Venom: 10 stacks kills. About one stack per globule set: about 3 by platform 2 and 6 by platform 3, and never more than 8.</li><li>Every set is rinse and repeat: beams and globules, slams, Spawns and Broodlings, Coiling Ichor, waves, Feast.</li><li>Vexhul first, around the last soak, then Ithraz right after: the survivor gets stronger every few seconds.</li></ul><h3>0:00–2:16</h3><p>Both tanks under the tip, one boss each; melee between them, ranged and healers behind toward the pool.</p><p>Both tanks use big defensives; crowd control the shielded globules, then low-stack players soak one each.</p><p>Ithraz's tank steps into each of the three slams, in the order they appeared.</p><p>Three hits, one per group: left, then right, then center, and each group then soaks its Tainted Blood orb.</p><h3>Submerge: to the next platform2:16–2:40</h3><h3>2:40–4:51</h3><p>First globule set here: about 3 venom stacks. 3 or fewer soak; 4 or more wait.</p><h3>4:51–5:15</h3><h3>5:15–6:43</h3><p>First set here: about 6 stacks. Never above 8: the Spawns add one, and a Spit on 9 kills.</p><p>Vexhul first, around the last soak (then stacks stop killing and globules stop exploding), Ithraz right after.</p><h2>Your job, by role</h2><p>Every line for each role. The Your job box at the top shows the first three.</p><ul><li>Protection Paladins: the Broodlings at the tip, then fill in.</li><li>Ravenous Feast: one of you in the right group, one in the left, then straight back to your boss.</li><li>Skip a globule at 4 stacks on set 3, 5 on set 4, 6 on set 5, 7 on set 6. Never carry more than 8.</li></ul>"},"the-coiled-altar/heroic":{"boss":"The Coiled Altar","d":"h","title":"The Coiled Altar Heroic guide: mechanics, positions and every job · Debrief","desc":"The Coiled Altar Heroic guide: Zul'jan first, then Hex Lord Malacrass, then both at once. Orbs go to the front for the frontal, axes get soaked by five,…","html":"<h1>The Coiled Altar Heroic guide</h1><p>Zul'jan first, then Hex Lord Malacrass, then both at once. Orbs go to the front for the frontal, axes get soaked by five, and ghosts get led into the frontal too.</p><h3>What wipes us</h3><ul><li>Too many orbs in one frontal: Nine orbs in one Sever: the raid takes nine hits at once and the healers can't keep up.</li><li>A ghost reaches its player: If it reaches them, they're mind controlled again.</li><li>A fragment gets through: Each one that gets through heals him about 10%.</li><li>Only 3 in the soak: With 3, it hits the whole raid instead (Execution).</li></ul><h3>Your job</h3><ul><li>Swap on every frontal: the other tank taunts just before Sever or Soul Sever.</li><li>Aim Sever through the orb pile and Soul Sever through the ghosts.</li><li>P3: Phase 3: Corrupted Toxin also forces swaps.</li><li>Dispel Venomfang fast (Poison Cleansing Totem does it on its own).</li><li>Each popped orb hits the raid: be ready when the frontal lands.</li><li>P2: Going into phase 2 the orb stacks are high: stack raid cooldowns there.</li><li>Carry orbs to the pile in front of the boss (5 seconds each).</li><li>Soak the axe when it's your group's turn, then run 40 yards.</li><li>Break Dreadmarch shields and the Eternal Nightfall shield fast.</li></ul><h2>Phase 1: Zul'jan</h2><p>One boss. Gather the orbs in front of him, soak the axes, and dodge the axes rolling around the room.</p><h3>Coalesced Venom (Orb Pops)</h3><p>What it does Circles land and leave green orbs.</p><ul><li>Everyone: Step out of the green circles before they land.</li><li>Nearest player: Walk over the orb to pick it up. Carrying it hurts players within 5 yards, so keep clear of them.</li><li>Carriers: Walk it to the pile in front of the boss. It drops after 5 seconds. Then step to the side, out of the frontal.</li><li>Off-tank: Taunt right before Sever, so the boss faces the pile.</li><li>Everyone: One Sever should pop 5 or 6 orbs. Never more than 7.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: Circles land and leave green orbs. The orbs hurt the raid until Sever, the tank frontal, pops them. Every pop hits the whole raid.</p><h3>Guillotine (Soak)</h3><p>What it does An axe drops on one player.</p><ul><li>Axe target: Walk to a corner, away from the raid, as soon as the axe marks you.</li><li>Soak group: Walk in with them. At least 5 must be inside. On Heroic you can send the whole raid.</li><li>Everyone in it: Press a defensive as it lands, then walk out 40 yards. The spot erupts 6 seconds later.</li><li>Everyone: Gate into a soak, never out of one. Orbs spawn on the path.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: An axe drops on one player. At least 5 must share its 9-yard circle, or it hits the whole raid. 6 seconds after it lands, the spot erupts 40 yards out.</p><h3>Axegrinder (Rolling Axes)</h3><p>What it does Zul'jan throws 5 axes out from himself.</p><ul><li>Melee: Watch for his Axegrinder cast. The axes fly out from him and knock you back, so don't stand with a pool right behind you.</li><li>Everyone: The axes now roll around the room for 3 minutes, often through the middle. Keep an eye on them.</li><li>Everyone: The dashed line shows where an axe is heading. Step aside early, a few yards, not when it's on you.</li><li>Everyone: A touch only hurts. One won't kill you, but they add up. If you must pick, stand in a pool rather than an axe.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: Zul'jan throws 5 axes out from himself. Anyone they fly into is knocked back. Then they roll around the room for 3 minutes and hurt anyone they touch.</p><h3>Fangs of the Coiled Altar (Pools)</h3><p>What it does A raid hit, and poison pools pour in from both side walls.</p><ul><li>Everyone: Fangs hits the raid and pours poison pools in from both side walls. Stay near the boss.</li><li>Everyone: These first pools stop well short of the boss. Stay out of them for about 25 seconds.</li><li>Everyone: Before the next Fangs lands, walk into the lane between the walls. It's wider to the north and south.</li><li>Healers: The pools nearly touch by the boss. The raid keeps taking damage for about 25 seconds, so heal through it.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: A raid hit, and poison pools pour in from both side walls. The second set nearly meets by the boss and leaves a lane down the middle. His next hits poison the tank.</p><h3>Venomfang (Dispel)</h3><p>What it does A poison on two players.</p><ul><li>Healers: Two players get Venomfang. Dispel it right away, one healer for each.</li><li>Shamans: A Poison Cleansing Totem dispels it on its own.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: A poison on two players. It keeps ticking until it's dispelled.</p><h2>Phase 2: Malacrass</h2><p>Hex Lord Malacrass takes over. Mind controls, ghosts led into the frontal, bombs, adds, and a shield to break.</p><h3>Dreadmarch (Mind Control)</h3><p>What it does A few players are mind controlled and walk to the edge with a big shield on.</p><ul><li>Mind controlled: Press your cancel macro. It drops your speed boosts and shields, so the raid can free you fast.</li><li>Everyone: Bring them to one spot. Stun or root them there, one after another, and break their shields.</li><li>Freed: Stand still until your ghosts spawn, two each. Then face them: a ghost only moves while you look away.</li><li>Freed: Lead your ghosts into one pile in front of the boss. Then turn and look at them so they freeze.</li><li>Boss tank: Aim Soul Sever through the pile. Anyone it hits: pick up your 3 Soul Fragments or you die.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: A few players are mind controlled and walk to the edge with a big shield on. Once freed, two ghosts chase each of them. A ghost only moves while its player looks away.</p><h3>Gloombomb (Bombs)</h3><p>What it does Two players get a bomb that explodes 15 yards around them.</p><ul><li>Marked: Run 15 yards from everyone right away and press a defensive.</li><li>Everyone: Stay away from marked players. Hit by a bomb? Pick up your Soul Fragments.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: Two players get a bomb that explodes 15 yards around them. Anyone it hits must pick up their Soul Fragments.</p><h3>Eternal Nightfall (Shield Break)</h3><p>What it does Malacrass shields himself and starts a cast that wipes the raid.</p><ul><li>DPS: Burst the shield on Malacrass. Save your damage cooldowns for it.</li><li>Everyone: The shield breaks and the cast stops. Step out of the purple circles.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: Malacrass shields himself and starts a cast that wipes the raid. Breaking the shield stops the cast. Then purple circles drop.</p><h3>Spiteful Soulcoiler (Adds)</h3><p>What it does Two adds come with each set.</p><ul><li>Kickers: Kick every Wail of Terror. It's a raid-wide fear.</li><li>Kickers: The add teleports after each kick. The next kicker follows it.</li><li>Kickers: Keep the order going on both adds.</li><li>DPS: Kill them before they reach full energy.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: Two adds come with each set. They cast Wail of Terror, a raid fear, and teleport after each kick. At full energy they can't be kicked.</p><p>The tank frontal. The other tank taunts after each one. Aim it through the ghosts.</p><h2>Intermission: Soulbinding</h2><p>Zul'jan comes back healing himself but taking double damage. Bloodlust here.</p><h3>Fragments of Malacrass (Intercepts)</h3><p>What it does Zul'jan comes back healing 2% a second for 35 seconds, but he takes double damage.</p><ul><li>Everyone: Bloodlust now, plus potions and every damage cooldown. Zul'jan takes double damage.</li><li>Assigned players: Watch your side. Fragments walk in from the edges toward him.</li><li>Assigned players: Step into a fragment's path to stop it. Each stop hits the whole raid.</li><li>Healers: Stops come a couple at a time, spread out. Keep healing cooldowns ready for them.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: Zul'jan comes back healing 2% a second for 35 seconds, but he takes double damage. Fragments walk to him, and each one that gets there heals him about 10%. Each stop hits the whole raid.</p><h2>Phase 3: Coiled Union</h2><p>Both bosses at once, with everything from phases 1 and 2 together.</p><h3>Grim Guillotine (Soak)</h3><p>What it does Phase 1's axe again, now shadow damage.</p><ul><li>Axe target: Walk to a corner, away from the raid and the orbs.</li><li>Soak group: Walk in with them. At least 5 must be inside.</li><li>Everyone in it: Press a defensive as it lands, then walk out 40 yards.</li><li>Everyone: Gate into a soak, never out of one.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: Phase 1's axe again, now shadow damage. At least 5 share its 9-yard circle. Then the spot erupts 40 yards out.</p><h3>Defilement (Tank Swap)</h3><p>What it does It replaces Fangs.</p><ul><li>Everyone: Stay out of the green Defilement patches. They stay down for the rest of the fight.</li><li>Off-tank: Taunt when Corrupted Toxin lands on the other tank.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: It replaces Fangs. Patches of ground put heal absorbs on you, and Corrupted Toxin poisons the tank.</p><p>When one boss dies, the other gets 500% stronger. Keep their health close and kill them at the same time.</p><p>The frontal now hits orbs and ghosts both, and anyone it hits gets Gravebound: pick up your Soul Fragments.</p><h2>Putting it all together</h2><p>The whole fight in the order of a real kill. Quiet stretches are cut, and a loop that repeats plays once; the time shown is the real fight clock. Pick a moment to jump there.</p><p>The whole fight in order, timed by a real kill (5:32). Coloured lines are moments when two things happen at once.</p><ul><li>Axegrinder: 5 axes roll around the room from 0:12 for about 3 minutes. Step out of their way: every touch hurts.</li><li>Orbs: never pop more than a handful at once, and pop them all before Zul'jan dies.</li><li>From phase 3, Defilement ground stays down: stay out of it.</li></ul><h3>Phase 1: Zul'jan0:00–1:48</h3><p>Boss tank: Take Zul'jan to the middle. The raid stands around him.</p><h3>Phase 2: Malacrass1:48–2:54</h3><h3>Intermission: Soulbinding2:54–3:32</h3><h3>Phase 3: Coiled Union3:32–5:32</h3><p>DPS: Burst the shield with the cooldowns saved for it. The cast wipes the raid if it finishes.</p><p>DPS: Kill both bosses close together. When one dies, the other gets 500% stronger.</p><h2>Your job, by role</h2><p>Every line for each role. The Your job box at the top shows the first three.</p><ul><li>Phase 3: Corrupted Toxin also forces swaps.</li><li>Going into phase 2 the orb stacks are high: stack raid cooldowns there.</li><li>Intermission: every fragment stop hits the raid, so keep cooldowns ready.</li><li>Bloodlust in the intermission: Zul'jan takes double damage.</li><li>Phase 3: kill both bosses together.</li></ul>"},"the-coiled-altar/mythic":{"boss":"The Coiled Altar","d":"m","title":"The Coiled Altar Mythic guide: mechanics, positions and every job · Debrief","desc":"The Coiled Altar Mythic guide: Zul'jan, then Hex Lord Malacrass, then both. Purple orbs never touch another orb, axe soakers are planned, ghosts never…","html":"<h1>The Coiled Altar Mythic guide</h1><p>Zul'jan, then Hex Lord Malacrass, then both. Purple orbs never touch another orb, axe soakers are planned, ghosts never touch, and bombs go on the adds. This follows a plan that killed it; your raid may mark the room its own way.</p><h3>What wipes us</h3><ul><li>A purple touches a green: The purple ring touches it and the raid wipes.</li><li>Two ghosts touch: If two ghosts touch each other, both of their players die.</li><li>A fragment gets through: Each one that gets through heals him about 10%.</li><li>A purple left behind: When the next Toxic Deluge lands, it blows up and wipes the raid.</li></ul><h3>Your job</h3><ul><li>Boss tank: a sturdy tank (Blood Death Knight, Guardian Druid or Protection Warrior) holds the boss.</li><li>Off-tank: a mobile tank runs purple orbs and taunts for the entrance frontal. Protection Paladin is best, but any mobile tank works.</li><li>P2: Phase 2: one tank can take every Soul Sever (a big defensive and an external on the even ones); step to the diamond or circle to line it up with the ghosts.</li><li>Five healers: lots of raid damage, and you hold damage often anyway.</li><li>P2: Into phase 2 at 9 to 11 orb stacks: stack Spirit Link and raid cooldowns.</li><li>Intermission: mobile healers cover the four sides; save the big cooldowns for the 7-to-10 turn.</li><li>P1: Greens only, unless you're assigned purples (Warlocks in phase 1, Mages all fight). Clear greens out of purple carriers' way first.</li><li>P3: Hunters: soak axe 1, then Turtle on axe 2. Rogues: Feint on axe 1, Cloak in phase 3.</li><li>Gloombomb: take it to your add (ranged to the ranged add, melee to the melee add) and use your best defensive.</li></ul><h2>Phase 1: Zul'jan</h2><p>One boss in the middle. Two orb triangles, one each side, popped by alternating frontals; two axe soaks.</p><h3>Coalesced Venom (Orb Pops)</h3><p>What it does Green and purple orbs drop, 8 in all.</p><ul><li>Purple carriers: Warlocks wait at the entrance corners, Mages at the far corners. Each of you takes the purple nearest you. The off-tank takes an awkward one.</li><li>Everyone else: Move the greens out of the purple carriers' way first. Greens only. Never touch a purple.</li><li>Entrance side: Warlocks walk their purples to the tip, up to the marker line. Greens go between them and the boss.</li><li>Off-tank: Taunt right before Sever. It pops the entrance triangle, about 4 orbs.</li><li>Far side: Mages walk their purples to the tip. The raid fills in greens in front of them.</li><li>Boss tank: Taunt back before the second Sever. It pops the far triangle.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: Green and purple orbs drop, 8 in all. If a purple's ring touches any other orb, on the ground or carried, the raid wipes. Each Sever pops one triangle.</p><ul><li>The entrance side always gets the first frontal, so set that side up first. Since the Oct 13 tuning there are four purples, two on each side.</li><li>Each Warlock takes the purple nearest their corner; the Mages wait and Blink in only if needed, because the off-tank is the safer runner.</li><li>Warlocks: wait until greens on your path are picked up before you teleport, or you'll run a purple into them.</li><li>The two side markers show how far the frontal reaches from the middle. Drop orbs at or inside that line; if some are further back, pull the boss a little off centre.</li><li>You may run a carried green past a carried purple. A green on the ground must never touch a purple's ring.</li><li>Every orb is one stack, purple or green. Each Toxic Deluge has 8 orbs: about 4 per frontal (3 to 6), never all 8 at once.</li><li>Tanks: avoid a second Sever stack in phase 1 (it needs a big defensive and an external). Two Evokers' Time Spiral resets the runners' movement abilities.</li><li>End of the phase: keep popping orbs as usual, and push when the raid is at 11 stacks or fewer, with raid cooldowns stacked. Every orb left blows up when Zul'jan dies.</li></ul><h3>Guillotine (Soak)</h3><p>What it does An axe drops on one player.</p><ul><li>Axe target: Walk to the moon corner as soon as the axe marks you. Healers put an external and a Zephyr on you there.</li><li>Soak 1: Both Hunters, a Shadow Priest, a Rogue and a Warrior walk in with them, 6 or 7 in all. Press a defensive as it lands. Hunters save Turtle.</li><li>Everyone in it: When it lands, walk out 40 yards, back toward the boss. You have 6 seconds before the spot erupts.</li><li>Soak 2: The next axe comes 85 seconds later, to the same corner. Exactly 5 go in: the target, both tanks and both Hunters.</li><li>Soak 2: Tanks bring the boss and press a defensive. Hunters press Turtle. A Paladin puts Blessing of Protection on the target.</li><li>Everyone in it: Walk out 40 yards again. Gate into a soak, never out of one.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: An axe drops on one player. At least 5 must share its 9-yard circle, or it hits the whole raid. 6 seconds after it lands, the spot erupts 40 yards out. Soaking marks you for 100 seconds, and the next axe would hit you 500% harder.</p><ul><li>Phase 1 has two axes, at about 0:43 and 2:08. Phase 3 has three more.</li><li>This guide uses the moon corner. Any corner works, as long as it's away from the raid.</li><li>Why the Hunters soak the first axe: a player who soaked can't be picked as the next target. So neither Hunter can get axe 2, and both can Turtle it.</li><li>Good soak 1 extras: Shadow Priest (Dispersion), a Warrior (walls work, the phase 1 axe is physical), a Rogue (Feint).</li><li>Phase 1 axes are physical, so Blessing of Protection on the target works. Phase 3 axes are magic: see Grim Guillotine.</li><li>Gate into a soak, never out of one: orbs spawn on the path right after.</li></ul><h3>Axegrinder (Rolling Axes)</h3><p>What it does Zul'jan throws 5 axes at 0:14 and 5 more at 1:39.</p><ul><li>Melee: He throws 5 axes at 0:14 and 5 more at 1:39. They fly out from him and knock you back.</li><li>Everyone: Each set rolls for 3 minutes. From 1:39 to about 3:15 there are 10 at once.</li><li>Everyone: The dashed line shows where an axe is heading. Step aside early, also during soaks and orbs.</li><li>Everyone: A touch only hurts, but the hits add up. Ranged, stay out of melee.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: Zul'jan throws 5 axes at 0:14 and 5 more at 1:39. Each set rolls for 3 minutes, so 10 are out at once until about 3:15.</p><h3>Fangs of the Coiled Altar (Pools)</h3><p>What it does A raid hit, and poison pools pour in from both side walls.</p><ul><li>Everyone: Fangs hits the raid and pours poison pools in from both side walls. Stay near the boss.</li><li>Everyone: These first pools stop well short of the boss. Stay out of them for about 25 seconds.</li><li>Everyone: Before the next Fangs lands, walk into the lane between the walls. It's wider to the north and south.</li><li>Healers: The pools nearly touch by the boss. The raid keeps taking damage for about 25 seconds, so heal through it.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: A raid hit, and poison pools pour in from both side walls. The second set nearly meets by the boss and leaves a lane down the middle. His next hits poison the tank.</p><h3>Venomfang (Dispel)</h3><p>What it does A poison on two players.</p><ul><li>Healers: Two players get Venomfang. Dispel it right away, one healer for each.</li><li>Shamans: A Poison Cleansing Totem dispels it on its own.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: A poison on two players. It keeps ticking until it's dispelled.</p><h3>Phase 2 Push (Raid Cooldowns)</h3><p>What it does Going into phase 2, everyone still has 9 to 11 orb stacks ticking.</p><ul><li>Raid lead: Push into phase 2 at 11 stacks or fewer. Kill Zul'jan as his next pools come.</li><li>Everyone: Stack together in a spot clear of the axes. Press Darkness, Anti-Magic Zone and Spirit Link Totem. Stay in until the stacks drop.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: Going into phase 2, everyone still has 9 to 11 orb stacks ticking. Raid cooldowns get the raid through them.</p><h2>Phase 2: Malacrass</h2><p>Malacrass. Mind controls always stack on the square marker, ghosts get frozen apart and swept by the frontal, bombs go on the adds, and every second set brings the shield.</p><h3>Dreadmarch (Mind Control)</h3><p>What it does A few players are mind controlled with a big shield.</p><ul><li>Arrow over your head: Press your cancel macro. Everyone stacks on the square marker.</li><li>CC players: Stun or root them on the square, one after another: Ring of Peace, Shockwave, Chaos Nova. Everyone breaks the shields.</li><li>Freed: Stand still. Your ghosts spawn where you stand, by the square. Look at yours right away.</li><li>Freed: Keep your ghosts frozen and apart. If yours is outside the frontal, look away for a moment and let it creep in.</li><li>Boss tank: Step toward the diamond or the circle, so Soul Sever hits the most ghosts.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: A few players are mind controlled with a big shield. Once freed, two ghosts chase each of them. Only you see your ghosts, and two ghosts touching kills both players.</p><ul><li>The cancel macro removes your speed boosts and shields (Power Word: Shield, Void Shield and the like) so the stack can break you out fast. Press it only if you were picked.</li><li>Put an air-horn sound alert on the ghost fixate, so you turn and look right away.</li><li>A ghost the frontal missed is fine: walk it back toward the square for the next one.</li><li>One tank took every phase 2 frontal: a defensive on the odd ones, a big defensive and an external on the even ones. The stacks fall off before the third.</li><li>After the kicks, the other tank taunts the boss back to the square, so the Mages can hit the shields on the mind controls.</li><li>End of the phase: if your ghost is still up, keep looking at it and it vanishes at the transition. The transition knocks you back: stay off the edge.</li></ul><h3>Gloombomb (Add Bombs)</h3><p>What it does The two adds take almost no damage until 2 Gloombombs hit each one.</p><ul><li>Kickers: Kick both adds the moment the Gloombomb cast shows, so they stand still.</li><li>Bombs: Ranged bombs go to the ranged add, melee bombs to the melee add. Stand one on each side of it and press your best defensive.</li><li>Everyone else: Stay 15 yards away. The bombs break the shields. Now kill the adds.</li><li>Kickers: Kick every Wail of Terror. The melee add's second kick must be a melee.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: The two adds take almost no damage until 2 Gloombombs hit each one. Each time, 2 ranged and 2 melee get a bomb that explodes 15 yards around them.</p><ul><li>One add is the ranged add and the other the melee add. One player marks the ranged add and calls where both are: marked means ranged kicks, unmarked means melee kicks.</li><li>Which is which, by where they spawn (the plan's rule): an add at the moon is always ranged; an add beside the stack spot is always melee.</li><li>Skull add: melee if the other is at the moon, ranged if the other is at the square or star. Star add: melee if the other is at the moon or skull, ranged if the other is at the square.</li><li>Every second set of adds is followed by Eternal Nightfall, so the adds must die before the next set but the shield comes first.</li><li>The last set before the intermission doesn't need bombs: their shields break at the transition. Just keep kicking.</li></ul><h3>Eternal Nightfall (Shield Break)</h3><p>What it does It comes after every second set of adds.</p><ul><li>DPS: Burst the shield with the cooldowns assigned to this shield. Boss damage barely matters in this phase.</li><li>Everyone: The shield breaks and the cast stops. Step out of the purple circles.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: It comes after every second set of adds. The shield is the real damage check of the phase. Then purple circles drop.</p><h3>Spiteful Soulcoiler (Adds)</h3><p>What it does Two adds come with each set.</p><ul><li>Raid lead: Mark the ranged add. Ranged kick the marked one, melee kick the other.</li><li>Kickers: Kick every Wail of Terror. The add teleports after each kick.</li><li>Kickers: The next kicker in the order takes the next cast, even after a teleport.</li><li>DPS: Kill them fast. They hit the raid while alive. Warlocks: Curse of Tongues on both.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: Two adds come with each set. They hit the raid every 3 seconds while alive and cast Wail of Terror, a raid fear.</p><p>One tank can take them all: a defensive on odd casts, a big defensive and an external on even ones. Aim it through as many ghosts as you can.</p><h2>Intermission: Soulbinding</h2><p>Zul'jan comes back healing himself but taking double damage. Bloodlust here.</p><h3>Fragments of Malacrass (Intercepts)</h3><p>What it does Zul'jan heals 2% a second but takes double damage.</p><ul><li>Everyone: Bloodlust, potions, every cooldown. You held damage at the end of phase 2 for this.</li><li>Mobile healers and off-tank: Cover your side of the room. The rest circle the boss. One fragment spawns on the inner ring.</li><li>Assigned players: Stop 3 to 5 by stepping in their path. Then let the stacks drop.</li><li>Assigned players: Again 3 to 5, wait, repeat. About five rounds.</li><li>Healers: Near the end, stack raid cooldowns in the middle. The raid stops 7 to 10 at once.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: Zul'jan heals 2% a second but takes double damage. Each fragment that reaches him heals him about 10%. Each stop hits the raid and makes the next one 20% stronger for 2 seconds.</p><ul><li>Spots: the mobile healers spread to the four sides, the priests stay near the melee (they're less mobile), and the off-tank soaks far fragments.</li><li>The Augmentation Evoker, the Hunters and the Warlocks stand together on one side, so the Augmentation buffs land on the right people for the burn.</li><li>Melee play defence: you're the most mobile, so make sure none get in.</li><li>Getting Zul'jan under about 35% here puts the kill within reach.</li></ul><h2>Phase 3: Coiled Union</h2><p>Both bosses, everything at once. Play on the star and square side; purples are all that matter; kill Zul'jan before his next pool cast.</p><h3>Blighted Sever (Purple Diamond)</h3><p>What it does The frontal is bigger now and hits orbs and ghosts.</p><ul><li>Purple carriers: Both Mages, the off-tank and the Evoker healer take the purples.</li><li>Everyone else: Move the greens out of the middle and line them up on one side. A missed green is fine.</li><li>Purple carriers: Build the purple diamond in the middle, each well apart. Use a purple that's already in place.</li><li>Off-tank: Taunt and aim the first Blighted Sever through the whole diamond.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: The frontal is bigger now and hits orbs and ghosts. The first one in phase 3 takes every purple at once.</p><ul><li>Gloombomb with no adds up: just run to the edge of the room, away from the raid. Speed boosts help.</li><li>The next orb sets are built around the circle and diamond markers: the circle side gets its frontal first (two purples by the circle).</li><li>Damage: kill Zul'jan before his pool cast, about a minute after the second shield, because every orb blows up then. Malacrass at 7 or 8% when Zul'jan dies is fine. Don't over-damage Malacrass.</li><li>The last frontals hit 0 or 1 orbs. The last adds just need kicks.</li><li>Axes in phase 3: the first at the star marker, the second and third at the square.</li></ul><h3>Grim Guillotine (Soak)</h3><p>What it does Three magic axes in phase 3.</p><ul><li>Axe 1 target: Walk to the star marker. Healers put big externals on you.</li><li>Soak 1: 4 players who never soaked walk in with them. Press a defensive, then walk out 40 yards.</li><li>Axe 2 target: Walk to the square marker. Big externals again.</li><li>Soak 2: Players who already soaked go in with Cloak, Bubble or Spellwarding. At least 5 in all, then walk out.</li><li>Axe 3 target: The square again. Axe 1's soakers are free again by now.</li><li>Soak 3: Send everyone free of the debuff, plus any immunity. Four wipes, so send six.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: Three magic axes in phase 3. At least 5 share each 9-yard circle. Soaking marks you for 120 seconds, so axe 2 needs new players or immunities.</p><ul><li>These axes are magic: a Rogue's Cloak, a Paladin's Bubble or Blessing of Spellwarding lets a player who already soaked take one. Blessing of Protection doesn't work here.</li><li>Big externals on every axe target.</li><li>Gate into a soak, never out of one: orbs spawn on the path.</li></ul><h3>Defilement (Tank Swap)</h3><p>What it does It replaces Fangs.</p><ul><li>Everyone: Stay out of the green Defilement patches. They stay down for the rest of the fight.</li><li>Off-tank: Taunt when Corrupted Toxin lands on the other tank.</li></ul><p>WHAT IT DOES: It replaces Fangs. Patches of ground put heal absorbs on you, and Corrupted Toxin poisons the tank.</p><p>When one dies the other gets 500% stronger. Kill Zul'jan first, before his pool cast, with Malacrass around 7 to 8%.</p><h2>Putting it all together</h2><p>The whole fight in the order of a real kill. Quiet stretches are cut, and a loop that repeats plays once; the time shown is the real fight clock. Pick a moment to jump there.</p><p>The whole fight in order, timed by a real kill (10:18). Coloured lines are moments when two things happen at once.</p><ul><li>Axegrinder: 5 axes roll around the room from 0:14, 10 from 1:39, and the last ones vanish around 4:40. Step out of their way: every touch hurts.</li><li>From phase 3, Defilement ground stays down: stay out of it.</li></ul><h3>Phase 1: Zul'jan0:00–2:57</h3><p>Boss tank: Take Zul'jan to the middle. Warlocks wait at the entrance corners, Mages at the far corners.</p><p>Purple carriers: Warlocks wait at the entrance corners, Mages at the far corners.</p><p>Healers: Two players get Venomfang. Dispel it fast. A Poison Cleansing Totem does it on its own.</p><p>Melee: He throws 5 more axes. With the first 5 still rolling, that's 10 at once.</p><p>Raid lead: Push into phase 2 at 11 stacks or fewer, with raid cooldowns ready.</p><h3>Phase 2: Malacrass2:57–6:32</h3><p>DPS: Burst the shield with the cooldowns saved for it. The cast wipes the raid if it finishes.</p><p>Everyone: Hold damage at the end of phase 2. Keep looking at your ghost and it vanishes at the transition.</p><h3>Intermission: Soulbinding6:32–7:10</h3><h3>Phase 3: Coiled Union7:10–10:18</h3><p>DPS: Kill Zul'jan first, before his pool cast, about a minute after the second shield.</p><h2>Your job, by role</h2><p>Every line for each role. The Your job box at the top shows the first three.</p><ul><li>Phase 2: one tank can take every Soul Sever (a big defensive and an external on the even ones); step to the diamond or circle to line it up with the ghosts.</li><li>Soak the second phase 1 axe with a defensive.</li><li>Into phase 2 at 9 to 11 orb stacks: stack Spirit Link and raid cooldowns.</li><li>Mobile healers (Evoker especially) can carry purples between mechanics; in phase 3 the Evoker healer is a carrier.</li><li>Greens only, unless you're assigned purples (Warlocks in phase 1, Mages all fight). Clear greens out of purple carriers' way first.</li><li>Hunters: soak axe 1, then Turtle on axe 2. Rogues: Feint on axe 1, Cloak in phase 3.</li><li>Pool damage and cooldowns for every Eternal Nightfall shield; boss damage barely matters in phase 2.</li><li>Bloodlust in the intermission. Phase 3: kill Zul'jan before his pool cast; don't over-damage Malacrass.</li></ul>"},"ulatek/heroic":{"boss":"Ula'tek","d":"h","title":"Ula'tek Heroic guide: mechanics, positions and every job · Debrief","desc":"Ula'tek Heroic guide: A long fight on a set clock. Phase 1: carry eggs, soak the coils, dodge her waves, burn her heart. Phase 2: the raid splits onto the…","html":"<h1>Ula'tek Heroic guide</h1><p>A long fight on a set clock. Phase 1: carry eggs, soak the coils, dodge her waves, burn her heart. Phase 2: the raid splits onto the two side ledges. Then the platform shatters and the last phase moves from quarter to quarter.</p><h3>What wipes us</h3><ul><li>An egg left on the floor: Nobody picks up one egg: her waves roll over it and it hatches a Viper, with Putrid Membrane on the whole raid.</li><li>Everyone snaps at once: All three tethers snap together: three stacks of Blight Vein on everyone at the same moment.</li><li>One group takes two in a row: Group one goes for the second coil too: Soul Constrictor means they don't count, so it hits almost unsoaked.</li><li>A clutch left to hatch: Nobody breaks the far quarter's clutch: it hatches a Shrieker, and nobody kicks its Vicious Echoes.</li></ul><h3>Your job</h3><ul><li>One tank on her and one on the tail at all times; never two Mother's Wraths in a row.</li><li>P1: Phase 1: after Mother's Wrath, run straight back in; when she dives, be at the bubbles first.</li><li>P2: Phase 2: tank the Warden on the gestating eggs, then grab the Doomscales and bring them back.</li><li>P1: Phase 1: every coil soak hits the raid: cooldowns right before the first.</li><li>P2: Phase 2: Blight Vein follows each tether break, so they break two at a time.</li><li>Intermission: the slams come back to back, often harder to heal than phase 3.</li><li>P1: Phase 1: carry eggs safely until a soak breaks them, and kill Rawlings fast.</li><li>P2: Phase 2: kick Malice and Anguished Cry.</li><li>P3: Phase 3: break the clutches, and stand in the red circles when it's your group.</li></ul><h2>Phase 1: Fury of the Serpent Mother</h2><p>She rises at the north edge with her tail beside her. Carry the eggs, soak the coils, dodge her waves, then burn her heart.</p><h3>Call of the Serpent (Carry the eggs)</h3><p>Eggs crash down around the platform.</p><p>Eggs crash down around the platform. Let one touch venom (a wave, the water, or a carrier dying) and a deadly Viper hatches. Carry them until a coil soak breaks them: then only a weak Rawling comes out.</p><ul><li>Eggs incoming: step out of the impact circles.</li><li>Each carrier walks to the egg in their zone.</li><li>Pick it up: carrying it hurts a little over time.</li><li>Keep it away from venom until a coil soak breaks it.</li><li>On the pull, the first egg anyone touches pulls her: pick up the three starting eggs together.</li><li>Dying with an egg, or taking a wave while carrying one, hatches a Viper: kill it at once.</li><li>An immunity keeps you alive in a wave, but the egg still hatches.</li></ul><h3>Spectral Coils (Two groups, two circles)</h3><p>Two coils rise at the edge by her tail and slam one after the other, about 3 seconds apart.</p><p>Two coils rise at the edge by her tail and slam one after the other, about 3 seconds apart. Everyone within 10 yards of a slam shares it. Soul Constrictor stops a soaker helping with the next one for 5 seconds, so two groups.</p><ul><li>The tail comes up at the edge; its tank stays on it.</li><li>Facing the tail, the right-hand coil slams first: group one in it.</li><li>It slams: carried eggs break into weak Rawlings. Kill them.</li><li>Group two takes the second slam.</li><li>Raid cooldowns right before the first slam: Spirit Link, Darkness, Anti-Magic Zone.</li><li>Never take a wave to reach a soak: take the other one instead.</li></ul><h3>Caustic Waves (Go to the other half)</h3><p>She pulls one wing back and waves of venom roll across that half of the platform, then the other half a few seconds later; her tail sends waves of its own.</p><p>She pulls one wing back and waves of venom roll across that half of the platform, then the other half a few seconds later; her tail sends waves of its own. A hit is a huge stacking poison, and it hatches a carried egg.</p><ul><li>Her wing goes back: that half goes first. Go to the other half.</li><li>Her waves roll over the first half.</li><li>Cross behind the first wave before the second half goes.</li><li>Stay out until it has rolled past. Carrying an egg? A hit hatches it.</li></ul><h3>Mother's Wrath (Run back in)</h3><p>A 5-second cast knocks her tank back, then 3 seconds of bites hit whoever is in the red circle in front of her.</p><p>A 5-second cast knocks her tank back, then 3 seconds of bites hit whoever is in the red circle in front of her. Each bite stacks Stone Venom and Hobbled (55% slower). If her tank is out of reach, the bites hit everyone.</p><ul><li>Mother's Wrath: her tank is knocked back.</li><li>Run straight back into the red circle in front of her.</li><li>Take the bites with a big defensive; Blessing of Freedom takes the slow off.</li><li>The other tank taunts: swap during her waves cast, never two in a row.</li></ul><h3>Mephitic Thrash (Get 35 yards away)</h3><p>Her tail sweeps the ground: a big hit and a knockback within 35 yards, a smaller hit and damage over time beyond.</p><ul><li>The tail winds up.</li><li>Everyone near it runs out of the 35 yards, its tank too.</li><li>It lands: back to your spots. Keep eggs away from it.</li></ul><h3>Rage of the Shackled (Burn the heart)</h3><p>Her Venomous Heart opens for 20 seconds and takes double damage, while she hits the raid every 4 seconds.</p><p>Her Venomous Heart opens for 20 seconds and takes double damage, while she hits the raid every 4 seconds. Falling Debris lands in brown circles that kill anyone they hit, tanks too.</p><ul><li>A 6-second cast, then her heart opens: save cooldowns and a potion.</li><li>Everyone on the heart, the adds dragged in for the cleave.</li><li>Raid damage every 4 seconds: heal through it.</li><li>Brown circles: step out before the debris lands.</li><li>On Heroic, Bloodlust here.</li><li>She ends on the left or right edge: that side's phase 2 half stays, the other walks across.</li></ul><h3>She dives (Watch the bubbles)</h3><p>After her first waves (1:02 on Heroic, 1:17 on Mythic) she dives and comes up where bubbles rise at the edge.</p><p>After her first waves (1:02 on Heroic, 1:17 on Mythic) she dives and comes up where bubbles rise at the edge. Her tank gets there before she lands: an empty melee range hits the whole raid.</p><ul><li>After her first waves she dives.</li><li>Bubbles rise at the edge where she will come up.</li><li>Her tank is there before she lands: an empty melee range hits the whole raid.</li></ul><p>One tank on her and one on the tail at all times. Nobody on the tail: Rattler Slam hits the raid. Nobody in melee of her: Unchecked Rage hits the raid.</p><p>A broken egg hatches a weak Rawling. Kill it.</p><p>An egg that touches venom hatches a deadly Viper: kill it first. Each Viper leaves Putrid Membrane, damage over time on the raid.</p><h2>Phase 2: Children of the Doomscale</h2><p>The raid splits in two and crosses to the side ledges. Each half kills a Warden, carries eggs to the cauldron and kills the Doomscales, then comes back for the second heart burn.</p><h3>Doomscale Warden (Kick Malice, cleave the clutch)</h3><p>Each half fights a Doomscale Warden on its ledge.</p><p>Each half fights a Doomscale Warden on its ledge. While it lives, eggs can't be picked up and hurt anyone within 7 yards. Writhing Gestation turns nearby eggs into a clutch that hatches in 20 seconds. Kick every Malice: a deadly raid channel.</p><ul><li>Wait for her last wave to pass, tank first, then cross: a Warlock gate or your own movement.</li><li>The Warden Shadow Molts to the eggs and starts Writhing Gestation.</li><li>Cleave the gestating eggs first; stay out of their 7 yards.</li><li>Kick every Malice, then kill the Warden.</li></ul><h3>Grasping Fangs (Break two at a time)</h3><p>The Warden tethers three players on each ledge.</p><p>The Warden tethers three players on each ledge. A tether breaks with distance and leaves Blight Vein, damage over time on everyone that stacks with every break.</p><ul><li>The Warden tethers three players.</li><li>They walk out to the edge of their tethers.</li><li>Two break first; the raid heals through their Blight Vein.</li><li>The last one breaks once it has fallen off.</li></ul><h3>Doomscale Eggs (Small first, the big one last)</h3><p>With the Warden dead, carry the small eggs to the cauldron in the pit and kill the Rawlings.</p><p>With the Warden dead, carry the small eggs to the cauldron in the pit and kill the Rawlings. The big egg goes last, before Hatching Doom ends, or everyone is stunned 20 seconds: a wipe. Kick the Weakened Doomscale's Anguished Cry; bring it to her.</p><ul><li>Warden dead: pick up the small eggs.</li><li>Small eggs into the cauldron first: kill the Rawlings.</li><li>Then one player brings the big Doomscale Egg, well before its Hatching Doom ends.</li><li>A Weakened Doomscale climbs out: tank it, kick Anguished Cry, aim Desperate Thrash away, and bring it back to her.</li></ul><h3>Rage of the Shackled (The second burn)</h3><p>The second heart burn (4:38 on Heroic, 4:48 on Mythic).</p><p>The second heart burn (4:38 on Heroic, 4:48 on Mythic). The same as the first, with the Doomscales dragged in for the cleave.</p><ul><li>Back on the platform by her: the heart opens for 20 seconds.</li><li>Everything on the heart, the Doomscales cleaved with it.</li><li>Raid damage every 4 seconds.</li><li>Brown circles kill in one hit: step out.</li></ul><p>The raid splits in two and crosses the venom to the side ledges. Wait for her last wave line to pass, tank first, then movement abilities or a Warlock gate. On the way back, the side without a gate needs its own way.</p><p>Debris falls on the ledges too: the brown circles kill even a tank. Run through one only right after it lands.</p><h2>Intermission: The Shattering</h2><p>Eggs rain down and coils rise around the platform for back-to-back soaks. Then the platform shatters into four.</p><h3>Spectral Coils (Two groups take turns)</h3><p>Six coils rise round the platform and slam one after another, each needing a soak.</p><p>Six coils rise round the platform and slam one after another, each needing a soak. Nobody soaks two in a row (Soul Constrictor), so two groups take turns. Bring falling eggs into the soaks to break them. Then the platform shatters and knocks everyone back.</p><ul><li>Eggs rain down and coils rise around the platform.</li><li>Group one soaks the first coil.</li><li>Group two the next, and so on, turn about; bring eggs into the soaks.</li><li>Then stand toward the inside: the shattering knocks everyone back.</li></ul><p>The platform breaks into four with a big knockback. Stand toward the middle: from the edge you land in the venom.</p><h2>Phase 3: Ula'tek's Ascension</h2><p>The platform is in four quarters. Clutches land, red circles need soaking, and she smashes the quarter she's on, so the raid moves one quarter counter-clockwise each time.</p><h3>Call of the Serpent (Break every clutch)</h3><p>A Blightscale Clutch lands on every quarter.</p><p>A Blightscale Clutch lands on every quarter. Break each one (a weak Rawling comes out), or it hatches a Blightscale Shrieker: kick its Vicious Echoes, a raid stun.</p><ul><li>Clutches land on the quarters: step out of the circles.</li><li>Melee break the one by her; ranged the one on their quarter and the one at the back.</li><li>Kill each before it hatches.</li></ul><h3>Serpent's Bite (Soak every red circle)</h3><p>A melee, a ranged and a healer each get a 7-yard red circle for 15 seconds.</p><p>A melee, a ranged and a healer each get a 7-yard red circle for 15 seconds. Others stand in it until it drains, or the target turns to stone and hurts the raid every second. Soakers then spread out. Four sets, one per quarter.</p><ul><li>Three red circles: on a melee, a ranged and a healer.</li><li>Each group soaks its own red circle.</li><li>Never leave one unsoaked.</li><li>Volatile Purge: spread out at once.</li><li>Keep a backup ready for an off-role player who gets a circle.</li><li>Pick one side per group for the circles and tell everyone.</li></ul><h3>Circling Prey (One quarter counter-clockwise)</h3><p>She destroys the quarter she's on: a huge hit and a knockback within 13 yards, a smaller hit on everyone else.</p><p>She destroys the quarter she's on: a huge hit and a knockback within 13 yards, a smaller hit on everyone else. Everyone moves one quarter counter-clockwise, to her left. Personals for the second and third.</p><ul><li>Circling Prey: she's about to destroy the quarter she's on.</li><li>Everyone moves one quarter counter-clockwise.</li><li>It lands: personals for the second and third.</li><li>She comes up at the next corner.</li></ul><h3>Mother's Wrath (Taunt swap)</h3><p>The same knockback on her tank, now from the corner of the quarter she's on.</p><p>The same knockback on her tank, now from the corner of the quarter she's on. Her tank walks straight back into the red circle in front of her before the bites start, and the tanks swap at her next waves cast.</p><ul><li>Mother's Wrath: a 5-second cast on her tank.</li><li>Knocked back: walk straight back into the red circle in front of her.</li><li>Inside before the bites start, with a big defensive.</li><li>The other tank taunts her back at her next waves cast.</li></ul><p>Her waves still come in this phase, from behind. Watch for them, step out, then spread back out.</p><p>Kick its Vicious Echoes (a raid stun and stacking damage over time; never let it reach 2 stacks) and tank it to the boss.</p><p>Rage of the Shackled one more time (9:27 on Heroic, 9:37 on Mythic). If she's nearly dead as it ends, jump off behind her: the venom hurts, but you dodge the slam and live a few seconds longer. Use a normal potion here.</p><h2>Putting it all together</h2><p>The whole fight in the order of a real kill. Quiet stretches are cut, and a loop that repeats plays once; the time shown is the real fight clock. Pick a moment to jump there.</p><p>The whole fight in order, timed by a real kill (9:48). Coloured lines are moments when two things happen at once.</p><ul><li>One tank on her and one on the tail all fight: an empty melee range hits the whole raid.</li><li>Her waves come out of her, half the platform at a time: go to the other half.</li></ul><h3>Phase 1: Fury of the Serpent Mother0:00–2:56</h3><p>She rises at the north edge, her tail on the west side. The carriers stand in their zones; on Mythic the off tank heads for the spectral image.</p><p>Her tank is knocked back. Run straight back into the red circle in front of her.</p><p>The tail comes up at the edge; its tank stays on it. Facing the tail, the right-hand coil slams first: group one in it.</p><p>The tail winds up. Everyone near it runs out of the 35 yards, its tank too.</p><h3>Phase 2: Children of the Doomscale2:56–5:09</h3><p>Warden dead: pick up the small eggs. Small eggs into the cauldron first: kill the Rawlings.</p><h3>Intermission: The Shattering5:09–6:07</h3><p>Eggs rain down and coils rise around the platform. Group one soaks the first coil.</p><h3>Phase 3: Ula'tek's Ascension6:07–9:48</h3><p>She's about to destroy the quarter she's on. Everyone moves one quarter counter-clockwise.</p><p>A 5-second cast on her tank. Knocked back: walk straight back into the red circle in front of her.</p><p>Three red circles: on a melee, a ranged and a healer. Each group soaks its own red circle.</p><p>Rage of the Shackled one last time: burn the heart. If she's at 1 or 2% as it ends, jump off behind her to live a few seconds longer.</p><h2>Your job, by role</h2><p>Every line for each role. The Your job box at the top shows the first three.</p><ul><li>Phase 1: after Mother's Wrath, run straight back in; when she dives, be at the bubbles first.</li><li>Phase 2: tank the Warden on the gestating eggs, then grab the Doomscales and bring them back.</li><li>Phase 3: swap on Mother's Wrath.</li><li>Phase 1: every coil soak hits the raid: cooldowns right before the first.</li><li>Phase 2: Blight Vein follows each tether break, so they break two at a time.</li><li>Phase 3: the red-circle soakers need big healing.</li><li>Phase 1: carry eggs safely until a soak breaks them, and kill Rawlings fast.</li><li>Phase 2: kick Malice and Anguished Cry.</li><li>Phase 3: break the clutches, and stand in the red circles when it's your group.</li></ul>"},"ulatek/mythic":{"boss":"Ula'tek","d":"m","title":"Ula'tek Mythic guide: mechanics, positions and every job · Debrief","desc":"Ula'tek Mythic guide: Egg carriers' 3-yard rings must never touch. A Wretch makes a bubble to stand in, four Doomscales need kicks, the intermission takes…","html":"<h1>Ula'tek Mythic guide</h1><p>Egg carriers' 3-yard rings must never touch. A Wretch makes a bubble to stand in, four Doomscales need kicks, the intermission takes three soak groups, and the last phase needs grips. Easier since the Oct 6 tuning than in the video.</p><h3>What wipes us</h3><ul><li>Two carriers touch: Two egg carriers drift within 3 yards: the egg hatches at once, a Viper, and Putrid Membrane on the raid.</li><li>The five snap one by one: After the solo snap, the five snap one after another instead of together: extra ticks of every Blight Vein.</li><li>One group takes two in a row: Group one goes for the second coil too: Soul Constrictor means they don't count, so it hits almost unsoaked.</li><li>A clutch reaches the venom: The far clutch is never gripped: it crawls into the venom and hatches a Shrieker.</li></ul><h3>Your job</h3><ul><li>P1: Phase 1: the off tank soaks the spectral venom (waves off the platform), then takes the tail and the Wretch; the boss tank runs back in after Mother's Wrath. Never two Mother's Wraths in a row.</li><li>She dives at 1:17: her tank waits at the bubbles.</li><li>P2: Phase 2: pull the Warden across the line so it teleports to the eggs; grab both Doomscales and bring them back.</li><li>P1: Phase 1: raid cooldowns just before the first coil slam; the brown circles in the heart burn kill in one hit.</li><li>P2: Phase 2: the five-player tether break needs Anti-Magic Zone and Rallying Cry, plus Spirit Link or Darkness.</li><li>Intermission: personals and potions around 5:50 for the back-to-back slams.</li><li>P1: Phase 1: 8 carriers in their zones, rings never touch; the egg-damage players break the shells. An immunity won't save your egg from a wave.</li><li>P1: Phase 1: the whole raid in the bubble on the first coil circle, carriers rings apart, then into the second circle.</li><li>P2: Phase 2: one mobile ranged breaks their tether alone, then five break together 4 seconds later.</li></ul><h2>Phase 1: Fury of the Serpent Mother</h2><p>Two rounds of 8 eggs (0:01 and 1:11), two Wretches, two coil soaks, two big wave sets with a dive between them, then the heart at 2:25. The hardest part of the fight.</p><h3>Call of the Serpent (Break, carry, spread)</h3><p>Eight eggs crash down at random, twice in phase 1.</p><p>Eight eggs crash down at random, twice in phase 1. Each one has a shell to break first, and carrying one puts a 3-yard ring on you. If two carriers' rings touch, a Viper hatches.</p><ul><li>Eggs incoming: step out of the impact circles.</li><li>The egg-damage players (two Balance Druids, or Balance and Shadow) break the shells.</li><li>Carriers: grab the egg in your zone; zones turn with her. Mages take the far left and far right.</li><li>Your ring is 3 yards: never let it touch another carrier's.</li><li>Pick 8 carriers and backups, no more than 2 or 3 melee. Each owns an area set by which way she faces, not by the floor markers.</li><li>Breaking an egg gives Rancid Yolk for 20 seconds; picking up another while it's on you kills you, so let it drop first.</li><li>Dying with an egg, or taking a wave while carrying one, hatches a Viper. An immunity keeps you alive, but the egg still hatches.</li><li>Second egg set (1:11): the bubble comes before the waves; melee take their eggs before it, ranged after.</li></ul><h3>Blightscale Wretch (Soak its venom, then the bubble)</h3><p>An add at 0:03 and 1:12 (and again in phase 3).</p><p>An add at 0:03 and 1:12 (and again in phase 3). A spectral Ula'tek at the edge pours venom at it; the off tank steps into that stream instead. About every 34 seconds it makes a 10-yard bubble and blasts everyone outside it.</p><ul><li>The Wretch lands where the Toxic Womb was.</li><li>Off tank: stand in the spectral venom with a defensive, and aim its waves off the platform.</li><li>Fester Burst: everyone inside its 10 yards, carriers too, rings apart.</li><li>Kill it before its third bubble.</li><li>The tail comes up on the same side as the spectral image, so you know early where the soaks will be.</li><li>Its enrage only adds tank damage: kill it between its second and third bubble. Kill the old Wretch as the new one lands.</li><li>First bubble: on the first coil circle, whole raid in it. Second bubble: after the second eggs, in the middle by her.</li></ul><h3>Spectral Coils (Two groups, two circles)</h3><p>First set: the Wretch's bubble goes up on the first circle and the carriers take that slam inside it; group two takes the second.</p><p>First set: the Wretch's bubble goes up on the first circle and the carriers take that slam inside it; group two takes the second. Second set: the carriers split between the circles, and right after both slams the bubble goes up between them.</p><ul><li>The tail comes up at the edge; its tank stays on it.</li><li>Facing the tail, the right-hand coil slams first: group one in it.</li><li>It slams: carried eggs break into weak Rawlings. Kill them.</li><li>Group two takes the second slam.</li><li>Outside the Wretch's bubble is a big hit and damage over time; the tanks decide where it goes by where they hold the Wretch.</li><li>Second set (2:11): carrying 8 eggs through her waves into the double slam is the hardest moment of phase 1. A wave is always coming: keep dodging in your soak.</li><li>Rawlings are padding for the burn: no careless area damage, keep them to the side of the boss.</li></ul><h3>Caustic Waves (The lane steps across, Mages Blink)</h3><p>The second set at 1:47 is the hardest moment of the fight: eggs out, her waves and the tails' waves at once, with carriers who must not touch.</p><p>The second set at 1:47 is the hardest moment of the fight: eggs out, her waves and the tails' waves at once, with carriers who must not touch. A wave kills unless you have a big defensive up.</p><ul><li>The caller says which half she starts on. The lane steps to the other half together, rings apart.</li><li>Her first half rolls; small tails rise at the corners and fire a moment later: step out of their paths.</li><li>Her other half next: the lane crosses back behind her first wave. Mages at the sides Blink back over it.</li><li>Ten seconds later two more tails fire from the edges.</li><li>Mages make the best outer carriers: they Blink over a wave when there's no room. Two Evokers' Time Spiral helps them.</li><li>Stay in your own area when the small tails fire. A big defensive lets you live through one hit.</li></ul><h3>Mother's Wrath (Run back in)</h3><p>A 5-second cast knocks her tank back, then 3 seconds of bites hit whoever is in the red circle in front of her.</p><p>A 5-second cast knocks her tank back, then 3 seconds of bites hit whoever is in the red circle in front of her. Each bite stacks Stone Venom and Hobbled (55% slower). If her tank is out of reach, the bites hit everyone.</p><ul><li>Mother's Wrath: her tank is knocked back.</li><li>Run straight back into the red circle in front of her.</li><li>Take the bites with a big defensive; Blessing of Freedom takes the slow off.</li><li>The other tank taunts: swap during her waves cast, never two in a row.</li></ul><h3>Mephitic Thrash (Get 35 yards away)</h3><p>Her tail sweeps the ground: a big hit and a knockback within 35 yards, a smaller hit and damage over time beyond.</p><ul><li>The tail winds up.</li><li>Everyone near it runs out of the 35 yards, its tank too.</li><li>It lands: back to your spots. Keep eggs away from it.</li></ul><h3>Rage of the Shackled (Burn the heart)</h3><p>Her Venomous Heart opens for 20 seconds and takes double damage, while she hits the raid every 4 seconds.</p><p>Her Venomous Heart opens for 20 seconds and takes double damage, while she hits the raid every 4 seconds. Falling Debris lands in brown circles that kill anyone they hit, tanks too.</p><ul><li>A 6-second cast, then her heart opens: save cooldowns and a potion.</li><li>Everyone on the heart, the adds dragged in for the cleave.</li><li>Raid damage every 4 seconds: heal through it.</li><li>Brown circles: step out before the debris lands.</li><li>Start your cooldowns when the cast is about 2.7 seconds from the end, so they're up as it opens.</li><li>Under about 66% after this burn means you're on pace for a kill.</li><li>Where she ends sets where the second burn starts: the half on her side gets the short walk back.</li></ul><h3>She dives (Watch the bubbles)</h3><p>After her first waves (1:02 on Heroic, 1:17 on Mythic) she dives and comes up where bubbles rise at the edge.</p><p>After her first waves (1:02 on Heroic, 1:17 on Mythic) she dives and comes up where bubbles rise at the edge. Her tank gets there before she lands: an empty melee range hits the whole raid.</p><ul><li>After her first waves she dives.</li><li>Bubbles rise at the edge where she will come up.</li><li>Her tank is there before she lands: an empty melee range hits the whole raid.</li></ul><p>One tank on her and one on the tail at all times. Nobody on the tail: Rattler Slam hits the raid. Nobody in melee of her: Unchecked Rage hits the raid.</p><p>Kill Rawlings within 25 seconds: once they enrage they cast Acidic Expulsion, damage over time on the whole raid.</p><p>An egg that touches venom hatches a deadly Viper: kill it first. Each Viper leaves Putrid Membrane, damage over time on the raid.</p><h2>Phase 2: Children of the Doomscale</h2><p>Two even halves (the right side has one extra egg). One Warlock can gate both sides. Mythic adds a five-player tether break under raid cooldowns, and four Doomscales that enrage when one dies.</p><h3>Doomscale Warden (Pull it across, cleave, kick)</h3><p>Each half gates to its ledge.</p><p>Each half gates to its ledge. Pull the Warden across the line and it teleports to the eggs and gestates every egg within 10 yards. Cleave those first, kick every Malice, and kill the Warden before its next gestation.</p><ul><li>Wait for her last wave line to pass, then gate across: the Warlock places a gate for each side.</li><li>The tank pulls the Warden to the stairs: it teleports to the eggs and starts Writhing Gestation.</li><li>Break those egg shields and kill the Rawlings by 3:12, everyone off the eggs.</li><li>Kick every Malice (a 15-second cooldown: the melee share it), and kill the Warden before its next gestation.</li><li>Start at the north end of the ledge, where most eggs are, then work south.</li><li>Split evenly: the right side has one extra egg. Make sure each side can kill its Warden in time.</li><li>With one Warlock: they place the other side's gate first, then Soulburn to place their own. The side without one takes the movement classes for the way back.</li></ul><h3>Grasping Fangs (One break, then five together)</h3><p>The Warden tethers six players.</p><p>The Warden tethers six players. One mobile ranged breaks theirs alone; then the other five break together, at full stretch, away from the eggs, inside stacked raid cooldowns.</p><ul><li>One mobile ranged (a Mage, Hunter or Balance Druid) walks out and breaks theirs alone.</li><li>The other five walk to the edge of their tethers, away from the eggs, inside Anti-Magic Zone and Rallying Cry.</li><li>Four seconds later, as that Blight Vein ends, the five break on the same second: strongest defensive and a potion or Healthstone.</li><li>Someone counts down the break; an addon timer shows it too.</li><li>Cooldowns per side: an Anti-Magic Zone and a Rallying Cry each, plus Spirit Link Totem on one side and Darkness on the other.</li><li>Be at the end of your tether two seconds early: a late break adds 1 to 3 ticks for everyone.</li><li>If the Warden dies early, don't pick up an egg until your Blight Vein is gone.</li></ul><h3>Doomscale Eggs (Two lanes, kill them evenly)</h3><p>Each side has two big Doomscale Eggs and small eggs whose carriers keep their 3-yard rings apart, so each half uses lanes.</p><p>Each side has two big Doomscale Eggs and small eggs whose carriers keep their 3-yard rings apart, so each half uses lanes. Two Weakened Doomscales climb out of each pit; when one dies the rest enrage, so kill them evenly.</p><ul><li>Warden dead: wait for your Blight Vein to drop, then pick up. Leave the big eggs for now.</li><li>Small eggs up the lanes, carriers never touching; wait at the pit and throw them in together.</li><li>Big eggs: a mobile healer runs back for the far one, a melee takes the close one, both in on one countdown.</li><li>Two Doomscales per side: kick every Anguished Cry, aim Desperate Thrash away, and kill them evenly.</li><li>Kill the Rawlings before Boiling Venom: everyone on them; stun any that enrage.</li><li>Watch Rancid Yolk: picking up an egg with it on kills you (unless you're a tank). Let it drop first.</li><li>Mark the Doomscales from the boss frames and set focus: the kick alert needs it. Call it when two drift apart.</li></ul><h3>Rage of the Shackled (The second burn)</h3><p>The second heart burn (4:38 on Heroic, 4:48 on Mythic).</p><p>The second heart burn (4:38 on Heroic, 4:48 on Mythic). The same as the first, with the Doomscales dragged in for the cleave.</p><ul><li>Back on the platform by her: the heart opens for 20 seconds.</li><li>Bloodlust and your second potion here.</li><li>Raid damage every 4 seconds; the tails fire at the raid again.</li><li>Brown circles kill in one hit: step out.</li><li>Under about 44% after this burn is killable.</li><li>On early pulls with deaths, use Bloodlust earlier.</li></ul><p>The raid splits in two and crosses the venom to the side ledges. Wait for her last wave line to pass, tank first, then movement abilities or a Warlock gate. On the way back, the side without a gate needs its own way.</p><p>Debris falls on the ledges too: the brown circles kill even a tank. Run through one only right after it lands.</p><h2>Intermission: The Shattering</h2><p>Eight coil soaks in about 20 seconds, three groups taking turns; the middle ones come back to back, so personals and potions.</p><h3>Spectral Coils (Three groups, eight soaks)</h3><p>Eight soaks in about 20 seconds, round the room, three groups taking turns.</p><p>Eight soaks in about 20 seconds, round the room, three groups taking turns. Where the first one lands sets the order, so the caller presses it and each group gets its spots.</p><ul><li>Eggs rain down and coils rise. Each group waits near the marker its note gives it.</li><li>The caller presses where the first slam lands: your spots pop up.</li><li>Round the room: group one, two, three, again; group three, the multi-dotters, only soaks twice. Personals around 5:50.</li><li>Finish on the line toward the diamond, inside: the knockback is big and outside is the venom.</li><li>Groups: a tank, a healer and 5 melee; a tank, a healer and 4 ranged; the other two healers and the rest of the DPS.</li><li>Only three patterns, set by where the first soak lands. Don't get baited by her real tail: wait for the spectral coil.</li><li>This is often harder to heal than phase 3.</li></ul><p>The knockback is bigger than on Heroic. Gather on the line toward the diamond, inside. Watch where her bubbles come up: that's the first quarter (usually north-east).</p><h2>Phase 3: Ula'tek's Ascension</h2><p>Grips for the Slithering Clutches, three stacked red circles with three soakers, a raid cooldown on every quarter, and one last heart burn. Most raids find it the easiest phase.</p><h3>Slithering Clutches (Grip them in)</h3><p>On Mythic the clutches crawl for the venom, and one that gets there hatches a Shrieker.</p><p>On Mythic the clutches crawl for the venom, and one that gets there hatches a Shrieker. Two Death Knights grip them over, quarter by quarter, then Gorefiend's Grasp pulls them into the raid's cleave.</p><ul><li>Clutches spawn on the quarters and crawl for the venom.</li><li>The Death Knights grip the near ones right away.</li><li>The far one first to the near quarter, then the rest of the way.</li><li>Gorefiend's Grasp them together and cleave them down before the waves reach them.</li><li>You need two Death Knights; a Blood Death Knight covers nearly every Gorefiend's Grasp.</li><li>They come back during Serpent's Bite, so on the first quarter you do it twice.</li><li>The last set (8:25) is the easy one: two grips, and two Shriekers to kick (half the raid on each).</li></ul><h3>Serpent's Bite (Three soakers, stacked)</h3><p>Three sets.</p><p>Three sets. The three targets stack, and three assigned soakers (one backup) stand on all three circles at once. Each soaker takes about 2 million as it ends, then 18 seconds of damage over time, and sends three waves out, one straight away from the raid.</p><ul><li>Three reds: a melee, a ranged and a healer. They stack on one spot toward the middle.</li><li>The three assigned soakers stand on it: inside all three circles at once.</li><li>As it ends every soaker takes about 2 million: a big defensive.</li><li>Spread out at once; your wave goes away from the raid. Dying is better than hitting them.</li><li>Since Oct 6 three soakers clear a set. Sets: two Mages and a Hunter; a Rogue and both tanks; a healer and both tanks; one backup each.</li><li>After the hit, rotate personals and externals through the damage over time.</li><li>Last set: you can die after it ends (just not during), so soakers walk into the water rather than hit the raid with their waves.</li></ul><h3>Circling Prey (One quarter counter-clockwise)</h3><p>She destroys the quarter she's on: a huge hit and a knockback within 13 yards, a smaller hit on everyone else.</p><p>She destroys the quarter she's on: a huge hit and a knockback within 13 yards, a smaller hit on everyone else. Everyone moves one quarter counter-clockwise, to her left. Personals for the second and third.</p><ul><li>Circling Prey: she's about to destroy the quarter she's on.</li><li>Everyone moves one quarter counter-clockwise.</li><li>It lands: personals for the second and third.</li><li>She comes up at the next corner.</li><li>A raid cooldown waits on each new quarter (Anti-Magic Zone, Anti-Magic Zone, Spirit Link Totem).</li><li>The third quarter is short: a Warlock gate to the fourth makes it easy.</li><li>Waves in this phase come from behind: turn your camera to see them coming.</li></ul><h3>Mother's Wrath (Taunt swap)</h3><p>The same knockback, from the corner of her quarter: her tank walks back into the red circle before the bites.</p><p>The same knockback, from the corner of her quarter: her tank walks back into the red circle before the bites. The tank without Stone Venom soaks the Wretch's spectral venom, then taunts her back before the next Mother's Wrath; the other takes the Wretch.</p><ul><li>Mother's Wrath: a 5-second cast on her tank.</li><li>Knocked back: walk straight back into the red circle in front of her.</li><li>Inside before the bites start, with a big defensive.</li><li>The other tank taunts her back at her next waves cast.</li></ul><p>Her waves still come in this phase, from behind. Watch for them, step out, then spread back out.</p><p>A Wretch comes back on the second quarter. Its bubble right after the second break overlaps the red circles: the reds stack inside it.</p><p>Two Shriekers come with the last clutches: half the raid kicks each. Kill them around their enrage.</p><p>Rage of the Shackled one more time (9:27 on Heroic, 9:37 on Mythic). If she's nearly dead as it ends, jump off behind her: the venom hurts, but you dodge the slam and live a few seconds longer. Use a normal potion here.</p><h2>Putting it all together</h2><p>The whole fight in the order of a real kill. Quiet stretches are cut, and a loop that repeats plays once; the time shown is the real fight clock. Pick a moment to jump there.</p><p>The whole fight in order, timed by a real kill (9:37). Coloured lines are moments when two things happen at once.</p><ul><li>Egg carriers: your 3-yard ring never touches another carrier's, you never take a wave, and you never die holding an egg.</li><li>Her waves come out of her, half the platform at a time: go to the other half, then cross behind the first wave.</li></ul><h3>Phase 1: Fury of the Serpent Mother0:00–2:53</h3><p>She rises at the north edge, her tail on the west side. The carriers stand in their zones; on Mythic the off tank heads for the spectral image.</p><p>The Wretch lands where the Toxic Womb was. Off tank: stand in the spectral venom with a defensive, and aim its waves off the platform.</p><p>The tail comes up at the edge; its tank stays on it. Facing the tail, the right-hand coil slams first: group one in it.</p><h3>Phase 2: Children of the Doomscale2:53–5:19</h3><p>The Warden tethers six players. One mobile ranged (a Mage, Hunter or Balance Druid) walks out and breaks theirs alone.</p><p>Warden dead: wait for your Blight Vein to drop, then pick up.</p><h3>Intermission: The Shattering5:19–6:17</h3><h3>Phase 3: Ula'tek's Ascension6:17–9:37</h3><p>She's about to destroy the quarter she's on. Everyone moves one quarter counter-clockwise.</p><p>Clutches spawn on the quarters and crawl for the venom. The Death Knights grip the near ones right away.</p><p>A 5-second cast on her tank. Knocked back: walk straight back into the red circle in front of her.</p><p>Rage of the Shackled one last time: burn the heart. If she's at 1 or 2% as it ends, jump off behind her to live a few seconds longer.</p><h2>Your job, by role</h2><p>Every line for each role. The Your job box at the top shows the first three.</p><ul><li>Phase 1: the off tank soaks the spectral venom (waves off the platform), then takes the tail and the Wretch; the boss tank runs back in after Mother's Wrath. Never two Mother's Wraths in a row.</li><li>Phase 2: pull the Warden across the line so it teleports to the eggs; grab both Doomscales and bring them back.</li><li>Phase 3: the two Death Knights grip the clutches quarter by quarter and Gorefiend's Grasp them; tanks soak two of the three red sets.</li><li>Phase 1: raid cooldowns just before the first coil slam; the brown circles in the heart burn kill in one hit.</li><li>Phase 2: the five-player tether break needs Anti-Magic Zone and Rallying Cry, plus Spirit Link or Darkness.</li><li>Phase 3: the three red soakers take about 2 million each as it ends, then big damage over time: externals on them.</li><li>Phase 1: 8 carriers in their zones, rings never touch; the egg-damage players break the shells. An immunity won't save your egg from a wave.</li><li>Phase 1: the whole raid in the bubble on the first coil circle, carriers rings apart, then into the second circle.</li><li>Phase 2: one mobile ranged breaks their tether alone, then five break together 4 seconds later.</li><li>Phase 3: the assigned soakers stand on the stacked reds, then aim their waves away from the raid.</li></ul>"},"kithix/heroic":{"boss":"Kith'ix","d":"h","title":"Kith'ix Heroic guide: mechanics, positions and every job · Debrief","desc":"Kith'ix Heroic guide: Lady Liadrin backs you up all fight. Phase one repeats until 25%: adds (beetles one at a time), Extinguish (grab her orbs), fears…","html":"<h1>Kith'ix Heroic guide</h1><p>Lady Liadrin backs you up all fight. Phase one repeats until 25%: adds (beetles one at a time), Extinguish (grab her orbs), fears and tank circles, then an intermission in her barrier. Phase two is a one-minute race: drop your Null Gate at your spot.</p><h3>What wipes us</h3><ul><li>Two beetles at once: A player runs through the middle and touches two Bomber Beetles together: two stacks of Digestive Juices on the whole raid at once, which is very dangerous.</li><li>The Hulk next to him: The tank drags the Hulk into his 25 yards: it takes 75% less damage, lives far too long and is still up when the intermission starts.</li><li>A fragment reached Liadrin: Damage over time on the raid, and her barrier shrinks.</li><li>A gate in the ranged group: A ranged drops their Null Gate where they stand: the hit and then the shards go through the ranged.</li></ul><h3>Your job</h3><ul><li>Off tank: the Hulk onto a Voidweaver, facing away from the raid (Venom Roar knocks back).</li><li>Dark Devastation: out to the drop spot with a defensive; swap every cast.</li><li>P2: Phase two: Negation: taunt at once and run into a Null Gate within 8 seconds.</li><li>Fears: dispel one at a time, the healer first.</li><li>Abyssal Grasp: clear the absorbs within 8 seconds.</li><li>P2: Phase two: if you get a Null Gate, drop it to the west; keep the raid up through the shards.</li><li>Adds away from him; kill the Voidweavers and the eggs first.</li><li>Grab a Light's Embrace orb for every Extinguish.</li><li>P2: Phase two: drop your gate at your role's spot, dodge the shards.</li></ul><h2>Phase one: Darkness' Domain</h2><p>Adds, Extinguish, fears and tank circles, until he reaches 100 energy and the intermission starts. Repeats until 25%.</p><h3>Voidswarm (Adds away from him, beetles one at a time)</h3><p>Raid damage, then adds: a Venomous Hulk, two Voidweavers that stand still and ramp up raid damage, and three Bomber Beetles.</p><p>Raid damage, then adds: a Venomous Hulk, two Voidweavers that stand still and ramp up raid damage, and three Bomber Beetles. Adds near him take 75% less damage. Every add must be dead before the intermission.</p><ul><li>Voidswarm: the adds come out.</li><li>The off tank takes the Hulk onto a Voidweaver, facing away from the raid.</li><li>Beetles: root them, then one player steps on each, one at a time.</li><li>Kill the Voidweaver, then its eggs before they hatch.</li><li>The Hulk's Venom Roar: a frontal and knockback on its tank.</li><li>Nobody stands in the middle while adds are up.</li><li>Each beetle that explodes puts stacking damage over time on the raid: one at a time.</li><li>Hulk and Voidweavers drop eggs when they die (4 and 2): cleave them. He can be moved onto eggs: they ignore his shield.</li></ul><h3>Commanding Presence (Adds away from him)</h3><p>Adds within 25 yards of him take 75% less damage.</p><p>Adds within 25 yards of him take 75% less damage. The eggs don't: he can stand on those.</p><ul><li>Voidswarm: the adds come out, well away from him.</li><li>Within 25 yards of him they take 75% less damage: the Hulk and the Voidweavers are fought out here.</li><li>Kill them out here: the Voidweavers first.</li><li>The eggs they drop are not covered: he can stand on those.</li></ul><h3>Extinguish (An orb for everyone)</h3><p>An 8-second cast, then a huge shadow hit on everyone.</p><p>An 8-second cast, then a huge shadow hit on everyone. Lady Liadrin throws Light's Embrace orbs toward the Hulk (or a Voidweaver, or him): run over one for 90% less shadow damage.</p><ul><li>Liadrin throws the orbs toward the Hulk.</li><li>One orb each; melee can catch them as they land.</li><li>Extinguish lands: the orb carries you through it.</li><li>No orb? A big defensive: on Heroic you usually live.</li><li>He stands still while he casts: the tanks swap then.</li></ul><h3>Unspeakable Horrors (Run out, one dispel at a time)</h3><p>A few players have 6 seconds before they're feared in place.</p><p>A few players have 6 seconds before they're feared in place. Dispelling the fear lets out a scream that hurts everyone, less the further away you are.</p><ul><li>Marked players get 6 seconds.</li><li>Run out of the raid to your raid marker: healer skull, melee star, ranged cross.</li><li>Dispel one, the healer first.</li><li>Let the raid heal, then the next.</li></ul><h3>Dark Devastation (Out to the drop spot, swap)</h3><p>A 15-yard hit round his tank that makes them take 150% more damage.</p><p>A 15-yard hit round his tank that makes them take 150% more damage. It leaves a Voidscar there, which knocks back anyone who touches it and sends fragments in the intermission.</p><ul><li>His tank runs the circle out to the next drop spot.</li><li>A defensive for the hit.</li><li>It lands: a Voidscar stays there.</li><li>The other tank taunts.</li><li>Drop spots 1, 2, 4 and 5 together in the north-east: one tank can catch their fragments together.</li><li>Spot 3 just past the moon marker in the first intermission, spot 6 just past the triangle marker in the second.</li></ul><p>Dead adds drop eggs. A hatched Mindstinger stuns random players and puts damage over time on them over and over: kill the eggs first.</p><p>Tentacles slam a short frontal toward a random player. Step out of it.</p><p>Any add alive when the intermission starts gets much faster, can't be crowd controlled, and pulses raid damage.</p><h2>Intermission: Suffocating Darkness</h2><p>At 100 energy darkness fills the room: everyone into Liadrin's barrier while he keeps casting. Once she gathers enough Light she clears it and stuns him. Moon, circle and triangle mark the barrier's edge, where the soakers stand.</p><h3>Suffocating Darkness (Inside the barrier)</h3><p>Darkness everywhere but Liadrin's barrier: outside it you're pacified and take heavy damage.</p><p>Darkness everywhere but Liadrin's barrier: outside it you're pacified and take heavy damage. Extinguish, fears and tank circles carry on in the small space. Abyssal Grasp drags a few players toward the dark until their heal absorb is gone.</p><ul><li>Everyone into Liadrin's barrier.</li><li>The boss at its north edge.</li><li>Abyssal Grasp: a heal absorb pulling you out.</li><li>Clear it within 8 seconds: healthstone, potion, healing.</li></ul><h3>Eclipse Fragments (Catch them at the barrier's edge)</h3><p>Every Voidscar sends five fragments floating to Liadrin, about 6 seconds apart.</p><p>Every Voidscar sends five fragments floating to Liadrin, about 6 seconds apart. Soaking one puts stacking damage over time on you and anyone within 3 yards. One that reaches her: damage over time on the raid and a smaller barrier.</p><ul><li>Fragments float in from the Voidscars.</li><li>A tank catches the north-east fragments at the circle marker.</li><li>Two players take turns at the other scar.</li><li>The tank without the Dark Devastation debuff soaks most of them.</li><li>With too many scars later on, spread them round the room with one soaker each.</li></ul><h3>Divine Radiance (Don't save cooldowns)</h3><p>When Lady Liadrin has gathered enough Light she clears the darkness and stuns him for 8 seconds.</p><p>When Lady Liadrin has gathered enough Light she clears the darkness and stuns him for 8 seconds. It isn't a burst window: nothing to hold cooldowns for. Each time she is left Exhausted: weaker orbs, a smaller barrier, 10% stronger darkness.</p><ul><li>Everyone is inside the barrier, still doing the usual jobs.</li><li>Lady Liadrin casts Divine Radiance: the darkness clears and he is stunned for 8 seconds.</li><li>It is not a burst window: keep your damage going, there is nothing to hold cooldowns for.</li><li>She is left Exhausted: weaker orbs, a smaller barrier and 10% stronger darkness next time.</li><li>Back out of the barrier for the next adds.</li></ul><h2>Phase two: Last Light (25% to the kill)</h2><p>Everyone is stunned, every add vanishes, and Liadrin gives the raid Luminous Grace. About a minute before the eighth Eclipse kills everyone.</p><h3>Null Gate (Drop it at your role's spot)</h3><p>A melee, a healer and a ranged get 5 seconds, then a 5-yard hit and a gate where they stand.</p><p>A melee, a healer and a ranged get 5 seconds, then a 5-yard hit and a gate where they stand. Each gate sprays Nullshards for the rest of the fight: every hit is a Nullify stack, and 5 stacks kill.</p><ul><li>Null Gate: 5 seconds.</li><li>Walk to your role's spot: melee off to his side, healers west, ranged far east.</li><li>The gate opens: nobody else within 5 yards.</li><li>Dodge the shards.</li></ul><p>Plan: The ranged drop theirs far to the east, a long run: a Warlock gateway (their portal) saves it.</p><h3>Negation (Into the gates in time)</h3><p>His tank takes 100% more physical damage but moves twice as fast and shards can't touch them.</p><p>His tank takes 100% more physical damage but moves twice as fast and shards can't touch them. After 8 seconds it kills them unless they've run into a Null Gate. Each gate hits the tank hard and clears gates and shards within 10 yards.</p><ul><li>Negation: the other tank taunts at once.</li><li>A defensive.</li><li>Run into the melee gate.</li></ul><p>An orb every few seconds, each a raid hit; the eighth kills everyone. Luminous Grace gives 50% more damage and healing.</p><p>Bloodlust here on Heroic: it's a race to the finish.</p><h2>Putting it all together</h2><p>The whole fight in the order of a real kill. Quiet stretches are cut, and a loop that repeats plays once; the time shown is the real fight clock. Pick a moment to jump there.</p><p>The whole fight in order, timed by a real kill (3:43). Coloured lines are moments when two things happen at once.</p><ul><li>The middle stays empty while adds are up: root the beetles, one player steps on each, one at a time.</li><li>Bloodlust at 25%, in phase two.</li><li>Phase two: about a minute before the eighth Eclipse.</li></ul><h3>0:00–0:52</h3><p>His tank takes him to the north side; the ranged and healers start west, by moon.</p><p>The adds come out. The off tank takes the Hulk onto a Voidweaver, facing away from the raid.</p><h3>Intermission: Suffocating Darkness0:52–1:54</h3><h3>1:54–2:41</h3><h3>Intermission: Suffocating Darkness2:41–2:57</h3><h3>Phase two: Last Light (25% to the kill)2:57–3:43</h3><p>5 seconds. Walk to your role's spot: melee off to his side, healers west, ranged far east.</p><p>Kith'ix dies before the eighth Eclipse.</p><h2>Your job, by role</h2><p>Every line for each role. The Your job box at the top shows the first three.</p><ul><li>Phase two: Negation: taunt at once and run into a Null Gate within 8 seconds.</li><li>Intermission: the tank without the Dark Devastation debuff soaks most fragments.</li><li>Phase two: if you get a Null Gate, drop it to the west; keep the raid up through the shards.</li><li>Raid cooldowns when the fears and Abyssal Grasp overlap.</li><li>Phase two: drop your gate at your role's spot, dodge the shards.</li><li>Beetles: they are rooted and one player steps on each, one at a time. Nobody else in the middle.</li></ul>"},"kithix/mythic":{"boss":"Kith'ix","d":"m","title":"Kith'ix Mythic guide: mechanics, positions and every job · Debrief","desc":"Kith'ix Mythic guide: Lady Liadrin backs you up all fight: adds, Extinguish, fears, tank circles, then an intermission in her barrier. Push to 25% fast to…","html":"<h1>Kith'ix Mythic guide</h1><p>Lady Liadrin backs you up all fight: adds, Extinguish, fears, tank circles, then an intermission in her barrier. Push to 25% fast to skip the third intermission. Grip beetles one at a time; Negation needs three gates in 12 seconds, and 3 Nullify stacks kill.</p><h3>What wipes us</h3><ul><li>Two beetles at once: A player runs through the middle and touches two Bomber Beetles together: two stacks of Digestive Juices on the whole raid at once, which is very dangerous.</li><li>The Hulk next to him: The tank drags the Hulk into his 25 yards: it takes 75% less damage, lives far too long and is still up when the intermission starts.</li><li>A fragment reached Liadrin: Damage over time on the raid, and her barrier shrinks.</li><li>A gate in the ranged group: A ranged drops their Null Gate where they stand: the hit and then the shards go through the ranged.</li></ul><h3>Your job</h3><ul><li>Off tank: the Hulk onto a Voidweaver, facing away from the raid (Venom Roar knocks back).</li><li>Dark Devastation: out to the drop spot with a defensive; swap every cast.</li><li>P2: Phase two: Negation: taunt at once and run into three Null Gates within 12 seconds.</li><li>Fears: dispel one at a time, the healer first.</li><li>Abyssal Grasp: clear the absorbs within 8 seconds.</li><li>P2: Phase two: if you get a Null Gate, drop it to the west; keep the raid up through the shards.</li><li>Adds away from him; kill the Voidweavers and the eggs first.</li><li>Grab a Light's Embrace orb for every Extinguish.</li><li>P2: Phase two: drop your gate at your role's spot, dodge the shards.</li></ul><h2>Phase one: Darkness' Domain</h2><p>Adds, Extinguish, fears and tank circles, until he reaches 100 energy and the intermission starts. Repeats until 25%.</p><h3>Voidswarm (Adds away from him, beetles one at a time)</h3><p>Raid damage, then adds: a Venomous Hulk, two Voidweavers that ramp up raid damage, and three Bomber Beetles (four later).</p><p>Raid damage, then adds: a Venomous Hulk, two Voidweavers that ramp up raid damage, and three Bomber Beetles (four later). Adds near him take 75% less damage; all must die before the intermission. The Hulk drops 5 eggs, each Voidweaver 3, each beetle one.</p><ul><li>Voidswarm: the adds come out.</li><li>The off tank takes the Hulk to the Voidweaver on the ranged side, facing away from the raid.</li><li>The Death Knight slows the beetles and grips them in one at a time, by the Hulk.</li><li>Kill the Voidweaver, then the eggs.</li><li>Then the Hulk goes across to the other Voidweaver, with the raid.</li><li>Slow the beetles, never root them: they get gripped. Drop Death and Decay on the second-closest and grip the closest.</li><li>Wait for each one's damage over time to end before the next grip. Two Death Knights make it easier.</li><li>No Death Knight? Root them instead, and have one player step on each, one at a time.</li><li>Each beetle that explodes is one stack on the raid: one is fine, two together is very dangerous, three kills.</li><li>Keep the Hulk alive and bring it to where the raid is: Lady Liadrin's orbs land at the Hulk.</li></ul><h3>Commanding Presence (Adds away from him)</h3><p>Adds within 25 yards of him take 75% less damage.</p><p>Adds within 25 yards of him take 75% less damage. The eggs don't: he can stand on those.</p><ul><li>Voidswarm: the adds come out, well away from him.</li><li>Within 25 yards of him they take 75% less damage: the Hulk and the Voidweavers are fought out here.</li><li>Kill them out here: the Voidweavers first.</li><li>The eggs they drop are not covered: he can stand on those.</li></ul><h3>Extinguish (An orb for everyone)</h3><p>An 8-second cast, then a huge shadow hit on everyone.</p><p>An 8-second cast, then a huge shadow hit on everyone. Lady Liadrin throws Light's Embrace orbs toward the Hulk (or a Voidweaver, or him): run over one for 90% less shadow damage.</p><ul><li>Liadrin throws the orbs toward the Hulk.</li><li>One orb each; melee can catch them as they land.</li><li>Extinguish lands: the orb carries you through it.</li><li>The orbs land at the Hulk while it lives, then a Voidweaver, then him: walk the Hulk to the raid's Voidweaver first.</li><li>He stands still while he casts: the tanks swap then.</li></ul><h3>Unspeakable Horrors (Run out, one dispel at a time)</h3><p>A few players have 6 seconds before they're feared in place.</p><p>A few players have 6 seconds before they're feared in place. Dispelling the fear lets out a scream that hurts everyone, less the further away you are.</p><ul><li>Marked players get 6 seconds.</li><li>Run out of the raid to your raid marker: healer skull, melee star, ranged cross.</li><li>Dispel one, the healer first.</li><li>Let the raid heal, then the next.</li></ul><p>Plan: Another top guild dispels the healer last, because the healer's fear can simply run out. This guide follows the healer-first plan.</p><h3>Dark Devastation (Out to the drop spot, swap)</h3><p>A 15-yard hit round his tank that makes them take 150% more damage.</p><p>A 15-yard hit round his tank that makes them take 150% more damage. It leaves a Voidscar there, which knocks back anyone who touches it and sends fragments in the intermission.</p><ul><li>His tank runs the circle out to the next drop spot.</li><li>A defensive for the hit.</li><li>It lands: a Voidscar stays there.</li><li>The other tank taunts.</li><li>Drop spots 1, 2, 4 and 5 together in the north-east: one tank can catch their fragments together.</li><li>Spot 3 just past the moon marker in the first intermission, spot 6 just past the triangle marker in the second.</li></ul><p>Dead adds drop eggs. A hatched Mindstinger stuns random players and puts damage over time on them over and over: kill the eggs first.</p><p>Tentacles slam a short frontal toward a random player. Step out of it.</p><p>Any add alive when the intermission starts gets much faster, can't be crowd controlled, and pulses raid damage.</p><h2>Intermission: Suffocating Darkness</h2><p>At 100 energy darkness fills the room: everyone into Liadrin's barrier while he keeps casting. Once she gathers enough Light she clears it and stuns him. Moon, circle and triangle mark the barrier's edge, where the soakers stand.</p><h3>Suffocating Darkness (Inside the barrier)</h3><p>Darkness everywhere but Liadrin's barrier: outside it you're pacified and take heavy damage.</p><p>Darkness everywhere but Liadrin's barrier: outside it you're pacified and take heavy damage. Extinguish, fears and tank circles carry on in the small space. Abyssal Grasp drags a few players toward the dark until their heal absorb is gone.</p><ul><li>Everyone into Liadrin's barrier.</li><li>The boss at its north edge.</li><li>Abyssal Grasp: a heal absorb pulling you out.</li><li>Clear it within 8 seconds: healthstone, potion, healing.</li></ul><p>Plan: Abyssal Grasp lands with the fears: the scariest moment. Rally, Anti-Magic Zone, Darkness, Spirit Link here.</p><h3>Eclipse Fragments (Catch them at the barrier's edge)</h3><p>Every Voidscar sends five fragments floating to Liadrin, about 6 seconds apart.</p><p>Every Voidscar sends five fragments floating to Liadrin, about 6 seconds apart. On Mythic soaking one hurts everyone within 6 yards for 10 seconds, so nobody soaks a scar alone. One that reaches her: damage over time on the raid and a smaller barrier.</p><ul><li>Fragments float in from the Voidscars.</li><li>The tank without the Dark Devastation debuff catches the north-east ones at the circle marker (two at a time, then swap).</li><li>Two ranged take turns at the moon marker (or triangle), with a backup.</li><li>Keep the moon and triangle soakers all ranged: a fear takes only one ranged player at a time, so you lose at most one soaker.</li><li>Everyone else stays near the middle, away from the 6-yard splashes (the spell text says 3 yards: we use 6 to be safe).</li><li>Immunities don't stop the damage over time; Anti-Magic Shell helps.</li></ul><h3>Divine Radiance (Don't save cooldowns)</h3><p>When Lady Liadrin has gathered enough Light she clears the darkness and stuns him for 8 seconds.</p><p>When Lady Liadrin has gathered enough Light she clears the darkness and stuns him for 8 seconds. It isn't a burst window: nothing to hold cooldowns for. Each time she is left Exhausted: weaker orbs, a smaller barrier, 10% stronger darkness.</p><ul><li>Everyone is inside the barrier, still doing the usual jobs.</li><li>Lady Liadrin casts Divine Radiance: the darkness clears and he is stunned for 8 seconds.</li><li>It is not a burst window: keep your damage going, there is nothing to hold cooldowns for.</li><li>She is left Exhausted: weaker orbs, a smaller barrier and 10% stronger darkness next time.</li><li>Back out of the barrier for the next adds.</li></ul><p>Bloodlust, potions and every cooldown on the pull. Reach 25% before the third beetle set explodes, by 4:15 at the latest (the fastest kills did it by about 3:50), then root the beetles and ignore them. Some guilds keep Bloodlust for phase two instead.</p><h2>Phase two: Last Light (25% to the kill)</h2><p>Everyone is stunned, every add vanishes, and Liadrin gives the raid Luminous Grace. About a minute before the eighth Eclipse kills everyone.</p><h3>Null Gate (Drop it at your role's spot)</h3><p>A melee, a healer and two ranged get 5 seconds, then a 5-yard hit and a gate where they stand.</p><p>A melee, a healer and two ranged get 5 seconds, then a 5-yard hit and a gate where they stand. Each gate sprays Nullshards for the rest of the fight: every hit is a Nullify stack, and on Mythic 3 stacks kill.</p><ul><li>Null Gate: 5 seconds.</li><li>Walk to your role's spot: melee off to his side, healers west, ranged far east.</li><li>The gate opens: nobody else within 5 yards.</li><li>Dodge the shards.</li></ul><p>Plan: The ranged drop theirs far to the east, a long run: a Warlock gateway (their portal) saves it.</p><h3>Negation (Into the gates in time)</h3><p>His tank takes 100% more physical damage but moves twice as fast and shards can't touch them.</p><p>His tank takes 100% more physical damage but moves twice as fast and shards can't touch them. On Mythic it kills them after 12 seconds unless they've run into three Null Gates. Each gate hits the tank and clears gates and shards within 10 yards.</p><ul><li>Negation: the other tank taunts at once.</li><li>A defensive.</li><li>The melee gate, then the two ranged gates: three in 12 seconds.</li></ul><p>An orb every few seconds, each a raid hit; the eighth kills everyone. Luminous Grace gives 50% more damage and healing.</p><p>Bloodlust already went on the pull. Save the 2-minute cooldowns that come back at about 4:00 for the start of this phase: it's a race to the finish.</p><h2>Putting it all together</h2><p>The whole fight in the order of a real kill. Quiet stretches are cut, and a loop that repeats plays once; the time shown is the real fight clock. Pick a moment to jump there.</p><p>The whole fight in order, timed by a real kill (4:41). Coloured lines are moments when two things happen at once.</p><ul><li>The middle stays empty while adds are up: two beetles touched together put two stacks on the raid, and the third one kills.</li><li>Bloodlust on the pull: skip the third intermission by reaching 25% before the third beetle set explodes (4:15 at the latest).</li><li>Phase two: about a minute before the eighth Eclipse.</li></ul><h3>0:00–0:52</h3><p>Mythic: Bloodlust, potions and every cooldown on the pull.</p><h3>Intermission: Suffocating Darkness0:52–1:54</h3><h3>1:54–2:41</h3><h3>Intermission: Suffocating Darkness2:41–3:43</h3><h3>3:43–3:47</h3><h3>Phase two: Last Light (25% to the kill)3:47–4:41</h3><p>Kith'ix dies before the eighth Eclipse.</p><h2>Your job, by role</h2><p>Every line for each role. The Your job box at the top shows the first three.</p><ul><li>Phase two: Negation: taunt at once and run into three Null Gates within 12 seconds.</li><li>Intermission: the tank without the Dark Devastation debuff soaks most fragments.</li><li>Phase two: if you get a Null Gate, drop it to the west; keep the raid up through the shards.</li><li>Raid cooldowns when the fears and Abyssal Grasp overlap.</li><li>Phase two: drop your gate at your role's spot, dodge the shards.</li><li>Beetles: the Death Knight slows and grips them in; no Death Knight, root them and step on one at a time. Nobody else in the middle.</li></ul>"}};
